var tafsirData_9 = {
  "1": "“˹This is˺ a discharge from all obligations, by Allah and His Messenger, to the polytheists you ˹believers˺ have entered into treaties with:” opens the chapter of disavowal after the Badr guardianship and inheritance order, marking a new legal declaration for the post-Tabuk reality. The phrase “This is a discharge from all obligations, by Allah and His Messenger, to the polytheists you have entered into treaties with” or “bara’atun mina Allahi wa rasoolihi ila allatheena aahadtum mina al-mushrikeen” uses “bara’ah,” freedom, release, disavowal, the same openness required when betrayal is feared, “openly terminating on equal footing.” Here the discharge is not for suspected betrayal but for confirmed persistent violation, the same “violate them every time, not fearing consequences” and “worst beings who persist never to have faith.” The treaties referenced were the pacts made after the previous chapter’s peace inclination and trust, which the polytheists broke by supporting enemies, hindering from the Sacred Mosque, and waging whistling prayer.\n\nThe agents of discharge are Allah and His Messenger together, echoing that distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger and that obedience is to both while you hear his call, showing that treaty, like spoils’ fifth, belongs to Allah and His Messenger’s authority, not to tribal will. The address “to the polytheists you believers have entered into treaties with” indicates the audience is those who had pacts, not all polytheists unconditionally, distinguishing those who honoured terms until end, as the next verses will honour until term, from those who broke.\n\nThis opening links to the previous surah’s “If you see signs of betrayal, openly terminate” and now performs it as a public “bara’ah” on the day of greater pilgrimage, ensuring the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers is contained by clear speech, not by hidden attack, because Allah does not like betrayers and loves the mindful who honour treaties until term.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats bara’ah as blanket hostility. The Quran shows it is a measured termination of pacts that were already violated, with a four-month grace, as the next verse will travel freely for four months but you will have no escape and Allah will disgrace disbelievers, and with exceptions for those who honoured every term and did not support an enemy, honour until term, for Allah loves the mindful. The discharge therefore demonstrates that the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous also discharges from the persistently treacherous, making the path perfectly straight distinct from the wicked’s way that was made appealing and then deterred by fearsome example.",
  "2": "“You ˹polytheists˺ may travel freely through the land for four months, but know that you will have no escape from Allah, and that Allah will disgrace the disbelievers” follows the discharge, giving a respite period with a warning. The verse addresses the polytheists directly: “You may travel freely through the land for four months” or “fa-seehoo fil-ardi arba‘ata ashhur.” The four months are the sacred months where no fighting was initiated, the same sacred months the spoils community knew for deterrent preparation, now as a grace period for those whose treaties are discharged to consider the call “If you repent it will be better for you.” The travel is “seehoo,” to move about, to look, like “travel through the land and see the end of corruptors,” an invitation to reflect before the term that cannot be delayed arrives.\n\nThe warnings that follow balance grace with severity: “but know that you will have no escape from Allah, and that Allah will disgrace the disbelievers” or “wa i‘lamoo annakum ghairu mu‘jizee Allahi wa anna Allaha mukhzi al-kafireen.” The phrase “no escape” repeats “They will have no escape” said to Badr’s disbelievers and “No escape” promised to those who would think they outstripped, and the “mukhz i,” disgracing, mirrors the disgrace of the Fire’s faces and backs beaten, the bed of Hell and flames as cover, the gates not opening, the wall between Paradise and Hell where the Heights recognized the disgraced by marks, and the later “Allah will disgrace the disbelievers” is the earthly foretaste.\n\nThe four months connect the new chapter’s legal timing to the previous chapter’s “Every community has an appointed term, when it comes it cannot be delayed, and why should Allah not punish them while they hinder pilgrims,” showing the term now has a calendar: sacred months as the appointed pause before “kill the polytheists who violated wherever you find them, capture, besiege, lie in wait” unless they repent, pray, and pay zakat.\n\nA common error views the four months as arbitrary. The Quran shows it is the same flawless planning that gave famine then prosperity until they flourished and were seized while unaware, the same gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend, but here made explicit as a mercy for the violators to hear the Word of Allah if they ask for protection and be escorted to safety, for they are a people who have no knowledge. For the mindful who honour treaties until term and who take the fifth for orphans and travellers, the four months prove that even discharge is governed by the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who hears humble and knows what is hidden in hearts, whose mercy encompasses everything yet whose disgrace is certain for those who persist beyond the grace.",
  "3": "“A declaration from Allah and His Messenger ˹is made˺ to all people on the day of the greater pilgrimage that Allah and His Messenger are free of the polytheists. So if you ˹pagans˺ repent, it will be better for you. But if you turn away, then know that you will have no escape from Allah. And give good news ˹O Prophet˺ to the disbelievers of a painful punishment” is the public proclamation of the discharge, ensuring no one is taken by surprise. The phrase “A declaration from Allah and His Messenger is made to all people on the day of the greater pilgrimage” or “wa athanun mina Allahi wa rasoolihi ila al-nasi yawma al-hajji al-akbar” uses “athan,” a call, an announcement, like the call to prayer and the caller who announced “May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers” between Paradise and Hell. The greater pilgrimage, or “hajj akbar,” is the pilgrimage itself, the gathering where the Sacred Mosque’s true guardianship was contested, where whistling and clapping had been prayer, where the treaty had been violated. Announcing there ensures the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers is addressed openly, as treaty termination must be on equal footing, not by stealth, because Allah loves the mindful and does not like betrayers.\n\nThe content “Allah and His Messenger are free of the polytheists” or “anna Allaha baree’un mina al-mushrikeen wa rasooluhu” repeats the bara’ah, freedom from obligations to those who violate every time, the same freedom from the worst beings who persist never to have faith.\n\nThe choice is then stated: “So if you repent, it will be better for you. But if you turn away, then know that you will have no escape from Allah” or “fa-in tubtum fa-huwa khairun lakum wa in tawallaitum fa-‘lamoo annakum ghairu mu‘jizee Allah.” Repentance, or “tawbah,” is the familiar door offered after calf worship where the self-wronged said “If our Lord does not have mercy we will be losers” and found Him All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, and after Badr where “If they desist, past is forgiven.” Better, or “khair,” is the same better Home far better for the mindful, the fertile abundant produce, the honourable provision, the hasanah in both worlds written for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe.\n\nThe closing “And give good news O Prophet to the disbelievers of a painful punishment” or “wa bashshiri allatheena kafaroo bi-athabin aleem” uses “basharah” ironically, as earlier the angelic bushra was glad tidings to believers, now painful punishment is the tidings for persisters, the same aleem torment that overtook those who harmed the she-camel and the brimstone-rained.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats bara’ah as ending invitation. The verse shows invitation remains: repent, better; turn away, no escape, but hear till the painful becomes avoidable through the same repentance, prayer, and zakat that set free after the sacred months.",
  "4": "“As for the polytheists who have honoured every term of their treaty with you and have not supported an enemy against you, honour your treaty with them until the end of its term. Surely Allah loves those who are mindful ˹of Him˺” provides the exception that proves the discharge is not blanket. After announcing travel for four months and warning no escape and disgrace, this verse says “As for the polytheists who have honoured every term of their treaty with you and have not supported an enemy against you, honour your treaty with them until the end of its term” or “illa allatheena aahadtum mina al-mushrikeen thumma lam yanqusookum shay’an wa lam yuzahiroo alaikum ahadan fa-atimmoo ilaihim ahdahum ila muddatihim.” The conditions, “honoured every term” and “have not supported an enemy,” echo the worst beings who violate every time and betray openly, here negated: they did not diminish the treaty, or “yanqusookum,” and did not back an enemy, or “yuzahiroo,” as those who took devils as allies or who allied disbelievers as guardians of one another. Honouring every term until its muddah, term, reflects the “Every community has an appointed term, when it comes it cannot be delayed” and the promise “Knowledge is only with Allah but most do not know,” showing contractual terms are part of divine ordering.\n\nThe reason “Surely Allah loves those who are mindful of Him” or “inna Allaha yuhibbu al-muttaqeen” places treaty fidelity among the best clothing, the fertile production, the establishment of prayer and donation, the firm abiding where reward is never discounted. Love for the muttaqeen is the same love that makes mercy close to good-doers and that kept the mountain from falling when they took the Scripture with strength.\n\nThis exception links directly to the earlier “If you see signs of betrayal openly terminate on equal footing; Allah does not like betrayers” and to “If the enemy inclines to peace incline and trust the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.” Honouring the treaty until term is the peacemaking side of preparation, the deterrent that prevents great oppression and corruption while keeping fitnah contained.\n\nA common error conflates all polytheists after bara’ah. The verse distinguishes faithful treaty-keepers from violators, showing the Quran’s “separate the evil from the good, pile the evil and cast into Hell, the true losers” is precise, not collective. For those dividing spoils and tempted to betray trusts for wealth that is test, this commands to keep amanah even with a polytheist who kept his, because you have no escape from Allah who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing and who knows what is hidden in hearts and who stands between person and heart.",
  "5": "“But once the Sacred Months have passed, kill the polytheists ˹who violated their treaties˺ wherever you find them, capture them, besiege them, and lie in wait for them on every way. But if they repent, perform prayers, and pay alms-tax, then set them free. Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” states the consequence for the violators after the four-month grace and the day of greater pilgrimage declaration, after excepting those who honoured every term. The opening “But once the Sacred Months have passed” or “fa-itha insalakha al-ashhuru al-hurum” uses “insalakha,” to slip out, shed, like the skin shed by the one given signs who abandoned them, marking the end of the sacred pause that was travel time to reflect. The command that follows “kill the polytheists who violated their treaties wherever you find them, capture them, besiege them, and lie in wait for them on every way” or “fa-uqtuloo al-mushrikeena haithu wajadtumoohum wa khuthoohum wa uhsuroohum wa uq‘udoo lahum kulla marsad” mirrors the Badr commands to make firm, to strike necks and fingertips, to prepare power and cavalry to deter, to make a fearsome example so followers may remember, all within the context of those who violate every time, persist never to have faith, and hinder pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque claiming guardianship only the mindful deserve.\n\nThe qualification for release is immediate and merciful: “But if they repent, perform prayers, and pay alms-tax, then set them free” or “fa-in taboo wa aqamoo al-salata wa ataw al-zakata fa-khallo sabilahum.” This triad—tawbah, salah, zakat—is the same as the calf-worshippers’ “If our Lord does not have mercy” plus the pillared true believer who establishes prayer and donates from what He provided, and the mindful who establish prayer and pay zakat and fear none but Allah at mosques. Repentance, prayer, and alms are the signs of shifting from fitnah-persecution where devotion is not entirely for Allah to a devotion entirely for Allah, the purpose of fighting until no persecution.\n\nThe seal “Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” or “inna Allaha Ghafoorun Raheem” repeats the forgiveness promised if they desist, past forgiven, and the mercy that encompasses everything and is ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, showing that even after the sacred months, the gate of “We will be forgiven if we repent and believe, for your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” remains open.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as indiscriminate command. The verse’s qualifiers—the parenthesis “who violated,” the exception for honourable treaty-keepers until term, the four months, the greater pilgrimage announcement, and the repentance-prayer-zakat exit—prove it is the wartime law for treaty violators who chose persistence after clear signs, not a general licence, and that the best of planners whose knowledge is only with Him and whose help is sufficient still prefers repentance over killing, as the next verse’s protection to hear the Word confirms.",
  "6": "“And if anyone from the polytheists asks for your protection ˹O Prophet˺, grant it to them so they may hear the Word of Allah, then escort them to a place of safety, for they are a people who have no knowledge” is the mercy that balances the severity of killing, capturing, besieging, and lying in wait for treaty violators after the sacred months. The previous verse commanded that for the violators, capture and besiege every way, but if they repent, pray, and pay zakat, set them free, for Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. This verse adds a case where repentance has not yet occurred but seeking safety has. The opening “And if anyone from the polytheists asks for your protection, grant it to them” or “wa in ahadun mina al-mushrikeena istajaraka fa-ajirhu” uses “istajara,” to seek shelter, protection, the same shelter sought when few and oppressed and then sheltered, strengthened, and given tayyibat. The command “fa-ajirhu” is imperative: grant protection, not as a favour to the violator’s past numbers and arrogance but as an Islamic duty, because the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous loves the mindful who honour terms.\n\nThe purpose “so they may hear the Word of Allah” or “hatta yasma‘a kalama Allah” shows the protection is not merely humanitarian but purposeful: hearing precedes guidance, as the true believers’ faith increases when revelations are recited and as the Quran is insight, guide, and mercy to be listened to attentively and silently so mercy is shown. The polytheist cause is described as “for they are a people who have no knowledge” or “bi-annahum qawmun la ya‘lamoon,” echoing that most do not know that all omens are with Allah, that most do not know the Hour is tremendous, and that even if they see every sign they will not believe because they are heedless. Lack of knowledge, not innate evil, explains why a single hearing may change the infertile hardly producing into fertile abundant.\n\nThe final duty “then escort them to a place of safety” or “thumma ablighhu ma’manahu” means convey them to where they feel secure, not merely push them out after hearing, showing that the grace period of four months and the day of greater pilgrimage declaration are personal and collective. This escort is the same “make peace and trust Allah” and “Honour your treaty until term” for those who kept it, now extended as safe conduct for the seeker.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads the kill command without this protection verse, as if violation ends all mercy. The Quran places hearing and safe escort immediately after, proving the aim is not extermination but the establishment of truth where devotion becomes entirely for Allah, where the Word can be heard. For the community dividing spoils and tempted to betray trusts for wealth that is fitnah, protecting the seeker and conveying to safety is the opposite of betrayal, preserving the honour that will not only affect wrongdoers if betrayed, and keeping Allah with those who persevere.",
  "7": "“How can such polytheists have a treaty with Allah and His Messenger, except those you have made a treaty with at the Sacred Mosque? So, as long as they are true to you, be true to them. Indeed Allah loves those who are mindful ˹of Him˺” questions the possibility of a general treaty after the discharge, while confirming the specific treaty that remained honoured. The previous verse granted protection so they may hear the Word, then escort to safety. This verse asks “How can such polytheists have a treaty with Allah and His Messenger?” or “kaifa yakoonu lil-mushrikeena ahdun inda Allahi wa inda rasoolihi” using “kaifa,” how, the astonishment at expecting a treaty with those who violate every time, flatter with tongues while hearts deny, and are mostly rebellious, the same worst beings, wilfully deaf and dumb, who buy fleeting gain over revelations and hinder from the Way. The answer is in the exception: “except those you have made a treaty with at the Sacred Mosque” or “illa allatheena aahadtum inda al-masjidi al-haram.” The Sacred Mosque, whose true guardians are only the mindful and whose prayer as whistling and clapping was exposed, is now the locus where a pact kept purely, like the one with Banu Damrah or Banu Kinana in history, remains. At the House where pilgrims were hindered, truthful treaty is most fitting.\n\nThe ethic “So, as long as they are true to you, be true to them” or “fa-ma istaqamoola lakum fa-istaqeemo lahu” uses “istaqama,” to stand straight, be upright, as the straight path made clear to the mindful, the upright way. Uprightness for uprightness mirrors “Hold firmly to Scripture” and “So as long as they are true to you, be true to them” is the treaty form of “And if the enemy inclines to peace, incline.” The basis is reciprocity within bounds, because Allah does not like betrayers even against betrayers, and loves the mindful who honour until term.\n\nThe seal “Indeed Allah loves those who are mindful of Him” or “inna Allaha yuhibbu al-muttaqeen” repeats the love for the muttaqeen stated for those who honoured every term until its end, showing that mindfulness is not only prayer and zakat but covenant fidelity, the same best clothing and fertile thankfulness that makes lessons vary abundantly. It links to the next verse’s description of those who, if they had the upper hand, would respect neither kinship nor treaty and spend to hinder.\n\nA common error treats bara’ah as ending all treaties. This verse shows the Sacred Mosque treaty remains, teaching that discharge is precise: the violators’ pacts are void, the keepers’ remain till term. For the successors who inherited Scripture and indulged in lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven,” this distinction between the violator and the upright is the furqan granted to the mindful to distinguish right from wrong, ensuring the land remains not corrupted after order.",
  "8": "“How ˹can they have a treaty˺? If they were to have the upper hand over you, they would have no respect for kinship or treaty. They only flatter you with their tongues, but their hearts are in denial, and most of them are rebellious” explains why no general treaty can be expected with the discharged polytheists, deepening the previous exception at the Sacred Mosque. The opening repeats “How can they have a treaty?” or “kaifa wa in yazharoo alaikum la yarquboo feekum illan wa la thimmah” using “yazharoo,” to gain ascendancy, to have the upper hand, the same upper hand promised over either caravan or army at Badr and the same dominance Pharaoh claimed “We will completely dominate.” If they gained it, “they would have no respect for kinship or treaty” or “la yarquboo feekum illan wa la thimmah” they would not observe, would not guard, a bond of kinship, or “ill,” or treaty, or “thimmah,” covenant, the same bonds whose violation made them worst beings who violate every time and whose betrayal signs require open termination on equal footing.\n\nThe second trait “They only flatter you with their tongues, but their hearts are in denial, and most of them are rebellious” or “yurdoonakum bi-afwahihim wa ta’ba quloobuhum wa aktharuhum fasiqoon” describes the gap between speech and heart, the same flattery and heart denial earlier noted, the same “They only flatter with tongues but hearts deny, most are rebellious.” Tongues offer peace, hearts hide the plan to capture, kill, or exile and to hinder from the Path while spending to hinder until regret. “Aktharuhum fasiqoon,” most are rebellious, matches “We did not find most of them true to covenant, rather most were rebellious” and “We found most not true to covenant,” showing the majority’s character, not a few.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next about choosing fleeting gain over revelations, hindering, evil what they have done, and not honouring bonds.\n\nA common misunderstanding judges treaties by words alone. The Quran judges by what they would do with upper hand and by hearts versus tongues, the same All-Hearing, All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in the heart and who stands between person and heart. For the believers commanded to prepare power and cavalry to deter, to stand firm and remember often, to obey and not dispute, and to persevere with Allah, the lesson is to not be deceived by flattering tongues when the pattern is violation every time, yet to remain the mindful who, as long as they are true, are true, whose uprightness itself deters those who would follow them, so perhaps they will remember.",
  "9": "“They chose a fleeting gain over Allah’s revelations, hindering ˹others˺ from His Way. Evil indeed is what they have done!” traces the treaty-lessness and flattery to a commercial choice that explains the bravery to violate. After saying they would respect neither kinship nor treaty if they had the upper hand, flattering with tongues while hearts deny and most are rebellious, this verse says “They chose a fleeting gain over Allah’s revelations, hindering others from His Way” or “ishtaraw bi-ayati Allahi thamanan qaleelan fa-saddoo an sabeelihi.” “Ishtaraw,” they purchased, exchanged, like the Sabbath town’s fish trick and the Scripture heirs who seized lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven” and would seize again if similar came, like the spoils’ near gain “You settled with the fleeting gains of this world.” The “thamanan qaleelan,” small price, fleeting gain, is the same “arad hatha al-adna,” the near lowly gain, the “thaman qaleel” for which some would sell even the mountain covenant after it was raised like a cloud. The gain could be a caravan’s profit, a market toll, or the price of staying allied with a stronger pagan bloc.\n\nThe consequence “hindering others from His Way” or “fa-saddoo an sabeelihi” is the same sadd that lay in ambush on every road striving to make it seem crooked, the same spending to hinder until regret, the same hindering pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque while claiming guardianship, the same whistling-clapping prayer that hindered true remembrance. Choosing price over revelations naturally leads to hindering, because the revelations themselves are the insight, guide, and mercy that, when listened to attentively and silently, brings mercy, and when hindered, keeps the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers.\n\nThe verdict “Evil indeed is what they have done!” or “innahum sa’a ma kanu ya‘maloon” uses “sa’a,” evil is what they did, the same “sa’a ma yahkumoon,” evil is what they judge when dividing shares unfairly, the same evil example of the dog and the evil judgment of dividing crops. Evil here is specifically made precise: trade revelation for price and then block its path.\n\nA common error minimizes small bribes as harmless. The verse calls that trade evil indeed, showing it makes the bearer among the doomed whose deeds become vain. The next verse adds they honour neither kinship nor treaty, and are transgressors, and that if they repent, pray, and pay zakat they become brothers, showing the way out is to quit the trade, protect the Way instead of hindering, and be among those who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted.",
  "10": "“They do not honour the bonds of kinship or treaties with the believers. It is they who are the transgressors” restates the social consequence of choosing fleeting gain over revelations and hindering from the Way. The previous verse said that trade leads to hindering and is evil. This verse says “They do not honour the bonds of kinship or treaties with the believers” or “la yarquboona fi mu’mineen illan wa la thimmah” they do not observe toward a believer a tie of kinship or a pledge, the same “illan wa thimmah” that would be ignored if they had the upper hand, the same bonds whose violation every time made them worst beings. Kinship, or “ill,” should have restrained even in ignorance, as later “Do not take your parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief over belief” shows kinship has limits but normally commands honour. Treaty, or “thimmah,” is the pact openly terminated on equality. Ignoring both toward believers shows the heart denial behind tongues’ flattery.\n\nThe verdict “It is they who are the transgressors” or “wa ula’ika humu al-mu‘tadoon” uses “mu‘tadoon,” those who cross limits, exceed bounds, the same transgressors whom He does not like when calling humbly, the same who spread corruption after order, who take a calf that cannot speak, who change “Hittah,” who break the Sabbath, and who do not honour kinship or treaties. Transgression is the infertile land that hardly produces despite rain because it exceeds the measure where just measure is commanded.\n\nThis verse links the fleeting-gain trade to a permanent character: transgressor. It also links to the next mercy: if they repent, pray, and pay zakat they become brothers in faith, the same brotherhood that made the hearts of Muhajirun and Ansar united when all riches could not, showing transgression can be left by returning to the right path that the revelations clear: so perhaps they will return.\n\nA common misunderstanding excuses kinship violation as politics. The Quran names it transgression even in war, where treaty on equal footing and safe escort for the hearer of the Word remain obligations. For the community handling the fifth for close relatives, orphans, the poor, and travellers, this warns that ignoring arham for gain, like the heirs who claim “We will be forgiven,” is the mu‘tadi path whose home is the bed of Hell and flames as cover, while honouring bonds is the muttaqi path Allah loves.",
  "11": "“But if they repent, perform prayer, and pay alms-tax, then they are your brothers in faith. This is how We make the revelations clear for people of knowledge” is the doorway after the accusation of transgressors who honour neither kinship nor treaty. The previous two verses said they traded revelations for small price, hindered, honoured no bond, and are the transgressors. This verse says “But if they repent, perform prayer, and pay alms-tax, then they are your brothers in faith” or “fa-in taboo wa aqamoo al-salata wa ataw al-zakata fa-ikhwanukum fi al-deen.” The three conditions repeat the triad offered after the sacred months: repent, pray, pay, then set free, and mirror the calf remorse “If our Lord does not have mercy and forgive we will be losers” turned into “If Allah finds goodness in hearts He will give you better and forgive” for captives, and the muhajirun’s belief, emigration, striving. Repentance, or “tawbah,” turns from taking faith as play and from feeling secure from planning; prayer, or “aqamoo,” establishes the firm abiding, the moderate inward remembrance morning and evening, the prostration of angels not too proud; zakat is the donation from what He provided, the honourable provision’s share, the orphans’ and traveller’s right, the opposite of spending to hinder.\n\nThe result “then they are your brothers in faith” or “fa-ikhwanukum fi al-deen” transforms yesterday’s transgressor into today’s brother, the same brotherhood that united hearts when all riches could not, the brotherhood that made Muhajirun and Ansar guardians of one another, that made successors. Brotherhood is not by blood alone but by the same arham now limited to inheritance in Allah’s Book, yet here faith brotherhood is granted immediately upon the three acts, showing the community’s openness, as earlier “Grant protection so they may hear the Word, then escort to safety” and “Make peace if they incline.”\n\nThe seal “This is how We make the revelations clear for people of knowledge” or “wa nufassilu al-ayati li-qawmin ya‘lamoon” repeats the varying of lessons for those who know, who reflect on wonders, who hold firmly, who consider the home of the rebellious, showing clarity is for the knowledgeable, the same knowledgeable for whom revelations were detailed in the Tablets.\n\nA common error keeps former transgressors at a distance even after the triad. The verse commands brotherhood, the same brotherhood that sheltered when few, strengthened with help, and provided tayyibat, proving that the discharge is not vengeance but purification where devotion becomes entirely for Allah, whose creation and command are His, blessed.",
  "12": "“But if they break their oaths after making a pledge and attack your faith, then fight the champions of disbelief—who never honour their oaths—so perhaps they will desist” addresses the relapse after the brotherhood offer. The previous verse said if they repent, pray, and pay zakat they are brothers, with revelations made clear for people of knowledge. This verse says “But if they break their oaths after making a pledge and attack your faith” or “wa in nakathoo aimanahum min ba‘di ahdihim wa ta‘anoo fi deenikum.” “Nakathoo,” to break, undo a knot, as with treaty violators who violate every time, as with the Sabbath fish nets set before Sabbath, as with the gate changers who changed words. “Aymanahum,” oaths, and “ahdihim,” covenant, echo the mountain covenant to hold firmly and the primordial testimony, now made with the Prophet and community. “Ta‘anoo fi deenikum,” to stab, attack your faith, is not private doubt but public assault, making the Way seem crooked, spending to hinder, whistling-clapping at the Sacred House, lying in ambush, the same hindering that made preparation of power and cavalry necessary to deter.\n\nThe command “then fight the champions of disbelief—who never honour their oaths—so perhaps they will desist” or “fa-qaatiloo a’imata al-kufri innahum la aimana lahum la‘allahum yantahoon” targets “a’imma,” leaders, heads, the chiefs, or “mala’,” who, like Pharaoh’s chiefs, Iblis who refused prostration saying “I am better,” and the town’s tyrants recognized by marks, lead the breaking. “La aimana lahum,” no oaths for them, means their oaths are void, unreliable, the same “worst beings” whose promise “We will certainly believe if you remove torment” is broken as soon as it is removed until their fate, and whose “We will be forgiven” is repeated while seizing again. “La‘allahum yantahoon,” so perhaps they will desist, shows even fighting the leaders aims at desistance, like making a fearsome example to deter followers and showing the home of the rebellious.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats fighting champions as embracing endless war. The Quran ties it to perhaps they will desist, the same perhaps that motivates varying signs, sending winds with heavy clouds to lifeless land so perhaps you will be mindful, and the caller who warns so perhaps they will abstain. The fertile alternative remains: the mindful granted furqan who, with the angels’ horror in disbelievers’ hearts and necks and fingertips struck, see the truth prevail and illusions fail, but whose ultimate hope is that the champion’s breaking ends and he becomes the brother prayed for in the prior verse.",
  "13": "“Will you not fight those who have broken their oaths, conspired to expel the Messenger ˹from Mecca˺, and attacked you first? Do you fear them? Allah is more deserving of your fear, if you are ˹true˺ believers” urges the believers to fight the champions who break oaths and stab at faith, now listing three concrete provocations. After saying fight the champions who never honour oaths so perhaps they will desist, this verse asks “Will you not fight those who have broken their oaths, conspired to expel the Messenger from Mecca, and attacked you first?” or “a-la tuqaatiloona qawman nakathoo aimanahum wa hammoo bi-ikhraji al-rasooli wa hum bada’ookum awwala marrah.” The three charges—breaking oaths after pledge, plotting to expel the Messenger, and initiating attack—recall the capture-kill-exile conspiracy where Allah planned and is best of planners, and the later hindering of pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque while claiming guardianship only the mindful deserve. Their attack first refers to Badr’s march where the pagans left homes arrogantly to be seen and to hinder, and to the treaty violations where they violated every time without taqwa.\n\nThe rhetorical question “Do you fear them? Allah is more deserving of your fear, if you are true believers” or “a-takhshawna hum fa-Allahu ahaqqu an takhshahu in kuntum mu’mineen” contrasts fear of people with fear of the Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days, established on the Throne, makes night and day overlap, and whose creation and command are His. Fear of Allah is the same humble secret reverence morning and evening, the trembling at remembrance that increases faith, the awe that makes angels glorify and prostrate while not too proud. True believers’ fear is like the Heights’ prayer not to be joined with wrongdoers, not like the Satan who said “I truly fear Allah, for He is severe” only when he saw angels.\n\nThis verse links directly to the previous “Do not be like those who say we hear but do not listen, the worst deaf and dumb who do not understand” and to “Had Allah known any goodness He would have made them hear.” Fear of people over Allah shows lack of goodness to be made to hear.\n\nA common error lets fear of the former expellers paralyse the community that was once few, oppressed, fearing being snatched, then sheltered and strengthened. The verse reminds that Allah sheltered, strengthened with help, and united hearts when all riches could not, and that Allah is sufficient for Prophet and followers, with the earth belonging to Him and outcome to the righteous, so fear should be directed where the Hour’s heaviness, the barrier, and the weighing truly lie, not toward those whose forces no matter how numerous will not benefit because Allah is with believers.",
  "14": "“˹So˺ fight them and Allah will punish them at your hands, put them to shame, help you overcome them, and soothe the hearts of the believers—” lists four divine effects that fighting the oath-breakers, expellers, and first attackers will bring, continuing the urge not to fear them but to fear Allah. The previous verse asked “Will you not fight those who broke oaths, conspired to expel the Messenger, and attacked first? Do you fear them? Allah is more deserving of your fear if you are true believers.” This verse says the fighting is the instrument, the punishment is His: “So fight them and Allah will punish them at your hands” or “qaatiloohum yu‘aththibhum Allahu bi-aideekum.” This mirrors “It was not you who killed them but Allah who did so, nor was it you who threw but Allah who threw” in the earlier beautiful test, and the rain and angels that bound hearts and firmed steps—human hands are the means, punishment is His, like the staff devouring illusion or the sea drowning Pharaoh, all subjected by His command.\n\nThe four effects: punish at your hands, put to shame, or “yukh z ihim,” disgrace, the same disgrace suffered by those whose arrogance made gates not open and by apes made khasi’een and by Pharaoh’s people drowned, help you overcome them, or “yansurkum alaihim,” the victory that only comes from Almighty, All-Wise after crying for help with a thousand angels murdifeen and with believers’ united hearts, and soothe the hearts of the believers, or “wa yashfi sudoora qawmin mu’mineen,” heal, cool, the chests of a believing people who had been oppressed, feared snatching, and whose hearts were bound, whose rage needed removal. Soothing follows the healing earlier promised as rivers beneath with bitterness removed, where “We will remove whatever bitterness they had in hearts.”\n\nThis soothing links to the next verse “removing rage from their hearts” and that Allah pardons whom He wills, for He is All-Knowing, All-Wise, showing the Hearts’ healing is not revenge but purification, like the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy.\n\nA common misunderstanding reduces fight to vengeance. The Quran shows fight as therapy for the oppressed believers’ hearts, as deterrence for oath-breakers perhaps they will desist, and as the establishment of truth by His Words to uproot disbelievers even to the wicked’s dismay. For the spoils generation told to be mindful, settle affairs, and not betray trusts for wealth that is test, fighting the champions is not for lowly gain but for the great reward with Allah, where the honourable provision is not the ransom but the soothed, healed chest that can then remember morning and evening without heedlessness and can listen to the Quran attentively and be silent so mercy is shown.",
  "15": "“removing rage from their hearts. And Allah pardons whoever He wills. For Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” continues the healing that fighting the oath-breakers whose hands are Allah’s hands brings, and adds the limit of divine pardon. The previous verse said fight so Allah will punish at your hands, put them to shame, help you overcome, and soothe the hearts of believers. This verse completes the soothing: “removing rage from their hearts” or “wa yuthhiba ghayza quloobihim.” “Ghayz,” rage, is the burning anger of the oppressed who were expelled, whose sons were to be killed, whose pilgrims were hindered, whose mosque prayer was mocked with whistling, whose treaties were violated every time. The same ghayz that made the thunderous challenge at Badr as if driven to death, the same that needed drowsiness, rain, angels, and the dream few to calm. Removing it mirrors “We will remove whatever bitterness they had in hearts, rivers flowing beneath” in Paradise, showing fighting that establishes truth also purifies emotion, not inflames it.\n\nThe second clause “And Allah pardons whoever He wills. For Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “wa yatoobu Allahu ala man yashaa’u wa Allahu Aleemun Hakeem” indicates that after rage is removed, some enemies may be pardoned, as earlier “If they repent it will be better, if they turn away no escape” and “If they repent, pray, and pay zakat they are brothers” and “If they desist past is forgiven.” The pardoning is “yatoob,” to turn in grace, the same turning that later generations who inherited Scripture claimed “We will be forgiven” while seizing again falsely, but here it is true turning after the oath-breakers perhaps desist, so the champion’s defeat may lead to the champion’s tawbah, as the magicians’ defeat led to prostration.\n\nThe names “All-Knowing, All-Wise” close, as with the creation in six Days, the heart-uniting, and the lightening of burden knowing weakness. He knows whose rage needs removal and whose heart holds goodness to be made to hear.\n\nA common error imagines soothing hearts means hardening them to rejoice in disgrace. The Quran joins soothing with pardoning: the same fight that disgraces the insolent also heals the believers and opens a door for the enemy, showing the best planner frustrates evil plans but also opens mercy that encompasses everything for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, and that the final invitation remains to the mindful who will firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, rather than the dog’s panting clinging to earth.",
  "16": "“Do you ˹believers˺ think that you will be left without Allah proving who among you ˹truly˺ struggles ˹in His cause˺ and never takes trusted allies other than Allah, His Messenger, or the believers? And Allah is All-Aware of what you do” tests the sincerity behind the rage removed and the pardoning promised. After saying fight so Allah will punish at your hands, disgrace, help, soothe, and remove rage, and pardons whom He wills, this verse asks “Do you think you will be left” or “am hasibtum an tutrakoo” that you will be left alone without being proven, tested, as earlier the succession was to see what you will do, the twelve springs and the city gate were to test, the Sabbath fish to test for rebelliousness, and the winds and fertile barren to vary lessons. The proof is “who among you truly struggles in His cause and never takes trusted allies other than Allah, His Messenger, or the believers” or “an ya‘lama Allahu allatheena jahadoo minkum wa lam yattakhithoo min dooni Allahi wa la rasoolihi wa la al-mu’mineena waleejah.” “Jahadoo,” struggle, includes striving with wealth and lives as earlier belief, emigration, striving, shelter and help define guardianship, and wealth and children as fitnah test. “Waleejah,” trusted inner ally, secret confiding, is the same guardianship, or “awliya,” warned not to take others as guardians besides Him and not to take parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief over belief, and whose opposite is the unlettered Prophet’s followers who honour and support him.\n\nThe requirement never to take waleejah besides the three shows that fighting the oath-breakers is not only external but internal, purging hidden loyalties that would betray Allah, Messenger, and trusts knowingly, the same betrayal that made the worst beings violate every time.\n\nThe seal “And Allah is All-Aware of what you do” or “wa Allahu khabeerun bima ta‘maloon” is the awareness that encompasses, never absent, that knows what is hidden in hearts, that hears humbly and secretly without liking transgressors, that writes not a leaf falls without a record.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks being left after Badr means rest. The verse says being left is being tested, as earlier “We dispersed them in groups and tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return” and “Is it not clear to successors that if We will We can punish for sins and seal hearts.” For the community handling one-fifth, captives, and spoils lawful and good, the test is whether striving and walayah remain pure, not dispersed among those whose large numbers and arrogance are of no use and whose spending to hinder becomes regret.",
  "17": "“It is not for the polytheists to maintain the mosques of Allah while they openly profess disbelief. Their deeds are void, and they will be in the Fire forever” returns to the guardianship of the Sacred Mosque that was questioned in “How can they have a treaty except those at the Sacred Mosque” and that was abused by hindering pilgrims while claiming to be its guardians and whistling-clapping as prayer. The phrase “It is not for the polytheists to maintain the mosques of Allah while they openly profess disbelief” or “ma kana lil-mushrikeena an ya‘muroo masajida Allahi shahideena ala anfusihim bil-kufri” uses “ya‘muroo,” to maintain, to visit, to build, to populate, the same “ya‘muroo” that will be praised for the true maintainers who believe, establish prayer, pay zakat, and fear none but Allah, and the same “ya‘muroo” that the Sacred House’s true guardians, the mindful, do. Maintaining while professing disbelief is contradictory, like taking the lowing calf that cannot speak as god while knowing Allah rescued from Pharaoh, or like claiming inheritance of Scripture while seizing lowly gain.\n\nThe verdict “Their deeds are void, and they will be in the Fire forever” or “ula’ika habitat a‘maluhum wa fin-naari hum khalidoon” repeats “habitat,” become vain, as with deeds of those who deny signs and meeting and with illusions that failed. Their visits, spending to hinder, and whistling are habit at a‘maluhum voided because foundation is shirk, the same associating without sultan that makes deeds vain for the dog’s panting example. The Fire’s abiding matches the bed of Hell with flames as cover for the wicked and the disgrace of apes for persistent violation, the home of the rebellious shown.\n\nThis verse links the treaty’s Sacred Mosque exception to its inner meaning: treaty is kept with those who honour terms until end because they show a trace of mindfulness; but maintaining the Mosque requires more—to believe, to fear only Allah, not to take parents as allies if they prefer disbelief. It also links to the spoils’ fifth and to the fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers: if polytheists maintain mosques, fitnah spreads as great oppression and corruption.\n\nA common error honors building alone regardless of belief. The Quran says maintaining without belief voids deeds, while the next verse honors even hopeful maintenance by those who combine belief, prayer, zakat, and fear of Allah alone, showing the House belongs to the mindful whose hearts tremble and whose faith increases, not to those whose deeds are void.",
  "18": "“The mosques of Allah should only be maintained by those who believe in Allah and the Last Day, establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and fear none but Allah. It is right to hope that they will be among the ˹truly˺ guided” gives the positive counterpart to the voided maintenance of polytheists who profess disbelief. The previous verse said their deeds are void and they will be in Fire forever; this verse defines the true maintainers. The phrase “The mosques of Allah should only be maintained by those who believe in Allah and the Last Day, establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and fear none but Allah” or “innama ya‘muru masajida Allahi man amana billahi wa al-yawmi al-akhiri wa aqama al-salata wa ata al-zakata wa lam yakhsha illa Allah” lists five traits that together describe the fertile land producing abundantly. Belief in Allah and the Last Day is the same belief whose meeting the losers denied until the Hour surprised them and whose denial makes deeds vain; establishing prayer is the same establishing that trembling hearts, true believers, and those who firmly abide do; paying zakat is the same donation from what He provided and the zakat that is the condition with repentance and prayer to become brothers, the opposite of spending to hinder; fearing none but Allah is the same trust that makes Allah Almighty, All-Wise sufficient when hypocrites call the faithful deluded, and that made the magicians prostrate despite threats to cut limbs.\n\nThe humble conclusion “It is right to hope that they will be among the truly guided” or “fa-asa ula’ika an yakoonoo mina al-muhtadeen” uses “asa,” perhaps, hope, the same perhaps that motivates “perhaps they will return, perhaps they will be mindful, perhaps they will abstain,” showing guidance is hoped for with these traits, not claimed as guarantee, keeping the chest humble and the call secret with hope and fear. The truly guided, or “muhtadeen,” are those whose Lord has opened their chest to Islam, who hear when the Quran is recited and are silent so mercy is shown, who remember inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness, the same guided ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice.\n\nThis links the Sacred Mosque’s guardianship, correctly assigned to the mindful, to the future of spoils, inheritance, and the trial of wealth and children: those who maintain mosques with these five are the guardians of one another who prevent great oppression and corruption, whose honourable provision is not the lowly gain seized but the elevated ranks and forgiveness. A common misunderstanding limits maintenance to bricks and carpets. The Quran defines it as living traits that make the maintained mosque the House where devotion is entirely for Allah, where the word “Hittah” would be said humbly, not changed, and where the fifth for orphans, poor, and travellers is honoured as part of the same establishment.",
  "19": "“Do you ˹pagans˺ consider providing the pilgrims with water and maintaining the Sacred Mosque as equal to believing in Allah and the Last Day and struggling in the cause of Allah? They are not equal in Allah’s sight. And Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people” answers the boast of those who, having been denied guardianship while professing disbelief, still claimed merit through services to the House. The opening question “Do you pagans consider providing the pilgrims with water and maintaining the Sacred Mosque as equal to believing in Allah and the Last Day and struggling in the cause of Allah?” or “a-ja‘altum siqayata al-hajji wa imarata al-masjidi al-harami ka-man amana billahi wa al-yawmi al-akhiri wa jahada fi sabeeli Allahi” contrasts two deeds: “siqayat al-hajj,” watering pilgrims, a noble service, and “imarat al-masjid,” maintaining the Sacred Mosque, the same maintenance just praised for the true believers, versus “amana billahi wa al-yawmi al-akhiri wa jahada,” believing and struggling with wealth and lives, emigrating, sheltering and helping, the same true believers granted elevated ranks, forgiveness, and honourable provision. The pagans argued, as the treaty violators flattered with tongues, that their water and building matched the believers’ faith and jihad, just as earlier they asked “Has Allah favoured these poor believers” and swore the humble would never receive mercy.\n\nThe verdict “They are not equal in Allah’s sight. And Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people” or “la yastawoona inda Allah wa Allahu la yahdi al-qawma al-zalimeen” uses “la yastawoona,” not equal, the same lack of equality as the heavy and light scales, the fertile and infertile, the truth and batil, the blind and seeing. The reason for non-guidance is “al-zalimeen,” wrongdoers, those who place things where they do not belong, who place worship on a lowing calf, who divide spoils unfairly, who take parents as allies if they prefer disbelief. Wrongdoing here is specifically claiming guardianship while hindering pilgrims, whistling as prayer, and feeling secure from planning as losers do.\n\nThis verse links Badr’s lesson that victory is only from Allah, Almighty, All-Wise, and that Allah frustrates evil plans, to the true measure of merit: not the visible water service but the invisible faith and the striving that was told “If there are twenty steadfast they will overcome two hundred” and that was tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return. A common error today equates social service without belief to belief with service. The Quran honours siqayah but subordinates it: without the five traits—belief, Last Day, prayer, zakat, fearing none but Allah—service is void as with the polytheists’ maintenance, whose deeds are void and who will be in Fire forever. The mindful who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted understand water is noble when it flows from faith, otherwise it is just whistling by another name.",
  "20": "“Those who have believed, emigrated, and strived in the cause of Allah with their wealth and their lives are greater in rank in the sight of Allah. It is they who will triumph” concludes the comparison that began with watering pilgrims and maintaining the Sacred Mosque versus Believing and struggling. The previous verse said they are not equal in Allah’s sight and He does not guide the wrongdoing people. This verse says who is greater: “Those who have believed, emigrated, and strived in the cause of Allah with their wealth and their lives are greater in rank in the sight of Allah” or “allatheena amanoo wa hajaroo wa jahadoo fi sabeeli Allahi bi-amwalihim wa anfusihim a‘zamu darajatan inda Allah.” Believed, or “amanoo,” is trembling at remembrance and increasing faith; emigrated, or “hajaroo,” is leaving homes like the Prophet was brought out for a just cause, like the oppressed sheltered after being few; strived with wealth and lives, or “jahadoo bi-amwalihim wa anfusihim,” is the complete infaq from what He provided and the striving that the trial wealth and children test, the opposite of betraying trusts for the lowly gain.\n\nRank, or “darajah,” is the same elevated ranks promised for true believers with forgiveness and honourable provision, the same ranks according to deeds where each will be told to taste for what hands sent forth. Greater in rank with Allah means the weighing that will be just favours them, the barrier’s heights recognize them by marks, the promise to those who believe with the Hereafter and guard prayers includes them, and the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured includes them. The closing “It is they who will triumph” or “wa ula’ika humu al-fa’izoon” uses “fa’izoon,” the triumphant, the successful, the same falah as the heavy scales who are successful and the “perhaps you will be successful” tied to remembering favours and to following what was sent down without taking other guardians. Triumph here is not the caravan that was wished as unarmed, not the spoils divided, not the stones prayed to rain, but the lasting Home far better for the mindful and the provision where rivers flow after bitterness is removed.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates rank with lineage to the House or with water service alone. This verse says rank is with Allah and is earned by the triad of belief, emigration, and striving with wealth and lives, the same triad that makes the believers guardians of one another and prevents great oppression. For those dividing the fifth for close relatives, orphans, the poor, and travellers, the message is that the highest rank is not in taking the share but in being of those who strived and gave shelter and helped, whose past, if they desist, is forgiven, whose future, if they persist on truth, is the honourable provision that never is discounted for those acting righteously.",
  "21": "“Their Lord gives them good news of His mercy, pleasure, and Gardens with everlasting bliss” describes the reward for those just praised as greater in rank who believed, emigrated, and strove with wealth and lives. The previous verse said those triad are greater in rank and will triumph; this verse details the triumph: “Their Lord gives them good news of His mercy, pleasure, and Gardens with everlasting bliss” or “yubashshiruhum Rabbuhum bi-rahmatin minhu wa ridwanin wa jannatin lahum fiha na‘eemin muqeem.” Three gifts: mercy, or “rahmah,” the same mercy encompassing everything, close to good-doers, ordained for those who shun evil; pleasure, or “ridwan,” the contentment of the Lord, higher than mere provision, the same pleasure sought by those who invoke morning and evening seeking His Face; and Gardens with everlasting bliss, or “jannatin... na‘eemin muqeem,” lasting delight where rivers flow and bitterness is removed and the noble Word fulfilled. The Lord as giver emphasizes personal care, as earlier “We will remove bitterness... Rivers will flow... Praise be to Allah who guided us” and as the shelter when few.\n\nThe sequence shows rank becomes mercy, then pleasure, then Garden, each deeper, the same tenfold and honourable provision, the hasanah in both worlds written for the mindful. A common misunderstanding equates triumph with spoils. The verse relocates triumph to mercy, divine pleasure, and everlasting Gardens, the true honourable provision versus the fleeting gain traded by successors who claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing again.\n\nThe invitation to the gate changers, treaty violators, and Sabbath fishers is that even they, if they repent, pray, and pay zakat, become brothers and may yet receive this good news, as earlier the disgrace of apes was not the only end for those who would firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted. This promise of honourable provision and great reward shows that rank with Allah is not a temporary title like guardianship claimed by those whose prayer was whistling and clapping, but a lasting placement that the weighing just will preserve and that the barrier will recognize by marks.",
  "22": "“to stay there for ever and ever. Surely with Allah is a great reward” emphasizes the permanence of the Gardens just promised with mercy, pleasure, and everlasting bliss. After saying their Lord gives good news of mercy, pleasure, and Gardens with everlasting bliss, this verse adds “to stay there for ever and ever. Surely with Allah is a great reward” or “khalideena fiha abadan inna Allaha indahu ajrun azeem.” “Khalideena... abadan,” abiding forever, eternally, marks the opposite of the near lowly gain, or “arad al-dunya,” the fleeting goods for which the believers were rebuked for settling and for which successors indulge while claiming forgiveness and then seize again. Everlasting stay contrasts with “as if they had never lived there” for the wiped, and with “as if they had never been” for the heedless, and with the punishment that comes at night while asleep where only a cry “We were wrongdoers” remains.\n\nThe second phrase “Surely with Allah is a great reward” or “inna Allaha indahu ajrun azeem” repeats the great reward, or “ajr azeem,” that wealth and children are only a test and with Allah is great reward, the same great reward that is hasanah in both worlds, the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, and the infinite bounty for the mindful granted furqan. Greatness here is not mere size but permanence, as the creation belonging to Him alone outlasts the creation, and the command belonging to Him endures.\n\nThis permanence links to the earlier “We will remove bitterness... Rivers flowing... We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth had they been faithful,” and to the promise that those who firmly abide and establish prayer never have reward discounted, showing the great reward with Allah is the preserved, multiplied version of the honourable provision already tasted as twelve springs and shaded clouds.\n\nA common error thinks great reward is immediate spoil. The verse relocates it: with Allah, forever, after ranks, forgiveness, and the barrier, not in the dispute over the near side of the valley and caravan below. For the heirs who inherited Scripture and are well-versed yet seize, the reminder is that the Home of the Hereafter is far better for the mindful, will you not understand, and the everlasting stay is the true “better” to choose over the four-month travel freely that ends with no escape and disgrace.",
  "23": "“O believers! Do not take your parents and siblings as trusted allies if they choose disbelief over belief. And whoever of you does so, they are the ˹true˺ wrongdoers” follows the everlasting Gardens to define loyalty’s limits. After promising great reward with Allah forever, this verse warns that kinship, though honoured as arham where inheritance is ordained, cannot override the guardianship just praised as greater in rank. The opening “O believers! Do not take your parents and siblings as trusted allies if they choose disbelief over belief” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo la tattakhithoo aba’akum wa ikhwanakum awliya’a in istahabboo al-kufra ala al-iman” uses “awliya,” trusted allies, guardians, the same wilayah that makes believers guardians of one another to prevent great oppression and that makes disbelievers guardians of one another, and that was warned “never take trusted allies other than Allah, His Messenger, or believers” in the test. Parents and siblings, or “aba’akum wa ikhwanakum,” are the closest arham whose inheritance was just ordained, yet if they prefer, or “istahabboo,” love, choose disbelief over belief, as earlier some dispersed were righteous others less so, and as later the families whose wealth you acquired and trade you fear may become more beloved than Allah, then their wilayah must be reoriented.\n\nThe verdict “And whoever of you does so, they are the true wrongdoers” or “wa man yatawallahum minkum fa-ula’ika humu al-zalimoon” names such loyalty as zulm, placing things where they do not belong, like associating idols that cannot create, cannot help, and taking faith as play. Wrongdoing here is measured against the well-versed inheritors who knew the covenant not to say about Allah except truth yet indulged; choosing kin over belief violates that covenant.\n\nThis verse links to the next, where parents, children, siblings, spouses, family, wealth, trade, and homes are listed as possible rivals to love for Allah, Messenger, and striving, and to the warning that Allah does not guide the rebellious, and to the earlier “Will you not fight those who broke oaths” where fear of people was contrasted with fear of Allah if you are true believers.\n\nA common misunderstanding excuses shirk for family harmony. The Quran honours parents in other places where they do not command disbelief, but here it draws the line: firm abiding by Scripture and establishing prayer where reward is never discounted requires that the best clothing of taqwa outrank even the dearest kin when kin choose disbelief.",
  "24": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘If your parents and children and siblings and spouses and extended family and the wealth you have acquired and the trade you fear will decline and the homes you cherish—˹if all these˺ are more beloved to you than Allah and His Messenger and struggling in His Way, then wait until Allah brings about His Will. Allah does not guide the rebellious people’” expands the kinship warning into a complete inventory of rival loves. After saying do not take parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief, this verse lists eight beloveds: parents, children, siblings, spouses, extended family, or “asheeratakum,” clan, wealth acquired, trade whose decline is feared, and homes cherished, or “masakinu tardawnaha.” These are the same livelihood, establishment, and palaces carved into mountains, the twelve springs, the commerce Midian defrauded, and the children prayed for as good offspring, now tested as fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers. The condition “are more beloved to you than Allah and His Messenger and struggling in His Way” or “ahabba ilaikum mina Allahi wa rasoolihi wa jihadin fi sabeelihi” measures heart, the same heart Allah stands between person and heart, the same heart that trembles, trusts, and is united when all riches could not.\n\nThe consequence “then wait until Allah brings about His Will. Allah does not guide the rebellious people” or “fa-tarabbasoo hatta ya’ti Allahu bi-amrihi wa Allahu la yahdi al-qawma al-fasiqeen” uses “tarabbasoo,” wait expectantly, as earlier “Then wait, I too am waiting with you” and “You will soon know who will fare best.” The awaited Will, or “amr,” is the same amr whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed, the same amr of the appointed term that seizes at night while asleep or day while at play, the same amr that made the mountain a cloud and levelled it. Not guiding the fasiqeen, the rebellious who step out, matches “We did not find most true to covenant, rather most were rebellious” and “We found most rebellious,” showing love of rivals over Allah is fisq.\n\nA common error treats such waiting as passive. The verse makes waiting an active suspense where the whole community’s fitnah hangs, as later Hunain will show great numbers prove of no advantage when hearts rely on numbers more than Allah. The antidote is the earlier “He has guided some while others are destined to stray because they took devils as masters thinking they are guided” versus taking the mindful as guardians, ensuring love for Allah and Messenger outranks even cherished homes.",
  "25": "“Indeed Allah has given you ˹believers˺ victory on many battlefields, even at the Battle of Ḥunain when you took pride in your great numbers, but they proved of no advantage to you. The earth, despite its vastness, seemed to close in on you, then you turned back in retreat” recalls the favour of the victory and its near reversal at Hunain, and the commentators describe the battle in full. The verse opens with the reminder of the divine favour: “Indeed Allah has given you believers victory on many battlefields,” or “laqad nasarakumu Allahu fee mawatin katheeratin,” the many “mawatin,” the battlefields, the occasions of the fight, upon which God had granted the believers the victory, and the commentators note that the reminder is the ground of the lesson, for the same Lord who had given the many victories was the Lord who now taught, through Hunain, the deeper truth of the victory. Ibn Kathir explains the scene of Hunain: it was the year 8 AH, after the conquest of Mecca, and the tribes of Hawazin and Thaqif had gathered a great host against the Muslims, and the believers marched out with twelve thousand men, and the verse records the first error: “when you took pride in your great numbers,” or “wa yawma Hunainin ith a‘jabatkum kathratukum,” the day when the abundance of the numbers pleased them, and the commentators preserve the words that were spoken: some of the believers said, “We will not be defeated today for lack of numbers,” and the saying was the pride that the verse names, the trust in the count of the men rather than in the Lord of the men.\n\nThe verse then records the consequence: “but they proved of no advantage to you,” or “fa-lam tughni ‘ankum shay’an,” the numbers availed nothing, and the collapse that followed: “The earth, despite its vastness, seemed to close in on you, then you turned back in retreat,” or “wa daqat ‘alaykum al-ardu bima rahubat thumma wallaytum mudbireen,” the narrowing of the earth upon them despite its vastness, the constriction of the heart in the moment of the panic, and the retreat, the “tawalli,” the turning of the backs, and the commentators describe the scene: the enemy had laid an ambush in the valley of Hunain, and when the Muslims entered the narrow pass, the arrows fell upon them from the heights, and the army broke and fled, and the vast earth seemed to close upon the fleeing host, so that the twelve thousand were scattered and the pride of the morning became the flight of the day. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s great lesson on the numbers: the victory is not from the multitude, and the count of the men is no advantage against the one who has not the Lord with him, and the pride in the numbers is the first step of the defeat, for the one who trusts the count has forgotten the Source of the victory. The verse is the record of the lesson of Hunain, and the warning against the pride that the abundance breeds.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the divine pedagogy, and the commentators connect it to the whole account of the battle. The Lord had given the many victories, and the believers had begun to attribute the victory to their own strength, and the trial of Hunain was the correction, the demonstration that the numbers are nothing and the Lord is everything, and the correction was the mercy that the pride required, for the community that learns the lesson of Hunain is the community that never again trusts the count over the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the introduction to the account of the divine rescue that the next verse records, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the victory: the pride in the numbers is the prelude of the flight, and the trust in the Lord is the prelude of the rescue. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the numbers: the many battlefields recalled, the pride in the count recorded, and the retreat described — the warning that the abundance is no advantage, and that the victory is the gift of the Lord alone.",
  "26": "“Then Allah sent down His reassurance upon His Messenger and the believers, and sent down forces you could not see, and punished those who disbelieved. Such was the reward of the disbelievers” records the rescue at Hunain, and the commentators describe the reassurance and the unseen forces. The verse states the first gift: “Then Allah sent down His reassurance upon His Messenger and the believers,” or “thumma anzala Allahu sakeenatahu ‘ala rasoolihi wa ‘ala al-mu’mineena,” the “sakinah,” the divine serenity, the tranquility that descended upon the Prophet and the believers in the moment of the flight, and the commentators describe the scene: in the midst of the panic, the Prophet stood firm upon his mule, calling out, “To me, O people! I am the Prophet, no lie! I am the son of Abdul-Muttalib!” and he gathered around him the small band of the steadfast — the emigrants and the helpers, and among them his uncle Abbas and Ali and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith — and the serenity of God descended upon them, so that the fleeing host turned and rallied, and the panic was reversed. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account: the Prophet took a handful of pebbles and cast it toward the enemy, saying, “May the faces be disfigured,” and the pebbles, by the divine power, reached the enemy, and the tide of the battle turned, and the reassurance and the casting were the two instruments of the rescue.\n\nThe verse then states the second gift: “and sent down forces you could not see,” or “wa anzala junoodan lam tarawha,” the unseen forces, the angels, the “junood” that the believers could not see, and the commentators note the parallel with Badr, for the Lord who had sent the angels at Badr sent them again at Hunain, and the unseen host was the reinforcement that the flight had made necessary. Then the punishment: “and punished those who disbelieved. Such was the reward of the disbelievers,” or “wa ‘aththaba allatheena kafaroo wa thalika jaza’u al-kafireen,” the punishment of the disbelievers in the defeat and the slaughter, and the verse closes with the declaration that the punishment was the “jaza’,” the recompense, the reward that the disbelief had earned. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the divine rescue: the serenity for the hearts, the forces for the field, and the punishment for the enemy, and the three together are the full answer of the Lord to the flight, the restoration of the victory that the pride had nearly lost. The verse is the record of the rescue, and the assurance that the Lord does not abandon the believers even in their stumble.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reversal, and the commentators connect it to the previous verse and to the whole account. The pride in the numbers had brought the flight, and the serenity of the Lord had brought the return, and the two verses together are the complete lesson of Hunain: the believer who trusts his own strength falls, and the believer who turns to the Lord rises, and the victory that the numbers could not hold was restored by the reassurance that the Lord sent. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the description of the rescue, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the return: the stumble is not the end, and the Lord who sends the reassurance after the flight is the Lord who restores the victory that the stumble had lost. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the rescue: the serenity sent, the forces unseen, and the punishment delivered — the restoration of the victory by the One whom the pride had forgotten.",
  "27": "“Then afterwards Allah will turn in grace to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” records the door of the repentance after the battle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the promise: “Then afterwards Allah will turn in grace to whoever He wills,” or “thumma yatoobu Allahu min ba‘di thalika ‘ala man yasha’u,” the turning of God in grace, the “tawbah” of the Lord upon whom He wills, and the commentators explain the occasion: after the defeat of the Hawazin and the Thaqif at Hunain, and after the siege and the surrender that followed, many of the defeated embraced Islam, and the verse declares that the door of the divine turning remained open, for the Lord who had punished them was the Lord who would turn to them in grace, and the turning was the mercy that followed the punishment. Ibn Kathir notes the fulfillment: the Hawazin, who had fought the Muslims at Hunain, came later to the Prophet as Muslims, and their embrace of the faith was the “tawbah” that the verse promised, and the fulfillment is the proof of the truth of the promise, for the Lord who turns in grace does not close the door upon the one who seeks the return.\n\nThe second part is the names that seal the promise: “And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “wallahu ghafoorun raheem,” the “Ghafoor,” the All-Forgiving, and the “Raheem,” the Most Merciful, and the commentators note the placement of the names, for the verse that began with the punishment of the disbelievers ends with the names of the forgiveness and the mercy, and the two ends are the two sides of the divine dealing, the justice that punishes and the mercy that turns, and the believer is taught to hold both, the fear of the punishment and the hope of the turning. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s constant balance: the punishment is real, but the mercy is wider, and the one who is punished in the world is not shut out of the mercy of the next, for the Lord turns in grace upon whom He wills, and the will of the Lord is the will of the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful. The verse is the promise of the return, and the assurance of the mercy.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the open door, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer who has stumbled, like the army at Hunain, is taught by the verse that the stumble is not the end, and that the Lord who punishes the persistence turns in grace upon the return, and the door of the turning is the mercy that the believer must never despair of, for the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful is the Lord who receives the one who comes back. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the account of Hunain, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the mercy: the punishment is followed by the possibility of the turning, and the turning is the gift of the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the open door: the turning promised, the grace declared, and the mercy named — the assurance that the Lord who punishes is the Lord who turns, and that the return is never closed.",
  "28": "“O believers! Indeed, the polytheists are ˹spiritually˺ impure, so they should not approach the Sacred Mosque after this year. If you fear poverty, Allah will enrich you out of His bounty, if He wills. Surely, Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” gives the social consequence of the discharge and the Ḥunain lessons. After the victory, the reassurance, the unseen forces, and the grace to whoever He wills, this verse addresses the House whose guardianship was disputed. The phrase “Indeed, the polytheists are spiritually impure, so they should not approach the Sacred Mosque after this year” or “innama al-mushrikoona najasun fa-la yaqraboo al-masjida al-haram ba‘da aamihim hatha” uses “najas,” impurity, not physical filth but spiritual pollution of associating false gods in what He gave, dividing crops unfairly, taking the lowing calf, whistling as prayer, hindering pilgrims while claiming guardianship only the mindful deserve. Their impurity is the same heedlessness that makes them like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless, with hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing. Preventing approach after this year protects the House’s maintenance, which should only be by those who believe, establish prayer, pay zakat, and fear none but Allah, lest the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers spread.\n\nThe economic fear is then addressed: “If you fear poverty, Allah will enrich you out of His bounty, if He wills” or “wa in khiftum ailatan fa-sawfa yughnikumu Allahu min fadlihi in shaa’a.” The believers feared loss of trade when polytheists, who brought commerce to the pilgrimage, would no longer come, just as they once feared being snatched when few and oppressed and as later they feared poverty from the fifth. The answer mirrors “We dispersed you in groups and tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps you would return” and “Remember when you were few, oppressed, fearing snatching, then He sheltered, strengthened, and provided tayyibat so perhaps you will be thankful.” Enrichment is “min fadlihi,” from His bounty, the same infinite bounty for the mindful granted furqan, the same honourable provision from the Lord, not from whistling merchants, and it is “in shaa’a,” if He wills, the same will that gives victory on many battlefields and that sends sakeenah and unseen forces.\n\nThe names “Surely, Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “inna Allaha Aleemun Hakeem” close, as with the prior decree that made spoils lawful and the lightening knowing weakness. He knows where to place His message, who is grateful, who will exploit impurity for gain, and He is wise to order the House’s purity over its market. A common misunderstanding treats economic dependence as excuse to tolerate impurity in the sacred. The verse says the House’s purity outranks its market, and the Provider who sent winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit can enrich beyond pilgrimage trade, as He did when He made oppressed successors of blessed eastern and western lands.",
  "29": "“Fight those who do not believe in Allah and the Last Day, nor comply with what Allah and His Messenger have forbidden, nor embrace the religion of truth from among those who were given the Scripture, until they pay the tax, willingly submitting, fully humbled” extends the logic of protecting the Sacred Mosque’s purity to the next perimeter. The previous verse declared polytheists spiritually impure should not approach after this year and that poverty fear would be met with bounty if He wills. This verse addresses another group whose impurity takes a different form: “those who do not believe in Allah and the Last Day, nor comply with what Allah and His Messenger have forbidden, nor embrace the religion of truth from among those who were given the Scripture” or “allatheena la yu’minoona billahi wa la bil-yawmi al-akhiri wa la yuharrimoona ma harrama Allahu wa rasooluhu wa la yadeenona deena al-haqqi mina allatheena ootoo al-kitab.” The description mirrors the true prohibitions earlier—open and secret indecencies, sin—now applied to People of the Scripture who were given Moses’ fundamental tablets and the Gospel, yet who took rabbis and monks as lords besides Allah and whose many rabbis and monks consume wealth wrongfully and hinder from the Way, whose deeds are to be separated as the foul from the good.\n\nThe command “until they pay the tax, willingly submitting, fully humbled” or “hatta yu’too al-jizyata an yadin wa hum saghiroon” offers a path other than the kill, capture, besiege, lie in wait for treaty violators who refused repentance, prayer, and zakat. “Jizyah,” a tax, willingly, or “an yadin,” by hand, and “saghiroon,” humbled, subdued, not humiliated as persons but as a political status where the hindering, the “We will be forgiven” while seizing, the fish tricks, and the “such rabbis as lords” cease. It is the same humbling that makes large numbers and arrogance of no use and that makes the bed of Hell and flames as cover for the wicked who persist.\n\nThis verse links to the previous “If you fear poverty, Allah will enrich” by showing jizyah as part of that enrichment from His bounty, a lawful provision unlike the lowly gain seized. It also links to “They wish to extinguish Allah’s light with mouths but Allah will perfect His light” and “He sent His Messenger with guidance and religion of truth to prevail over all, even to the dismay of polytheists,” showing fighting here is to protect light, not to extinguish people.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as compulsion of faith. The Quran’s “no compulsion in faith” remains, as the jizyah allows those given Scripture to keep faith while living under justice that prevents great oppression and corruption in the land if believers guard each other, and while they are free to hear the Word and be escorted to safety if they ask, as the discharge chapter began.",
  "30": "“The Jews say, ‘Ezra is the son of Allah,’ while the Christians say, ‘The Messiah is the son of Allah.’ Such are their baseless assertions, only parroting the words of earlier disbelievers. May Allah condemn them! How can they be deluded ˹from the truth˺?” exposes the theological root behind the People of the Scripture described in the previous verse. The phrase “The Jews say, ‘Ezra is the son of Allah,’ while the Christians say, ‘The Messiah is the son of Allah’” or “wa qalat al-yahoodu Uzairun ibnu Allahi wa qalat al-nasara al-Maseehu ibnu Allah” attributes a claim of divine sonship, the same shirk earlier condemned when they associated jinn though He created them and falsely attributed sons and daughters out of ignorance, and when they took rabbis and monks as lords besides Allah even though commanded to worship One God. “Uzair,” Ezra, the scribe who restored Torah after exile, is honoured but not divine; the Messiah, or “Maseeh,” son of Mary, is honoured as the Word, but not a son. The Quran’s wording “qalat,” they say, indicates a saying among some, not a universal creed of every individual, but a representative doctrine that violates “How could He have children when He has no mate? He created all things and has perfect knowledge” and “That is Allah your Lord, there is no god except Him.”\n\nThe evaluation “Such are their baseless assertions, only parroting the words of earlier disbelievers” or “thalika qawluhum bi-afwahihim yudaahi’oona qawla allatheena kafaroo min qabl” shows mouths, or “afwah,” speaking without the mountain covenant’s “We will not say about Allah except truth” and without the primordial testimony. “Yudaahi’oon,” parroting, imitating, shows repetition of earlier disbelievers’ words, the same disbelievers whose deeds were beautified and who felt secure from planning.\n\nThe imprecation “May Allah condemn them! How can they be deluded from the truth?” or “qatalahum Allahu anna yu’fakoon” uses “yu’fakoon,” to be turned, deluded, as earlier “How can you then be deluded from the truth?” when Allah brings living from dead and causes seeds to sprout. Delusion is being turned away from the straight path despite the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy.\n\nA common error excuses sonship as metaphor. The Quran treats it as association that makes Paradise’s water forbidden to disbelievers and gates not opened, yet the same chapter opened a discharge with four-month grace, protection to hear the Word, and brotherhood if they repent, pray, pay, showing even this baseless saying can be forgiven with true faith that establishes prayer and donation.",
  "31": "“They have taken their rabbis and monks as well as the Messiah, son of Mary, as lords besides Allah, even though they were commanded to worship none but One God. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. Glorified is He above what they associate ˹with Him˺!” deepens the sonship error into a system of lordship. The previous verse quoted the baseless “son of Allah” sayings parroting earlier disbelievers; this verse shows the practical consequence: “They have taken their rabbis and monks as well as the Messiah, son of Mary, as lords besides Allah” or “ittakhathoo ahbarahum wa ruhbanahum arbaban min dooni Allahi wa al-Maseeha abna Maryam.” “Ahbar,” rabbis, scholars, and “ruhban,” monks, ascetics, were taken as “arbab,” lords, by obeying their allowances and prohibitions without Allah’s permission, the same associating without sultan condemned in “How could He have children when He has no mate?” and “Associate jinn though He created them.” The earlier question “Has He forbidden the two males or the two females? Bring witnesses who can testify Allah forbade this” showed taking law from other than the best of judges is shirk; here taking rabbis’ law as lordship is the same, even though commanded to worship One God, or “illa li-ya‘budoo ilahan wahidan.” The creed “There is no god worthy of worship except Him” or “la ilaha illa huwa” is the individual testimony that follows the valley appointment and the dream few, the same that made the staff devour illusions.\n\nThe seal “Glorified is He above what they associate with Him!” or “subhanahu amma yushrikoon” is the same glorification uttered after good offspring were associated in what He gave, and after the mountain was levelled and Moses glorified, declaring Allah free from having a son or a need for lord-partners.\n\nThis verse links to the next about wishing to extinguish light with mouths and many rabbis and monks consuming wealth wrongfully and hindering from the Way and hoarding gold and silver without spending in Allah’s cause. The rabbinic lordship is not abstract; it funds hindrance, just as spending to hinder and whistling prayer funded the earlier barrier.\n\nA common misunderstanding limits lords to idols with names. The Quran shows lords can be scholars whose words are obeyed as law without the detailed Book and the Tablets’ best, turning the well-versed who seized lowly gain into lords. The remedy is the same as for spoils and treaties: follow what was sent down without taking other guardians, be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, and call upon Him by the most beautiful names, keeping away from those who abuse them, so the religion of truth prevails even to the polytheists’ dismay.",
  "32": "“They wish to extinguish Allah’s light with their mouths, but Allah will only allow His light to be perfected, even to the dismay of the disbelievers” states the outcome of the rabbinic lordship just described. The previous verse said they took rabbis and monks and the Messiah as lords besides the One God; this verse says “They wish to extinguish Allah’s light with their mouths” or “yureedoona an yutfi’oo noora Allahi bi-afwahihim.” “Noor Allah,” Allah’s light, is the revelation that is insight from your Lord, a guide and mercy, the same light that lets dead hearts walk among people, that made the staff a real snake and the hand shining for all to see, that wrote on Tablets the fundamentals of everything as guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe. “Bi-afwahihim,” with mouths, mouths that flatter while hearts deny, mouths that say “We have heard, we could produce similar, it is ancient fables,” mouths that pray “Rain stones upon us if this is truth,” mouths that parrot “Ezra is son of Allah” and “Messiah is son,” mouths that hinder on every road striving to make it seem crooked, mouths that, as earlier, devils whisper elegant words to incline hearts.\n\nThe counter “but Allah will only allow His light to be perfected, even to the dismay of the disbelievers” or “wa ya’ba Allahu illa an yutimma noorahu wa law kariha al-kafireen” uses “yatimm,” to complete, perfect, the same word for completing favours upon those who do good with the Tablets, completing the term of forty nights, completing the noble Word for those who endured. Perfection of light is the same as truth prevailing and illusions failing, the same as “so truth was established and falsehood wiped out even to the wicked’s dismay” at Badr, and “He sent His Messenger with guidance and religion of truth to prevail over all even to the polytheists’ dismay” that follows.\n\nA common error thinks hostile mouths can dim the light by spending to hinder, whistling in the House, or claiming the Quran is fables. The verse says Allah refuses except to perfect it, as He united hearts when all riches could not, as He sent reassurance and unseen forces after the earth closed at Hunain, as He frustrated the evil plans of disbelievers and gave victory only from Almighty, All-Wise. The dismay of disbelievers is precisely the proof of perfection, like the magicians’ prostration after great magic, showing the light that the heedless are turned away from even if they see every sign is still protected for the thankful who produce abundantly and for the mindful who will have the Home far better.",
  "33": "“He is the One Who has sent His Messenger with ˹true˺ guidance and the religion of truth, making it prevail over all others, even to the dismay of the polytheists” gives the means by which light will be perfected despite mouths that wish to extinguish it. The previous verse said they wish to extinguish with mouths but Allah will perfect His light even to disbelievers’ dismay; this verse says how: “He is the One Who has sent His Messenger with true guidance and the religion of truth, making it prevail over all others, even to the dismay of the polytheists” or “huwa allathee arsala rasoolahu bil-huda wa deeni al-haqqi li-yuzhirahu ala al-deeni kullihi wa law kariha al-mushrikoon.” “Bil-huda,” with guidance, is the straight path made clear to the mindful, the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the insight that is guide and mercy for those who believe, the same guidance that opens chests to Islam and makes the mindful choose right path over crooked. “Deeni al-haqqi,” religion of truth, is the deen whose devotion should be entirely for Allah, the upright way that is not the other ways that divide, the same deen whose establishment truth and wiping out batil at Badr was for.\n\nThe purpose “making it prevail over all others” or “li-yuzhirahu ala al-deeni kullihi” uses “yuzhira,” to make appear, to make victorious, to make manifest, as the staff’s snake prevailed over every clever magician’s great display, as the sea prevailed over Pharaoh’s constructions, as the earthquake left worshippers lifeless in homes while warners were rescued, as the rain of brimstone showed the end of the wicked. Prevailing is not merely argument but establishment where the weighing will be just and the barrier will stand, where the fifth for close relatives, orphans, poor, travellers is honoured and the Sacred Mosque is maintained only by those who believe, establish prayer, pay zakat, and fear none but Allah.\n\nThe clause “even to the dismay of the polytheists” or “wa law kariha al-mushrikoon” repeats the dismay of disbelievers from the previous verse, now directed at polytheists whose discharge with four months was just announced and who were told to travel freely but will have no escape and disgrace, whose prayer was whistling and clapping, whose spending to hinder becomes regret.\n\nA common misunderstanding reduces prevailing to military dominance alone. The verse shows it is guidance’s prevalence: the light perfected, the Word fulfilled for those who endured, the honourable provision, the elevated ranks, all prevailing over the void deeds of those who denied signs and meeting. For the community that was instructed to prepare power and cavalry to deter, to incline to peace if they incline, to trust the All-Hearing, All-Knowing, prevailing reminds that deterrence serves truth’s manifestation, not hoarding, and that the infinite bounty for the mindful is part of that prevailing, ensuring the light remains for those who firm abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted.",
  "34": "“O believers! Indeed, many rabbis and monks consume people’s wealth wrongfully and hinder ˹others˺ from the Way of Allah. Give good news of a painful torment to those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend it in Allah’s cause” exposes the economic engine behind the wish to extinguish light and the lordship taken besides Allah. The previous verses traced light perfected via the Messenger with guidance, but many rabbis and monks are not its bearers. The phrase “Indeed, many rabbis and monks consume people’s wealth wrongfully and hinder others from the Way of Allah” or “inna katheeran mina al-ahbari wa al-ruhbaani la-ya’kuloona amwala al-nasi bil-batili wa yasuddoona an sabeeli Allah” uses “ya’kuloona,” to eat, consume, as earlier many devoured the spoils’ near gain, seized lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven,” changed “Hittah,” and broke the Sabbath for fish. “Bil-batili,” wrongfully, without right, is like the batil that was wiped out at Badr and devoured by the staff, and like the whistling-clapping prayer that was void. Hindering from the Way is the same sadd on every road making it seem crooked, the spending to hinder until regret, the same “We will be forgiven” inheritors’ indulgence.\n\nThe second sentence widens the warning: “Give good news of a painful torment to those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend it in Allah’s cause” or “wa allatheena yaknizoona al-thahaba wa al-fiddata wa la yunfiqoonaha fi sabeeli Allahi fa-bashshirhum bi-athabin aleem.” “Yaknizoona,” to hoard, to treasure in vaults, is like the treasure heated for branding foreheads, sides, and backs on the Day, the same painful torment promised to those who spend to hinder and to the calf worshippers afflicted with wrath and disgrace. The hoarding here is not saving for lawful provision and deterrent horses, which is permitted as preparation what you can and spending will be paid in full, but hoarding that withholds zakat and blocks the Way, the opposite of donating from what He provided and paying the poor and traveller their fifth.\n\nThe verse connects the earlier “Who has forbidden adornments and tayyibat Allah brought forth? They are for believers in this worldly life” and “Eat and drink but do not waste; He does not like the wasteful” to show that adornments hoarded wrongfully become a cover for hindering. A common error imagines rabbinic piety guarantees guidance. The Quran shows many rabbis and monks eat wrongfully and block, the dog’s panting clinging to earth example of those who abandon signs, the worst deaf and dumb, the heedless like cattle. The painful good news is the same Aleem torment that tasted at necks and faces, now for the hoarder whose treasure will brand him, while the mindful who spend in His cause find the great reward with Allah.",
  "35": "“The Day ˹will come˺ when their treasure will be heated up in the Fire of Hell, and their foreheads, sides, and backs branded with it. ˹It will be said to them,˺ ‘This is the treasure you hoarded for yourselves. Now taste what you hoarded!’” details the painful torment warned for hoarders who consume wrongfully and hinder. The previous verse promised painful good news to those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend in Allah’s cause; this verse shows its form. The phrase “The Day will come when their treasure will be heated up in the Fire of Hell, and their foreheads, sides, and backs branded with it” or “yawma yuhma alaiha fi nari Jahannama fa-tukwa biha jibahuhum wa junoobuhum wa zuhooruhum” uses “yuhma,” to heat, make scorching, like the wind that ushers heavy clouds heated into torment for those who persist, and “tukwa,” to brand, to sear, the same branded sides and backs that earlier were beaten by angels taking souls and the same backs that will bear burdens. The three locations, foreheads, sides, backs, correspond to the directions a hoarder turns from the needy, the same three that will be shown for the rebellious whose home is displayed.\n\nThe taunt “This is the treasure you hoarded for yourselves. Now taste what you hoarded!” or “hatha ma kanaztum li-anfusikum fa-thooqoo ma kuntum taknizoon” uses “thooqoo,” taste, as earlier the punishment for whistling prayer was “Taste for disbelief” and the Fire’s residents taste burning, and the spending to hinder becomes regret. Tasting what was hoarded makes the hoard itself the instrument of punishment, the lowly gain seized while claiming “We will be forgiven” now heated and pressed.\n\nThe verse links to the fifth for close relatives, orphans, poor, travellers, and to the tayyibat for believers to eat without waste, and to the “We will never discount reward of those acting righteously” for those who firmly abide and establish prayer. The hoarder’s treasure is the opposite of the honourable provision where bitterness is removed; his branding is the opposite of the shining faces that, like Moses’ hand, are white for all to see, but his is branding for all to see.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats hoarding as prudent saving. The Quran distinguishes saving that spends in Allah’s cause—preparation of power and horses to deter, payment in full without wrong—and hoarding that withholds zakat and blocks the Way. The branded foreheads show that the same gold that could have been zakat for the poor, the fifth for the traveller, or the deterrent horses, now brands the forehead that refused to prostrate with angels who are not too proud, teaching that the Day’s heat makes the earth, despite its vastness, close in on those who took pride in numbers, just as at Ḥunain.",
  "36": "“Indeed, the number of months ordained by Allah is twelve—in Allah’s Record since the day He created the heavens and the earth—of which four are sacred. That is the Right Way. So do not wrong one another during these months. And together fight the polytheists as they fight against you together. And know that Allah is with those mindful ˹of Him˺” returns to sacred time after the hoarding warning, grounding the four months of the discharge and the sacred months’ sanctity. The phrase “Indeed, the number of months ordained by Allah is twelve—in Allah’s Record since the day He created the heavens and the earth—of which four are sacred” or “inna iddata al-shuhoori inda Allahi ithna ashara shahran fi Kitabi Allahi yawma khalaqa al-samawati wal-arda minha arba‘atun hurum” uses “Kitab Allah,” the same Record whose number of months was written on the Tablets as fundamentals of everything and whose Word is perfected. Twelve months and four sacred are the same sacred months that made the four-month travel for polytheists meaningful and that made the capture, besiege, lie in wait after they slip out a timed law, not arbitrary vengeance. Four sacred preserves the order after the land was set in order, not to spread corruption, the same order that angels and rain and winds maintain.\n\nThe command “That is the Right Way. So do not wrong one another during these months” or “thalika al-deenu al-qayyim fa-la tazlimoo feehinna anfusakum” calls the twelve-month, four-hurum system “al-deen al-qayyim,” the Right, upright Way, the same straight path made clear to the mindful, the true religion of the Messenger to prevail. Wronging in them, like reallocating sanctity next verse, is the zulm that makes deeds vain and that makes the worst beings persist.\n\nThe collective duty “And together fight the polytheists as they fight against you together” or “wa qaatiloo al-mushrikeena kaffatan kama yuqaatiloonakum kaffah” mirrors “Obey Allah and His Messenger and do not dispute, or you will lose courage and wind” and “Prepare power and horses to deter,” showing unity after the hearts were united when all riches could not, and opposite to the dispersed groups some righteous others less so.\n\nThe seal “And know that Allah is with those mindful of Him” or “wa i‘lamoo anna Allaha ma‘a al-muttaqeen” is the same withness that made the few overcome, that sent sakeenah at Ḥunain, that is the Protector who revealed the Book, the same withness that deters enemies known and unknown. A common error treats months as human convention to shift. The next verse shows reallocating them is increase in disbelief, led astray, their evil deeds made appealing, and Allah does not guide the disbelieving people, so holding the twelve and four as written is part of holding firmly to Scripture where reward is never discounted.",
  "37": "“Reallocating the sanctity of ˹these˺ months is an increase in disbelief, by which the disbelievers are led ˹far˺ astray. They adjust the sanctity one year and uphold it in another, only to maintain the number of months sanctified by Allah, violating the very months Allah has made sacred. Their evil deeds have been made appealing to them. And Allah does not guide the disbelieving people” exposes the manipulation of the twelve-month system just declared as the Right Way. The phrase “Reallocating the sanctity of these months is an increase in disbelief” or “innama al-nasee’u ziyadatun fil-kufri” uses “nasee’,” to postpone, to reallocate sanctity, a pre-Islamic trick where a sacred month like Muharram was declared profane to allow raiding, then another month was made sacred to keep the count at four, preserving the number but violating the very month Allah made sacred, like changing “Hittah” to another word at the city gate and like the Sabbath town’s fish nets set before Sabbath. “Ziyadatun fil-kufr,” increase in disbelief, shows the trick is not a minor calendar error but an addition to kufr, the same kufr that denies signs and meeting makes deeds vain, that makes the scale light, that makes the gates not open.\n\nThe mechanism “by which the disbelievers are led far astray” or “yudallu bihi allatheena kafaroo” shows the trick’s effect: it misleads, like Satan’s beautification that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter to be pleased and persistent, and like devils plunging associates deeper without sparing effort. Adjusting sanctity one year and upholding another to maintain the number while violating the sacred is the same elegant, formalistic deception as hoarders who consume wrongfully while appearing pious.\n\nThe verdict “Their evil deeds have been made appealing to them. And Allah does not guide the disbelieving people” or “zuyyina lahum soo’u a‘malihim wa Allahu la yahdi al-qawma al-kafireen” repeats that beautification is both self-chosen and left to harden, as with Pharaoh’s wont, the worst beings, and the heedless cattle-like, and that lack of guidance follows persistent disbelief as after seeing every sign they still would not believe and prefer the crooked path.\n\nA common misunderstanding excuses calendar manipulation as administrative. The Quran calls it a disbelieving increase that, like spending to hinder, becomes regret, like preparing to violate treaties every time without taqwa, and like saying “We will be forgiven” while seizing lowly gain again. The remedy is the same as holding firmly to the Scripture under the raised mountain and observing its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful, so perhaps you will return, holding the twelve and four as written, fighting together as they fight together, knowing Allah is with the mindful.",
  "38": "“O believers! What is the matter with you that when you are asked to march forth in the cause of Allah, you cling firmly to ˹your˺ land? Do you prefer the life of this world over the Hereafter? The enjoyment of this worldly life is insignificant compared to that of the Hereafter” directly challenges the reluctance that follows the calendar corruption and hoarding warnings. The phrase “What is the matter with you that when you are asked to march forth in the cause of Allah, you cling firmly to your land?” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo ma lakum itha qeela lakum infiroo fi sabeeli Allahi iththaqaltum ila al-ardi” uses “iththaqaltum,” to become heavy, to cling heavily to the earth, the same heaviness as “akhlada ila al-ardi” clung to this life following desires like the dog panting whether chased or left, and like the fertile expectation to hold firmly to the Tablets but instead clinging to land. The call “infiroo,” march forth, is like the winds sent to drive heavy clouds, like the angels making firm and the rain fir m ing steps, a light command that the heavy earth-bound heart resists.\n\nThe diagnosis “Do you prefer the life of this world over the Hereafter? The enjoyment of this worldly life is insignificant compared to that of the Hereafter” or “a-radeetum bil-hayati al-dunya mina al-akhirah fa-ma mata‘u al-hayati al-dunya fil-akhirati illa qaleel” mirrors the earlier “The enjoyment of this worldly life is play and amusement, but far better is the Home of the Hereafter for those mindful” and “The Home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful, will you not understand?” and the everlasting Gardens with great reward with Allah versus the arad al-dunya fleeting gains and the lowly gain seized claiming “We will be forgiven.” Insubstantial, or “qaleel,” little, like the small price sold for revelations and the hoarded gold heated to brand foreheads, sides, and backs, shows the trade is poor, like the dog’s panting for the earth.\n\nThis reluctance links to Ḥunain’s pride in numbers and to Tabuk’s distance that seemed too long, where gain within reach would have been followed but the long march exposed hearts in doubt torn by doubts, and to the wealth and children fitnah and the beloveds more dear than Allah, Messenger, and striving that makes one wait for His Will as rebellious.\n\nA common error excuses clinging as prudence. The verse calls it preference for dunya over akhirah, the same preference that made successors indulge and the heedless like cattle, and that Allah counters with the next warning “If you do not march forth, He will afflict you with painful torment and replace you with other people, you are not harming Him, Allah is Most Capable” showing the heavy earth is not a refuge, for the earth belongs to Allah who grants it to whom He chooses and whose outcome belongs to the righteous.",
  "39": "“If you do not march forth, He will afflict you with a painful torment and replace you with other people. You are not harming Him in the least. And Allah is Most Capable of everything” gives the consequence of clinging heavily to the land and preferring the little enjoyment of this world over the Hereafter. The previous verse asked what is the matter when asked to march you cling to land, preferring dunya, which is little compared to akhirah. This verse says the clinging will not preserve the land; Allah will. The threat “He will afflict you with a painful torment and replace you with other people” or “illa tanfiroo yu‘aththibkum athaban aleeman wa yastabdil qawman ghairakum” mirrors the replacement warning after “How many societies We destroyed” and “We dispersed them through land in groups” and “We would have overwhelmed them with blessings had they been faithful but they disbelieved so We seized them.” Replacement, or “yastabdil,” is the same succession as “He made you successors after former residents” and “He made the oppressed successors of eastern and western blessed lands” and “perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors to see what you will do.” The oppressor succeeded after being few, now the clingers will be succeeded if they cling, showing the earth’s grant is conditional on striving and not on hoarding gold or reallocating sacred months, which only increases in disbelief.\n\nThe reassurance “You are not harming Him in the least. And Allah is Most Capable of everything” or “wa la tadurroohu shay’an wa Allahu ala kulli shay’in qadeer” uses the capability that created heavens and earth in six Days, established the Throne, makes night and day overlap, subjects sun, moon, stars, sends unseen forces and sakeenah, and is Lord of all worlds, so failure to march harms only the lingerers, as earlier “You will not harm Him if you do not support him, for Allah already supported him when disbelievers drove him out and he was one of two in the cave” and the tenfold and deterrent horses whose spending is paid in full.\n\nThis warning connects to the wealth and children fitnah and to the beloveds more beloved than Allah, whose love determines whether march is easy or difficult, as later “March forth whether easy or difficult, strive with wealth and lives, that is best if only you knew.”\n\nA common misunderstanding trusts that clinging to land preserves it. The verse says Allah will replace the clingers with others, as He replaced those who felt secure from His planning at night while asleep or day while at play, and as He will show the home of the rebellious, proving the land belongs to Allah, outcome to the righteous, not to the heavy.",
  "40": "“˹It does not matter˺ if you ˹believers˺ do not support him, for Allah did in fact support him when the disbelievers drove him out ˹of Mecca˺ and he was only one of two. While they both were in the cave, he reassured his companion, ‘Do not worry; Allah is certainly with us.’ So Allah sent down His serenity upon the Prophet, supported him with forces you ˹believers˺ did not see, and made the word of the disbelievers lowest, while the Word of Allah is supreme. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” consoles after the replacement threat, grounding the call to march in the cave’s lesson. The verse opens with the declaration of the divine sufficiency: “It does not matter if you believers do not support him, for Allah did in fact support him when the disbelievers drove him out of Mecca and he was only one of two,” or “illa tansuroohu fa-qad nasarahu Allahu ith akhrajahu allatheena kafaroo thaniya ithnayni,” and the commentators explain the scene: the verse recalls the Hijrah, the expulsion of the Prophet from Mecca by the disbelievers, and the moment when he was “one of two,” the second of the two, and the commentators identify the second as Abu Bakr as-Siddiq, the companion of the cave, and the point of the verse is the sufficiency of God, for the Prophet who had no supporter but the one companion was supported by the Lord, and the Lord who supported him then supports him now, whether the believers march with him or hold back. Ibn Kathir notes the occasion: the verse was revealed in the context of the expedition of Tabuk, when some of the believers were slow to march, and the verse answers their hesitation with the reminder that the cause of the Prophet does not depend upon them, for the Lord who protected him in the cave protects him always.\n\nThe verse then describes the cave: “While they both were in the cave, he reassured his companion, ‘Do not worry; Allah is certainly with us,’” or “ith huma fi al-ghari ith yaqoolu li-sahibihi la tahzan inna Allaha ma‘ana.” The commentators describe the scene in full: the Prophet and Abu Bakr took refuge in the cave of Thawr during the flight from Mecca, and the pursuers of the Quraysh reached the very mouth of the cave, and Abu Bakr, fearing for the Prophet, said, “If one of them looks at his feet, he will see us,” and the Prophet answered with the words that the verse preserves: “Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us,” and the commentators note the tenderness of the address, the “la tahzan” that calmed the companion, and the “inna Allaha ma‘ana” that was the ground of the calm, for the presence of the Lord was the protection that the cave and the pursuers could not touch. Then the divine response: “So Allah sent down His serenity upon the Prophet, supported him with forces you believers did not see, and made the word of the disbelievers lowest, while the Word of Allah is supreme,” or “fa-anzala Allahu sakeenatahu ‘alayhi wa ayyadahu bi-junoodin lam tarawha wa ja‘ala kalimata allatheena kafaroo as-sufla wa kalimatu Allahi hiya al-‘ulya,” the serenity that descended upon the Prophet, the unseen forces that supported him, and the two words — the word of the disbelievers lowered and the Word of God made supreme — and the verse closes with the two names: “And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise,” or “wallahu ‘azeezun hakeem.” Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the complete portrait of the divine sufficiency: the cave, the companion, the serenity, the forces, and the two words, and the believer reads in it the truth that the cause of the Prophet is upheld by the Lord, not by the supporters, and that the one who is with God is the one whom no cave can confine and no enemy can reach.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the companionship, and the commentators connect it to the station of Abu Bakr. The “one of two” and the “companion” of the verse are the titles of Abu Bakr, the “Sahib,” the companion, the second of the two in the cave, and the commentators note that the verse is the Quran’s own testimony to the station of Abu Bakr, the man who shared the cave and the fear and the reassurance, and whose companionship is the model of the believer’s loyalty to the messenger of God. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the eternal record of the cave, and that the believer reads in it the comfort of the presence: the one who is with the Lord, even as one of two, is the one whom the Lord supports with the serenity and the forces, and the word of the Lord is supreme, whatever the word of the disbelievers may claim. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the cave: the one of two, the companion reassured, and the serenity sent — the sufficiency of the Lord, and the supremacy of His Word.",
  "41": "“˹O believers!˺ March forth whether it is easy or difficult for you, and strive with your wealth and your lives in the cause of Allah. That is best for you, if only you knew” generalizes the march after the cave’s proof that support does not depend on numbers. The previous verse said if you do not support him, Allah already supported him as second of two in the cave with serenity and unseen forces, making the disbelievers’ word lowest and Allah’s supreme. This verse says “March forth whether it is easy or difficult for you, and strive with your wealth and your lives in the cause of Allah” or “infiroo khifafan wa thiqalan wa jahidoo bi-amwalikum wa anfusikum fi sabeeli Allah.” “Khifafan wa thiqalan,” light or heavy, easy or difficult, young or old, well-equipped or barely, mirrors the earlier “whether it is easy or difficult” after the wealth and children fitnah and the beloveds more beloved test, and the “cling heavily to land” heaviness that made the earth close at Ḥunain. The command covers both states, as earlier “obey and do not dispute, persevere, Allah is with those who persevere” and “prepare what you can of power and horses” covers what is within capacity.\n\nStriving “with your wealth and your lives” or “bi-amwalikum wa anfusikum” places the two loves that rival Allah, Messenger, and striving in the beloveds list, the same wealth that is a test and the same lives that are entrusted, now to be spent as infaq in Allah’s cause that will be paid in full, not as hoarded gold that will brand foreheads, sides, and backs. Striving in His Way is the same striving that makes guardians of one another to prevent great oppression, that makes ranks greater with Allah and triumph, that makes the true believers’ forgiveness and honourable provision.\n\nThe evaluation “That is best for you, if only you knew” or “thalikum khairun lakum in kuntum ta‘lamoon” uses “khair,” better, as earlier the Home of the Hereafter is far better for the mindful, the Gardens with everlasting bliss are great reward, and the enjoyment of this world is little compared to the Hereafter. Best is measured by the All-Knowing, All-Wise, the Almighty who sent sakeenah in the cave, not by the fleeting gain within reach that would have been followed if the journey were shorter but the long distance exposed the lying, ruined selves.\n\nA common misunderstanding waits for easy conditions to march. The verse says both easy and difficult are commanded, because the earth belongs to Allah who grants it to whom He chooses, whose outcome belongs to the righteous, and because the Hour is too tremendous to wait for ease. For the heedless like cattle with hearts not understanding, the march with wealth and lives is the fertile production that varies lessons abundantly and that, when the Quran is recited, listens attentively and silently so mercy is shown.",
  "42": "“Had the gain been within reach and the journey shorter, they would have followed you, but the distance seemed too long for them. And they will swear by Allah, ‘Had we been able, we would have certainly joined you.’ They are ruining themselves. And Allah knows that they are surely lying” dissects the psychology behind clinging to land and preferring the little enjoyment. After commanding to march whether easy or difficult and strive with wealth and lives as the best if only you knew, this verse explains who did not. The condition “Had the gain been within reach and the journey shorter, they would have followed you, but the distance seemed too long for them” or “law kana aradan qareeban wa safaran qasidan la-ittaba‘ooka wa lakin ba‘udat alaihimu al-shuqqah” uses “arad qareeb,” near gain, within reach, the same lowly near gain successors seized claiming “We will be forgiven,” and “safaran qasidan,” moderate journey, like the unarmed caravan wished for at Badr, the easy path where no persecution remains and no mountains are raised like a cloud. “Ba‘udat alaihim al-shuqqah,” distance was far, heaviness, difficulty, the same heaviness as “iththaqaltum ila al-ardi,” clinging to land, and “akhlada ila al-ardi,” clinging to earth like the dog panting. The long march to Tabuk through heat exposed the gap between claim and heart.\n\nThe oaths that follow “And they will swear by Allah, ‘Had we been able, we would have certainly joined you.’ They are ruining themselves. And Allah knows that they are surely lying” or “wa sa-yahlifoona billahi law istata‘na la-kharajna ma‘akum yuhlikoona anfusahum wa Allahu ya‘lamu innahum la-kathiboon” shows the same false “We hear but do not listen” and “We will be forgiven” while seizing again, the same “If you had known any goodness He would have made them hear, but even if made to hear they would turn heedlessly.” The self-ruin, or “yuhlikoona anfusahum,” is like the worst deaf and dumb, the lowing calf wrongdoers, the deed vain, the woes who persist, the scales light who doom themselves.\n\nA common error excuses the oath as politeness. The Quran calls it lying self-ruin known to the All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in hearts and who stands between person and heart. For those who seek goodness in captives’ hearts to give better and forgive, this shows the criterion is not oath but preparation, as the next verse will say “Had they intended to march they would have prepared, but Allah disliked they should go so He let them lag and it was said ‘Stay with those who remain,’” distinguishing true inability from unwilling heaviness.",
  "43": "“May Allah pardon you ˹O Prophet˺! Why did you give them permission ˹to stay behind˺ before those who told the truth were distinguished from those who were lying?” gently corrects the Prophet’s permission that allowed the unready to stay. The previous verse said had gain been near and journey short they would have followed, but distance seemed long and they swore “Had we been able we would have joined,” ruining themselves, Allah knows they lie. This verse says “May Allah pardon you! Why did you give them permission to stay behind before those who told the truth were distinguished from those who were lying?” or “afa Allahu anka lima athinta lahum hatta yatabayyana laka allatheena sadaqoo wa ta‘lama al-kathibeen.” The pardon, or “afa Allahu anka,” is gracious, like the most beautiful names and the cave’s serenity, showing the Prophet’s ijtihad to give permission was from mercy, not from betrayal; the correction is to make distinction, or “yatabayyana,” clear, like the furqan for the mindful, the variations for the thankful, the barrier recognizing by marks. Those who told truth, or “sadaqoo,” versus those lying, or “kathibeen,” mirrors the same truth-lie divide that made the staff devour great magic where truth prevailed.\n\nThe permission had been given before the truth-tellers were distinguished, so the hypocrites’ “We are ruined” remained hidden, like the Sabbath town’s middle group that asked “Why warn them, they will be destroyed,” or the wealth inheritors well-versed yet seizing. Distinguishing requires the test of marching, as earlier “Do you think you will be left without Allah proving who truly struggles and never takes allies other than Allah, Messenger, believers? Allah is All-Aware” and as later “Had they intended to march they would have prepared, but Allah disliked they should go, so He let them lag and it was said Stay with those who remain.” The disliked lag is itself a distinction, showing Allah stands between person and heart.\n\nA common misunderstanding views the pardon as blame. The Quran frames it as honourable correction, as with Moses’ anger then “Forgive me and my brother, You are most merciful,” showing even the elevated chosen by messages and speech need pardon, and that the community’s welfare requires that exemptions be granted only after the mindful are known by their striving with wealth and lives, not by oaths that Allah knows are lies.\n\nFor the march whether easy or difficult that is best if only you knew, this teaches the leader to hold the standard of truth before granting leave, so the fifth, the spoils lawful and good, and the great reward with Allah remain for those whose preparation proves truth, and so the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers does not spread through unexamined permission.",
  "44": "“Those who believe in Allah and the Last Day do not ask for exemption from striving with their wealth and their lives. And Allah has perfect knowledge of those who are mindful ˹of Him˺” defines who would not have asked for leave, contrasting the truth-tellers to be distinguished from liars in the pardon. The previous verse pardoned the Prophet for giving permission before distinguishing, and asked why permission was given before truth-tellers were known. This verse says “Those who believe in Allah and the Last Day do not ask for exemption from striving with their wealth and their lives” or “la yasta’thinuka allatheena yu’minoona billahi wa al-yawmi al-akhiri an yujahidoo bi-amwalihim wa anfusihim.” The description repeats the greater-in-rank triad—believed, emigrated, strove with wealth and lives—and the true believers who establish prayer and donate from what He provided, whose hearts tremble and faith increases. Not asking for exemption mirrors the fertile land producing abundantly for the thankful, the firm mountain held, the twelve springs known by each tribe, and the sincere advice that does not seek to make the Way seem crooked.\n\nThe second clause “And Allah has perfect knowledge of those who are mindful of Him” or “wa Allahu aleemun bil-muttaqeen” uses “aleem,” the All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in hearts, who knows any goodness to make hear, who stands between person and heart, who heard the cave’s “Allah is with us” and sent sakeenah, who knows the goodness in captives’ hearts to give better and forgive. His knowledge makes the distinction that permission should have awaited, now promised as furqan for the mindful, absolute. The mindful, or “muttaqeen,” are those who wear the best clothing, who remember the mountain, who do not wrong one another during sacred months, who prepare power to deter and incline to peace and trust the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\n\nThis connects to the next verse, where only those with no faith, no Last Day, and hearts in doubt ask for exemption and are torn by doubts, the same doubtful who took parents as allies if they choose disbelief and whose love for wealth, trade, homes outweighs Allah, Messenger, and striving, making them rebellious not guided.\n\nA common error imagines exemption requests are neutral. The Quran says the true believer, mindful of the Hereafter whose Gardens are great reward forever, does not request exemption from striving with wealth and lives, because marching whether easy or difficult is seen as best, as lawful and good after the prior decree, as the honourable provision that elevates ranks. The leader’s pardon teaches to distinguish, but this verse teaches the believer to be of those who need no pardon for asking.",
  "45": "“No one would ask for exemption except those who have no faith in Allah or the Last Day, and whose hearts are in doubt, so they are torn by their doubts” completes the dichotomy between those who do not ask and those who do. After saying those who believe in Allah and the Last Day do not ask for exemption from striving with wealth and lives and Allah has perfect knowledge of the mindful, this verse says “No one would ask for exemption except those who have no faith in Allah or the Last Day, and whose hearts are in doubt, so they are torn by their doubts” or “innama yasta’thinuka allatheena la yu’minoona billahi wa la bil-yawmi al-akhiri wa irtabat quloobuhum fa-hum fi raybihim yataraddadoon.” The exempt-seekers are precisely those lacking the two pillars of the maintainer’s mosque—belief in Allah and Last Day, establishing prayer, paying zakat, fearing none but Allah—and whose hearts are sick, the same sickness in hypocrites who said believers are deluded and the same doubt that makes the heavy earth close at Hunain when pride in numbers fails.\n\nThe phrase “whose hearts are in doubt, so they are torn by their doubts” or “irtabat quloobuhum fa-hum fi raybihim yataraddadoon” echoes the turning away from signs for arrogance and the wandering blindly in defiance for those Allah leaves to stray, the same “yataraddadoon” hesitation as the thunderstruck mountain. Their doubt, or “rayb,” is the same rayb that made successors say “We will be forgiven” while seizing lowly gain, and that made the Sabbath town think fish tricks hide from the never-absent.\n\nThis connects to the earlier wealth-and-children fitnah and beloveds test: the doubt-ridden love near gain, near journey, and fear the long march that seemed too far, so they swear “Had we been able we would have joined” ruining themselves, Allah knows they lie, and the next verse will say had they intended to march they would have prepared, but Allah disliked they should go so He let them lag and it was said “Stay with the helpless who remain,” confirming the heart’s doubt decides preparation.\n\nA common misunderstanding pities exemption seekers as overwhelmed. The Quran diagnoses lack of faith and Last Day and a doubtful heart torn by doubt, the fertile opposite of trembling hearts that increase in faith when revelations are recited and trust in the Lord, Almighty, All-Wise. For those who seek goodness in captives’ hearts to give better and forgive, this shows goodness is the opposite of doubt, and the All-Knowing, All-Wise distinguishes, granting furqan to the mindful, whose sins are absolved and forgiven by the Lord of infinite bounty.",
  "46": "“Had they ˹really˺ intended to march forth, they would have made preparations for it. But Allah disliked that they should go, so He let them lag behind, and it was said ˹to them˺, ‘Stay with those ˹helpless˺ who remain behind’” tests the claim of the exempt-seekers whose hearts are in doubt and torn by doubts. The previous verse said only those with no faith and doubt in hearts ask for exemption and are torn. This verse gives the practical sign: “Had they really intended to march forth, they would have made preparations for it” or “wa law aradoo al-khurooja la-a‘addoo lahu uddah.” Preparation, or “uddah,” includes arranging mounts, provisions, weapons, the same preparation commanded as “Prepare against them what you can of power and cavalry” and the same striving with wealth and lives. The fact they made none proves intention was absent, like the Sabbath fish nets prepared before Sabbath proved intention to violate.\n\nThe divine response “But Allah disliked that they should go, so He let them lag behind, and it was said to them, ‘Stay with those helpless who remain behind’” or “wa lakin kariha Allahu inbi‘athahum fa-thabbat ahum wa qeela uq‘udoo ma‘a al-qa‘ideen” uses “kariha,” disliked, the same dislike that makes Him not like transgressors and betrayers and hoarders, here disliking their going because their presence would be trouble, scrambling to spread discord, with some believers eagerly listening, as the next verse warns. “Thabbat ahum,” He made them lag, held them back, is like the mountain raised and the hearts sealed for those who even if they see every sign will not believe, where the heedless are turned away for arrogance.\n\nThe command “Stay with those helpless who remain behind” or “uq‘udoo ma‘a al-qa‘ideen” lumps the able-bodied hypocrites with the truly helpless—women, children, ill, and the excused poor—exposing their choice as a preference for the helpless bench despite being able, the opposite of the mindful who march whether easy or difficult.\n\nA common misunderstanding pities their lagging as fate. The verse shows lagging follows lack of preparation, which follows lack of intention, which follows lack of faith and Last Day. For the community told “Respond to what gives life, Allah stands between person and heart, beware fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers,” this warns that the heart between which Allah stands can be left to lag if preparation for the cause is abandoned, and that the best clothing to march is readiness, not the oath “Had we been able we would have joined” that Allah knows is lying.",
  "47": "“Had they gone forth with you ˹believers˺, they would have been nothing but trouble for you, and would have scrambled around, seeking to spread discord in your midst. And some of you would have eagerly listened to them. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the wrongdoers” explains the mercy in disliking that they should go. The previous verse said had they intended they would have prepared, but Allah disliked their going and let them lag, saying “Stay with the helpless.” This verse reveals what their going would have caused: “Had they gone forth with you believers, they would have been nothing but trouble for you” or “law kharajoo feekum ma zadookum illa khabala.” “Khabal,” confusion, corruption, trouble, is the same corruption warned “Do not spread corruption after it was set in order” and “great corruption in the land if you do not guard each other.” Hypocrites going would have increased it, not decreased.\n\nThe method “and would have scrambled around, seeking to spread discord in your midst” or “wa la-awda‘oo khilalakum yabghoonakumu al-fitnah” describes scurrying, rushing through your ranks to seek fitnah, the same fitnah that is wealth and children, that will not only affect wrongdoers, that is persecution until devotion is entirely for Allah. Their fitnah is the same beautified elegant words that incline hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter and the same devils who persistently plunge associates deeper without sparing effort.\n\nThe warning “And some of you would have eagerly listened to them” or “wa feekum samma‘oona lahum” acknowledges that even among true believers, hearts trembling and trusting, some are eager listeners to rumour, the same listeners warned “Do not be like those who say we hear but do not listen, the worst deaf and dumb.” This eagerness is not hypocrisy but a vulnerability that the hypocrites exploit, as earlier those who took devils as masters thought they were guided.\n\nThe seal “And Allah has perfect knowledge of the wrongdoers” or “wa Allahu aleemun bi-al-zalimeen” parallels “Allah has perfect knowledge of the mindful” for those who do not ask exemption, showing He knows who is zalim, transgressor who does not honour kinship or treaties, whose spending to hinder is evil, whose hoarding will brand foreheads.\n\nA common error assumes more numbers always help, even hypocrites. The verse shows their presence would have added only khabal and fitnah, just as Hunain’s pride in great numbers proved of no advantage and earth closed. The divine dislike that let them lag was thus mercy to the believers who firmly abide and establish prayer, and justice to the wrongdoers whose preparation never came because intention never was.",
  "48": "“They had already sought to spread discord before and devised every ˹possible˺ plot against you ˹O Prophet˺, until the truth came and Allah’s Will prevailed—much to their dismay” places the Tabuk lagging in a longer history of hypocrisy. The previous verse said had they gone they would have been trouble, scrambling to spread discord, with some eager listeners, and Allah has perfect knowledge of wrongdoers. This verse says the discord is not new: “They had already sought to spread discord before and devised every possible plot against you O Prophet, until the truth came and Allah’s Will prevailed—much to their dismay” or “la-qad ibtaghaw al-fitnata min qablu wa qallaboo laka al-umoor hatta ja’a al-haqqu wa zahara amru Allahi wa hum karihoon.” The earlier plots include the capture-kill-exile conspiracy where Allah planned and is best of planners, the “We will kill sons” after the magicians believed, the treaty violations every time, the whistling prayer and spending to hinder until regret, the “We will be forgiven” seizure, and the Tabuk-specific plots like the mosque of harm built to divide.\n\nThe phrase “until the truth came and Allah’s Will prevailed—much to their dismay” or “hatta ja’a al-haqqu wa zahara amru Allahi wa hum karihoon” mirrors the truth prevailing over illusions when the staff devoured great magic and the purpose of Badr to establish truth and wipe out falsehood even to the wicked’s dismay, and the light perfected even to disbelievers’ dismay and religion of truth prevailing over all even to polytheists’ dismay. “Karihoon,” dismay, averse, is the same aversion that made the brave wish for the unarmed caravan and the hoarders dislike spending in Allah’s cause, the same aversion the heights’ entrants will not fear.\n\nThis history shows the hypocrites’ pattern is increase in disbelief by reallocating sacred months, beautified deeds made appealing, and planning that Allah frustrates. Their planning is the same kaid that Allah promised to frustrate as moohinu kaidi al-kafireen and whose flawless planning, mateen, gives rope in ways they cannot comprehend.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats Tabuk’s hypocrites as a new group. The verse says they sought discord before and plotted every plot, so the Tabuk test where gain within reach would have been followed but long distance exposed them is the culmination, not the start, of the fertile-infertile distinction where the same wind-driven rain made one land produce abundantly and the other hardly. For the mindful granted furqan, the lesson is to see the plot history and not be among those who would have eagerly listened to them, but among those who firmly abide and whose reward is never discounted.",
  "49": "“There are some of them who say, ‘Exempt me and do not expose me to temptation.’ They have already fallen into temptation. And Hell will surely engulf the disbelievers” quotes the excuse of a specific hypocrite who tried to avoid Tabuk by claiming moral danger. The phrase “There are some of them who say, ‘Exempt me and do not expose me to temptation’” or “wa minhum man yaqoolu i’than li wa la taftinnee” was said by a man who argued that marching against the Byzantines, with their fair women and wealth, would tempt him, a claim that mirrors the Beloveds more dear test where parents, spouses, trade, and homes rival love for Allah. The excuse inverts reality: asking exemption to avoid fitnah is itself falling into fitnah, the same fitnah that wealth and children are, that will not only affect wrongdoers, that is persecution until devotion is entirely for Allah, and that the hypocrites had already sought before.\n\nThe divine verdict “They have already fallen into temptation” or “ala fil-fitnati saqatoo” uses “saqatoo,” fell, collapsed, like the mountain levelled to dust when it could not remain firm, and like the disbelievers who turned backs and earned displeasure, whose home is Hell, the evil destination. Fallen into fitnah here means they chose the very temptation they claimed to avoid, the same temptation the rabbinic lordship and hoarding and sacred-month reallocating represent, all of which increase in disbelief and beautify deeds.\n\nThe threat “And Hell will surely engulf the disbelievers” or “wa inna Jahannama la-muheetatun bil-kafireen” uses “muheetah,” encompassing, the same encompassing that Allah is Fully Aware of what they do when leaving homes arrogantly to be seen, the same never absent when giving a full account, the same encompassing knowledge that wrote twelve months in the Record since He created heavens and earth. Hell’s encompassing is the counterpart to the best clothing of taqwa that covers, and to the shade of clouds and the shelter when few; for the transgressor, the cover becomes flames as bed and flames as cover for the wicked, and the encompassing becomes Hell.\n\nA common error romanticizes temptation-avoidance as piety. The Quran exposes that seeking exemption from marching whether easy or difficult, the best if only you knew, under the guise of avoiding fitnah, is the fitnah itself, as earlier the stone-prayer asked for stones if truth rather than guidance. The next verses will show the hypocrites grieve at blessing befalling the Prophet but rejoice saying “We took precaution” when disaster befalls, proving the exemption was not precaution but desire to remain with the helpless while claiming fear of temptation.",
  "50": "“If a blessing befalls you ˹O Prophet˺, they grieve, but if a disaster befalls you, they say, ‘We took our precaution in advance,’ and turn away, rejoicing” describes the emotional pattern of those who said “Exempt me, do not tempt me” and already fell into fitnah. The verse says “If a blessing befalls you O Prophet, they grieve, but if a disaster befalls you, they say, ‘We took our precaution in advance,’ and turn away, rejoicing” or “in tusibka hasanatun tasu’hum wa in tusibka museebatun yaqooloo qad akhathna amrana min qablu wa yatawallaw wa hum farihoon.” Blessing, or “hasanah,” includes Badr’s victory, Hunain’s reassurance and unseen forces, the opening of Mecca, the honourable provision and elevated ranks for true believers, all of which the believers’ hearts trembling and trusting celebrate. The hypocrites grieve, or “tasu’hum,” it harms them, like the hypocrites and sick-hearted who called believers deluded, whose spending to hinder became regret, whose whistling prayer was void.\n\nDisaster, or “museebah,” includes the earth closing at Hunain, the turning backs in retreat, the thirst at Tabuk’s heat, the cave’s second-of-two fear. When it befalls the Prophet, they say “We took our precaution in advance” or “qad akhathna amrana min qablu” we took our affair before, precaution like the hypocrites’ “We took precaution” that the next verse will contrast with “Nothing will befall us except what Allah has destined, He is our Protector, in Him let believers trust.” Their turning away rejoicing, or “wa yatawallaw wa hum farihoon,” mirrors the “We will kill sons” boasting and the “This is what we deserve” in prosperity and blaming Moses in adversity, the same joy that made the sacred months’ reallocators feel clever while their deeds were made appealing.\n\nThis pattern reveals the heart between which Allah stands: the true believers respond to what gives life and know to Him they will be gathered, while these grieve at hasanah and rejoice at museebah, proving they have hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, even less than cattle, entirely heedless, destined for Hell sa Hell will engulf disbelievers.\n\nA common misunderstanding views grieving at another’s blessing as private envy. The Quran makes it a sign of the same rebellious who violate treaties every time and of the hoarders whose treasure will brand foreheads, sides, backs, showing the fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers spreads through such hearts. The next verse’s cure is to say “Nothing will befall us except what Allah has destined, He is our Protector, in Him let believers trust,” the same trust that made twenty steadfast overcome two hundred and that united hearts when riches could not.",
  "51": "“Say, ‘Nothing will ever befall us except what Allah has destined for us. He is our Protector.’ So in Allah let the believers put their trust” is the answer to the grief-rejoice pattern just described. The previous verse showed hypocrites grieve at blessing for the Prophet but say “We took precaution” and rejoice at disaster. This verse commands the Prophet and believers to declare the creed that counters precaution as self-saving charm. The opening “Say, ‘Nothing will ever befall us except what Allah has destined for us’” or “qul lan yuseebana illa ma kataba Allahu lana” uses “kataba,” to write, to ordain, the same writing that ordained twelve months in the Record since He created heavens and earth, the same writing that made spoils lawful and that wrote hasanah in both worlds for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, the same decree that made the valley positions and the dream few and the double fewness. What befalls, or “yuseebana,” is the same touching that afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine to make them remember and that tested the Sabbath town with fish, now placed under divine writing.\n\nThe next phrase “He is our Protector” or “huwa mawlana” declares the only Wali, the same Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, who brought hearts together when riches could not, who is sufficient for the Prophet and followers, whose earth belongs to Him to grant to whom He chooses and whose outcome is for the righteous, the same Mawla and Naseer excellent protector and helper for those who face an enemy, stand firm, and remember often. Mawla contrasts with the disbelievers who are guardians of one another and whose guardianship leads to great oppression, and with the parents and siblings taken as allies if they choose disbelief over belief, the true wrongdoers.\n\nThe seal “So in Allah let the believers put their trust” or “wa ala Allahi fa-l-yatawakkali al-mu’minoon” repeats the trust that defines true believers whose hearts tremble and who are Almighty with a thousand angels and All-Wise, whose trust made Hunain’s reassurance and unseen forces and whose trust answers the hypocrites’ “deluded by their faith” taunt with “Whoever trusts in Allah, Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” Trust is the same reliance Moses urged “Seek help and be patient” and Shu‘ayb placed “In Allah we trust.”\n\nA common error thinks precaution exempts from what is written, as the hypocrites’ “We took precaution” implies. The verse says precaution without trust is the fallen fitnah itself, while trust with precaution—preparing power and horses to deter, repenting, praying, paying—is the fertile path where nothing befalls except what is written for us, not against us, the hasanah that removes rage and is pardoned by the All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  "52": "“Say, ‘Are you awaiting anything to befall us except one of the two best things: ˹victory or martyrdom˺? But We are awaiting Allah to afflict you with torment either from Him or at our hands. So keep waiting! We too are waiting with you’” frames the waiting that both sides do after the creed “Nothing will befall us except what Allah has written.” The question “Are you awaiting anything to befall us except one of the two best things: victory or martyrdom?” or “qul hal tarabbasoona bina illa ihda al-husnayain” uses “tarabbasoon,” awaiting, the same waiting as “Then wait until Allah brings about His Will” for those who love kin and wealth more than Allah, and as “Wait until Allah judges” that Shu‘ayb and Hud offered. The two best, or “husnayain,” are victory, or “nasr,” and martyrdom, or “shahadah,” both good, as earlier “Those who believe, emigrated, strived, and helped are true believers, forgiveness and honourable provision” and “Their Lord gives them good news of mercy, pleasure, Gardens forever, great reward,” and as the Beautiful test where Allah killed and threw, rendering believers a great favour. Both are victory for the trusters, unlike the hypocrites’ awaiting of blessing to grieve and disaster to rejoice.\n\nThe counterpart “But We are awaiting Allah to afflict you with torment either from Him or at our hands” or “wa nahnu natarabbasu bikum an yuseebakumu Allahu bi-athabin min indihi aw bi-aideena” mirrors “Fight them and Allah will punish at your hands, put to shame, help, soothe, remove rage” and the general “We afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine so they might remember” and “We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend” and the flawless planning that gives rope. Torment from Him is like the quake, brimstone, flood, and the punishment at night while asleep or day while at play; at our hands is like Badr’s necks and fingertips struck, the fearsome example to deter followers, and the “kill the violators wherever you find them, capture, besiege, lie in wait” after sacred months.\n\nThe closing “So keep waiting! We too are waiting with you” or “fa-tarabbasoo inna ma‘akum mutarabbisoon” is the same “Keep waiting, we too are waiting” as earlier “They ask you when is the Hour, say knowledge only with Allah” and as the valley appointment where both sides waited for the destined matter. For the believers, waiting with Allah’s written good is hope and fear; for the hypocrites and treaty breakers, waiting is for torment whose knowledge is only with the Best of Planners who frustrates evil plans.\n\nA common misunderstanding sees victory as the only husna. The verse says martyrdom is also husna, both are better than the lowly gain seized while claiming “We will be forgiven” and then seized again.",
  "53": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘˹Whether you˺ donate willingly or unwillingly, it will never be accepted from you, for you have been a rebellious people’” addresses the donations of those awaiting the two best things for believers but torment for themselves. The previous verse left both sides waiting; this verse turns to the hypocrites’ spending, the counterpart to preparation spending that will be paid in full and to the fifth for orphans, poor, travellers. The phrase “Whether you donate willingly or unwillingly, it will never be accepted from you, for you have been a rebellious people” or “qul anfiqoo taw‘an aw karhan lan yutaqabbala minkum innakum kuntum qawman fasiqeen” uses “anfiqoo,” to spend, donate, the same verb for “donate from what We provided” for true believers whose trembling hearts increase in faith, and for “those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend in Allah’s cause” and “spending to hinder.” Willingly, or “taw‘an,” or unwillingly, or “karhan,” covers both showy compliance and grudging compulsion, as earlier they come to prayer half-heartedly and donate resentfully, and as later they are critical of alms distribution, pleased if given, enraged if not.\n\nThe reason “for you have been a rebellious people” or “innakum kuntum qawman fasiqeen” names fisq, stepping out, rebellious, the same fisq of most found not true to covenant, of the Sabbath town tested for rebelliousness, of those turned away from signs for arrogance who even if they see every sign will not believe, of the hoarders whose treasure will brand, and of the disbelievers whose evil plans are frustrated. Rebellion severs the root that makes donations accepted, which is belief in Allah and Last Day, not taking parents as allies if they prefer disbelief, and the five traits for maintaining mosques.\n\nThis connects to the next verse’s fuller diagnosis: what prevented donations from being accepted is they lost faith, come to prayer half-heartedly, donate resentfully.\n\nA common error thinks any donation counts regardless of heart. The Quran says rebellious fisq blocks acceptance whether willing or unwilling, just as the calf worshippers’ deeds were void and the whistling prayer was nothing but whistling. The contrast is spending to deter Allah’s enemies and your enemies as well as unknown but known to Allah, which will be paid in full without wrong, and the alms-tax only for the poor, needy, administrators, those whose hearts are attracted, freeing slaves, debtors, Allah’s cause, and travellers, an obligation from Allah, All-Knowing, All-Wise, where the rebellious have no share.",
  "54": "“And what prevented their donations from being accepted is that they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger, they never come to prayer except half-heartedly, and they never donate except resentfully” details the three inward reasons behind the rejection just announced. The previous verse said whether you donate willingly or unwillingly, it will never be accepted for you are a rebellious people. This verse explains why: “And what prevented their donations from being accepted is that they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger” or “wa ma mana‘ahum an tuqbala minhum nafaqatuhum illa annahum kafaroo billahi wa bi-rasoolihi,” loss of faith in Allah and His Messenger, the foundation of the true believers’ five traits for mosque maintenance and of the “If you are mindful He will grant you furqan” and of the “Allah is with those who persevere.” Without faith, even gold hoarded wrongly and then offered remains within the bed of Hell with flames as cover.\n\nThe next two signs are the visible symptoms: “they never come to prayer except half-heartedly” or “wa la ya’toona al-salata illa wa hum kusala,” lazy, heavy, like those who cling firmly to land when asked to march forth, preferring the little enjoyment of this world, like those who said “Do not go to war in the heat” and were told the Fire of Hell is hotter, like those whose eyes will turn to Hell and pray not to be joined with wrongdoers but whose bodies lagged. Prayer half-heartedly is the opposite of establishing prayer, the same establishing praised for the true believers and for those who firmly abide where reward is never discounted, and of remembering inwardly with humility morning and evening without heedlessness.\n\nThe third symptom “and they never donate except resentfully” or “wa la yunfiqoona illa wa hum karihoon” means they hate to spend, even when they spend willingly or unwillingly, the same karh that made the share for Allah be shared with associates while the associate’s share not shared, the same karh that made the hypocrites grieve at blessing and rejoice at disaster saying “We took precaution.” Resentful donation is the opposite of donating from what We provided and of the fifth for close relatives, orphans, poor, travellers, and of the spending that will be paid in full.\n\nTogether the three show the praying and donating body is present but the heart between which Allah stands is absent, like the cattle with hearts not understanding, ears not hearing, eyes not seeing, even less guided, entirely heedless. A common misunderstanding measures donations by amount. The Quran measures by faith, eagerness to prayer, and willingness to donate, without which even a mountain of gold remains the treasure heated to brand foreheads, sides, and backs where it will be said “Taste what you hoarded.”",
  "55": "“So let neither their wealth nor children impress you ˹O Prophet˺. Allah only intends to torment them through these things in this worldly life, then their souls will depart while they are disbelievers” warns the Prophet after diagnosing the hypocrites’ faithless, lazy, resentful worship. The phrase “So let neither their wealth nor children impress you O Prophet” or “fa-la tu‘jibka amwaluhum wa la awladuhum” uses “tu‘jibka,” to impress, to amaze, the same amazement the believers felt at Hunain’s great numbers that proved of no advantage and the earth closed, and the same delight many rabbis and monks cause when consuming wealth wrongfully and hindering. Wealth and children here are the same test, or “fitnah,” that “Wealth and children are only a test and with Allah is great reward” and that will not only affect wrongdoers, the same beloveds more dear than Allah, Messenger, and striving that make one wait for His Will as rebellious.\n\nThe divine intent “Allah only intends to torment them through these things in this worldly life” or “innama yureedu Allahu li-yu‘aththibahum biha fil-hayati al-dunya” reveals that the wealth that seems to be favour is in fact the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend, with flawless planning that gives rope, where the treasure hoarded will brand, where the spending to hinder becomes regret, where the Prince’s sufficiency is sufficient and the hoarder’s sufficiency is not. Torment through wealth includes anxiety to protect it, the fitnah of preparing for it, the laziness in prayer, the resentment in donating, the fear that makes them rush to refuges, caves, hiding-places, and the breaking of oaths every time.\n\nThe end “then their souls will depart while they are disbelievers” or “wa tazuha anfusuhum wa hum kafiroon” or “tazhaqa,” to depart with difficulty, describes the soul-taking beating faces and backs with “Taste burning” that follows the fitnah, the same departure warned as “If only you could see when angels take souls of disbelievers, beating faces and backs.” This worldly torment through wealth and children is the foretaste of that taking.\n\nA common error envies the hypocrites’ wealth and large families as signs of success. The verse says do not be impressed; those very things are the instrument of torment in this life for those whose donations are not accepted, whose hearts are in doubt torn by doubts, who lag behind saying “Stay with the helpless,” who would have been trouble if they had gone, who had already sought discord, who grieve at blessing and rejoice at disaster saying “We took precaution.” The true impressiveness is the everlasting Gardens forever, the great reward with Allah, the mercy, pleasure, and honourable provision for those who believed, emigrated, strove, sheltered, and helped, whose past, if they desist, will be forgiven.",
  "56": "“They swear by Allah that they are part of you, but they are not. They only say so out of fear” exposes the oath that hypocrites use to cover the wealth-children torment just described. The previous verse said not to be impressed by their wealth and children, for Allah intends to torment them through these in this world then their souls depart while disbelievers. This verse adds “They swear by Allah that they are part of you, but they are not” or “wa yahlifoona billahi innahum la-minkum wa ma hum minkum.” The oath by Allah is the same solemn oath that will be taken about Hour, that was taken about Hour, that will be taken on the Day “By Allah, our Lord, we were not polytheists” while lying, the same oath they will swear “Had we been able we would have joined you,” Allah knows they lie. Swearing they are part of you claims the wilayah, guardianship that makes believers guardians of one another and that prevents great oppression, the true guardianship of those who believed, emigrated, strove, sheltered, and helped, the guardianship that needs the twelve-month sacred way and the deterrent power and horses where spending is paid in full.\n\nThe correction “but they are not” severs the false claim, as earlier “It is not for polytheists to maintain mosques while they profess disbelief, their deeds void, they will be in Fire forever” severed void worship, and as “Do not take your parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief” severed kinship that chooses disbelief.\n\nThe motive “They only say so out of fear” or “wa lakin nahum qawmun yafraqoon” fear, not faith, the same fear that made them rush to refuges, caves, hiding-places, the same fear that made them ask for exemption “Do not expose me to temptation” while already fallen, the same fear that made them grieve at blessing and rejoice at disaster with “We took precaution.” Fear, or “faraq,” is the opposite of the humble secret fear that accompanies hope in calling Allah, the fear that removes bitterness in Paradise’s rivers. Their fear is worldly, of being exposed, of losing wealth and children that torment them, of being among the “If you encounter them make a fearsome example so perhaps others remember” and “Kill the violators wherever you find them” and “Fight them and Allah will punish at your hands.”\n\nA common misunderstanding takes their oath as proof of belonging. The Quran says oath without the five traits—belief, Last Day, prayer, zakat, fearing none but Allah—does not make them part of you, just as whistling at the Sacred House did not make them its guardians. The next verse, if only they could find a refuge they would rush, shows the fear that drives the oath, inviting the mindful who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted to see through the oath and to not be impressed.",
  "57": "“If only they could find a refuge, or a cave, or any hiding-place, they would rush headlong towards it” completes the portrait of fear that makes them swear they are part of you but they are not. The previous verse said they swear by Allah they are from you yet they are not, they only say so out of fear. This verse shows what that fear would do if it had a place to go: “If only they could find a refuge, or a cave, or any hiding-place, they would rush headlong towards it” or “law yajidoona malja’an aw magharatin aw muddakhalan la-wallaw ilaihi wa hum yajmahoon.” “Malja’,” refuge, safe haven, “magharat,” caves in mountains, like the cave where the Prophet and companion were second of two and Allah sent serenity and unseen forces, but here sought not for Allah’s companionship but for concealment from the mindful. “Muddakhal,” an entrance, a hole to enter, to hide, like the Sabbath fish nets to hide violation, like the gold hoarded in vaults to hide. “Yajmahoon,” rushing headlong, hastening with eagerness, is the same haste that made the Sabbath breakers hasten to fish on the forbidden day and the gate changers hasten to change “Hittah.” The rush is the opposite of the firm abiding, the remembering at the mountain, and the preparation of power to deter.\n\nThis image mirrors the earlier “We will be forgiven” inheritors who would seize lowly gain whenever it came, always rushing to the next hiding of their deeds, and mirrors the disbelievers whose spending to hinder becomes regret then defeat—rushing to spend, then rushing to hide when defeat comes, then being driven into Hell.\n\nThe verse links to the broader fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers: a community where some rush to hidings while others firmly stand and remember often cannot be united, and the wind that should go, or “reeh,” is lost. The seekers of refuge would turn their backs except for manoeuvre, earning displeasure and Hell as home, while the mindful are told to stand firm and remember much so they may triumph, and to obey and not dispute so wind remains, and to persevere with Allah who is with those who persevere.\n\nA common error pities their desire for refuge as understandable caution. The Quran presents it as the natural hideout of those who claim “We are part of you” while grieving at blessing and rejoicing at disaster, whose donations are rejected for loss of faith, lazy prayer, and resentful giving, whose wealth and children are the very torment in this world, whose hearts are in doubt torn by doubts, who already sought discord before and whose every plot until truth came was frustrated by the best of planners, whose end, if they do not turn, is the Fire’s Fire forever for maintaining mosques while professing disbelief.",
  "58": "“There are some of them who are critical of your distribution of alms ˹O Prophet˺. If they are given some of it they are pleased, but if not they are enraged” turns from their rush to refuge to their complaint about the very help that the mindful share. After saying they swear they are part of you out of fear and would rush to any hiding-place, this verse says “There are some of them who are critical of your distribution of alms O Prophet. If they are given some of it they are pleased, but if not they are enraged” or “wa minhum man yalmizuka fi al-sadaqat fa-in u‘tooo minha radoo wa in lam yu‘taw minha itha hum yaskhatoon.” The “sadaqat,” alms-tax, the same zakat that is one of the three brotherhood conditions—repent, pray, pay—and one of the mosque maintenance five, and one of the alms-tax only for the eight categories obligation from Allah, All-Knowing, All-Wise, becomes the hypocrites’ litmus of loyalty, as earlier the lowly gain seized was their measure.\n\n“L yalmizuka,” to criticize, to find fault, to slander, is the same fault-finding that made them call the skilled magician’s great magic a trick and the Book ancient fables, and the same critical eye that will later hurt the Prophet by saying “He listens to anyone.” Their pleasure and rage, or “radoo... yaskhatoon,” hinges on portion, the same conditional faith that made the treaty violators keep faith only if it favoured them and break every time otherwise, and that made the Sabbath town’s fish appear only on the forbidden day to test. This conditional pleasure is the opposite of the true believers whose hearts tremble and who are content with what Allah and His Messenger give, as the next verse will say “If only they had been content... Allah is sufficient for us.”\n\nThis criticism of alms links to the hoarding of gold and silver and the branding of foreheads, sides, backs, where the treasure heated is the alms withheld, and to the spending to hinder that becomes regret.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats criticism of distribution as mere administrative preference. The Quran treats it as a sign of the worst deaf and dumb, the wilfully deaf and dumb who do not understand, the entirely heedless like cattle, whose donations even if given willingly or unwillingly will never be accepted because they lost faith, pray lazily, give resentfully. For the community that prepares power and horses to deter and whose spending will be paid in full without wrong, the lesson is to see alms not as patronage to be criticized but as the fifth for close relatives, orphans, the poor, travellers, the obligation for the eight whose hearts are attracted, freeing slaves, debtors, Allah’s cause, travellers, where the mindful’s reward is never discounted and the rancour of the critic is the sign of the rebellious whose example is the dog panting whether chased or left.",
  "59": "“If only they had been content with what Allah and His Messenger had given them and said, ‘Allah is sufficient for us! Allah will grant us out of His bounty, and so will His Messenger. To Allah ˹alone˺ we turn with hope’” offers the contentment that would have replaced criticism of alms distribution. The previous verse said some are critical of your distribution of alms, pleased if given, enraged if not. This verse says “If only they had been content with what Allah and His Messenger had given them” or “wa law annahum radoo ma atahumu Allahu wa rasooluhu” using “radoo,” to be content, the same contentment that makes the poor, needy, and traveller content when the fifth is shared and the alms for eight is distributed, the same contentment that made the oppressed successors of blessed lands thankful and that made the magicians content to be among those closest to Pharaoh turned to content to be with the Lord whose mercy is close. Contentment, or “rida,” is the fertile land producing abundantly, opposite to the infertile hardly producing that criticizes distribution.\n\nThe statement they should have said, “Allah is sufficient for us! Allah will grant us out of His bounty, and so will His Messenger. To Allah alone we turn with hope” or “hasbuna Allahu sa-yu’teen a Allahu min fadlihi wa rasooluhu inna ila Allahi raghiboon” is the counterpart to the cave’s “Allah is with us” and to “Allah is sufficient for you and the believers who follow you” and “If their intention is to deceive you, Allah is sufficient for you” and “Allah frustrates the evil plans.” “Hasbuna Allah,” sufficient is Allah, mirrors “Hasbiya Allah” that would be enough, as earlier wealth and children test and beloveds more beloved require waiting until His Will, and as Hunain’s pride in numbers needed the earth closing to learn sufficiency is not numbers. “Sa-yu’teen a min fadlihi,” He will grant out of His bounty, or “fadl,” is the same infinite bounty for the mindful granted furqan, the same bounty that would enrich after the Sacred Mosque is purified if He wills, the same bounty that is great reward with Allah forever.\n\nThe final “To Allah alone we turn with hope” or “inna ila Allahi raghiboon” is the raghbah, longing, hope, the same hope and fear with which to call humbly and secretly, the same hope of the Heights to enter Paradise.\n\nA common error thinks sufficiency is passive. The Quran ties it to contentment with what Allah and His Messenger give, which for alms is the eight-category obligation from the All-Knowing, All-Wise, and to hopeful turning that, unlike hoarding gold to be heated and branded or reallocating sacred months as increase in disbelief, trusts the bounty to come. This contentment is the alternative to the rage that makes donations not accepted because they come lazily and resentfully, and it keeps the community from becoming the heedless like cattle whose lesson the next alms distribution will detail.",
  "60": "“Alms-tax is only for the poor and the needy, for those employed to administer it, for those whose hearts are attracted ˹to the faith˺, for ˹freeing˺ slaves, for those in debt, for Allah’s cause, and for ˹needy˺ travellers. ˹This is˺ an obligation from Allah. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” gives the divinely fixed categories that make contentment with the Prophet’s distribution the only right response, answering the criticism that pleased or enraged the hypocrites. The verse says “Alms-tax is only for the poor and the needy, for those employed to administer it, for those whose hearts are attracted to the faith, for freeing slaves, for those in debt, for Allah’s cause, and for needy travellers. This is an obligation from Allah. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “innama al-sadaqatu lil-fuqara’i wa al-masakeeni wa al-aamileena alaiha wa al-mu’allafati quloobuhum wa fi al-riqabi wa al-gharimeena wa fi sabeeli Allahi wa ibni al-sabeeli fareedatan mina Allahi wa Allahu Aleemun Hakeem.” The eight groups are: the poor, or “fuqara,” destitute; the needy, or “masakeen,” with some but insufficient, like the oppressed sheltered; administrators, or “aamileena,” those who collect and distribute, ensuring the fifth and the preparation of power and horses are managed justly; those whose hearts are attracted, or “mu’allafati quloobuhum,” the same hearts Allah stands between person and heart and united when all riches could not, needing softening not deceiving; freeing slaves, or “riqab,” necks, like freeing the lowing calf’s worshippers from disgrace; debtors, or “gharimeen,” like those who spent to hinder until regret and now burdened; Allah’s cause, or “sabeel Allah,” the same cause for which striving with wealth and lives and marching whether easy or difficult is best; and wayfarer, or “ibn al-sabeel,” traveller cut off, like the protected seeker escorted to safety to hear the Word.\n\nThe exclusive “innama,” only for, removes the hypocrites’ claim to distribution by favour, as earlier “They ask you regarding spoils, say distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger” and as “Know that whatever spoils you take, one-fifth is for Allah and His Messenger, close relatives, orphans, poor, travellers if you believe.” The seal “This is an obligation from Allah. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” mirrors the creation of twelve months in His Record since He created heavens and earth, the Record that wrote the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful and good, the All-Knowing who knows best what is hidden in hearts and who lightened burden knowing weakness, and the All-Wise who sent the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats zakat as discretionary charity. The verse makes it fareedah, obligation, fixed shares, whose criticism, or “yalmizuka,” is the sign of the rebellious whose donations even willing or unwilling will never be accepted because they lost faith, pray lazily, give resentfully, and whose wealth and children impress but are torment. Contentment with what Allah and Messenger give, saying “Allah is sufficient for us, He will grant from His bounty,” is the fertile response that varies lessons thankfully, where alms become the honourable provision whose reward with Allah is forever, not the hoarded treasure heated to brand foreheads, sides, and backs.",
  "61": "“And there are others who hurt the Prophet by saying, ‘He listens to anyone.’ Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘He listens to what is best for you. He believes in Allah, has faith in the believers, and is a mercy for those who believe among you.’ Those who hurt Allah’s Messenger will suffer a painful punishment” exposes the hurt of the hypocrites after the alms criticism and hoarding warning. The phrase “who hurt the Prophet by saying, ‘He listens to anyone’” or “wa minhum allatheena yu’thoona al-nabiyya wa yaqooloona huwa uthunun” uses “uthun,” ear, one who listens to anyone, implying the Prophet is gullible, easily influenced, the same ear accusation that earlier made them criticize distribution of alms. The behaviour mirrors the earlier “We hear but do not listen” worst deaf and dumb and the heedless cattle, but here they accuse the Prophet of hearing too much, while they themselves are the deaf. Their hurt is not mere words but undermining trust in the ear that hears revelation and the community, the same trust that made Allah bring hearts together when riches could not.\n\nThe reply “He listens to what is best for you. He believes in Allah, has faith in the believers, and is a mercy for those who believe among you” or “qul uthunu khairin lakum yu’minu billahi wa yu’minu lil-mu’mineen wa rahmatun lillatheena amanoo minkum” reframes the ear as “ear of good for you.” The Prophet listens to hear confession, to give excuse before Allah so perhaps they may abstain, to grant protection so they may hear the Word then escort to safety, to accept the excuse “Had we been able we would have joined” even while Allah knows they lie, to motivate to fight, and to accept the eight-category alms. “Yu’minu billahi,” believes in Allah, is the foundation that Allah and His Messenger are more deserving of fear, and “yu’minu lil-mu’mineen,” has faith in believers, is the trust that made the thousand angels bushra and the valley’s dream few. The ear is mercy for those who believe, the same mercy close to good-doers and ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, the same mercy that made the mountain a cloud and the sea a road.\n\nThe warning “Those who hurt Allah’s Messenger will suffer a painful punishment” or “wa allatheena yu’thoona Rasool Allahi lahum athabun aleem” is the same Aleem that branded hoarders’ foreheads, that awaited Sabbath breakers as apes, and that awaits those who take parents as allies if they prefer disbelief. Painful here is appropriate because hurt was by ear, by mocking listening, so their hearing will be hurt by punishment from heaven as the gate changers were.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats the Prophet’s listening as weakness. The Quran says his listening is khair, good, for you, the opposite of Satan beautifying and then deserting, and the criterion is not gullibility but mercy that distinguishes, as earlier “May Allah pardon you, why did you give permission before the truthful were distinguished” shows. For successors who claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing lowly gain, hurting the ear that listens is the path of the rebellious whose home is shown, while being among those who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted is to benefit from the ear that is mercy.",
  "62": "“They swear by Allah to you ˹believers˺ in order to please you, while it is the pleasure of Allah and His Messenger they should seek, if they are ˹true˺ believers” follows the hurt-by-ear accusation, showing the hypocrites’ oath strategy after being told their listening accusation brings painful punishment. The phrase “They swear by Allah to you believers in order to please you” or “yahlifoona billahi lakum li-yurdookum” is the same swearing that earlier “We took precaution” and “Had we been able we would have joined” and “We are part of you” but they are not, out of fear, rushing to refuge, caves, hiding-places, and that later swearing about spending to please, now aimed to please, or “yurdookum,” the believers. Their pleasure-seeking is social, as earlier they were pleased if given alms, enraged if not, and as the Sabbath town’s warners were asked “Why warn those who will be destroyed” but warned for excuse and perhaps they may abstain.\n\nThe correction “while it is the pleasure of Allah and His Messenger they should seek, if they are true believers” or “wa Allahu wa rasooluhu ahaqqu an yurdoohu in kanu mu’mineen” shifts pleasure, or “rida,” to the Lord who gives good news of mercy, pleasure, and Gardens with everlasting bliss and great reward with Allah, the same ridwan that is the second of the three gifts after mercy. Seeking the believers’ pleasure as performed by swearing is the flattery with tongues while hearts deny that characterized treaty violators who respect neither kinship nor treaty and are mostly rebellious. True believers’ pleasure is sought as a byproduct of seeking Allah and His Messenger, not as a substitute for the alms criticism or the distribution’s eight categories obligation from the All-Knowing, All-Wise.\n\nThis links to the next verse: whoever opposes Allah and His Messenger will be in Hell forever, ultimate disgrace, showing that oath to please people while opposing the two pleasures is opposition.\n\nA common error measures success by pleasing the community with oaths. The Quran measures by pleasing Allah and Messenger, whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed, whose most beautiful names are to be called upon, whose sufficiency, whose heart-uniting, whose unseen forces and sakeenah are with the believers who are sufficient. For the heir who swears “We are part of you,” the lesson is to seek the pleasure that is mercy and pleasure and Garden, not the momentary rida from a distribution that, if resented, proves the donation never accepted because they lost faith, came lazily, gave resentfully, and whose wealth and children impress but are worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers.",
  "63": "“Do they not know that whoever opposes Allah and His Messenger will be in the Fire of Hell forever? That is the ultimate disgrace” is the verdict on swearing to please believers while the true pleasure of Allah and His Messenger should be sought. The previous verse warned that swearing to please you while the more deserving are Allah and Messenger, if they are true believers, is misplaced. This verse asks “Do they not know that whoever opposes Allah and His Messenger will be in the Fire of Hell forever?” or “a-lam ya‘lamoo annahu man yuhadidi Allaha wa rasoolahu fa-anna lahu nara Jahannama khalidan fiha.” The verb “yuhadid,” to oppose, to be on the border against, to split, is the same shaqq that made defying Allah and Messenger severe in punishment, striking necks and fingertips, beating faces and backs to taste burning, and whose wont is that of Pharaoh and those before them seized for sins, and the worst beings who persist never to believe and violate treaties every time.\n\nThe consequence “will be in the Fire of Hell forever” or “fa-anna lahu nara Jahannama khalidan fiha” is the abiding that earlier “They will be in Fire forever” for those who maintain mosques while professing disbelief, whose deeds are void, and the same “khalideena fiha abadan” for the Gardens is here reversed for Hell. Khalid, abiding forever, is the opposite of the lowly gain seized while claiming “We will be forgiven,” the fleeting, the near, the arad al-dunya.\n\nThe evaluation “That is the ultimate disgrace” or “thalika al-khizyu al-azeem” uses “khizyu,” disgrace, the same disgrace the Fire’s begging for water and the barrier’s Heights recognized, the same disgrace after the trail of spending to hinder becomes regret, the same khizyu that makes large numbers and arrogance of no use. The ultimate, or “azeem,” great, shows no disgrace compares, just as the great reward with Allah is no reward compares.\n\nThis links to the next fear that a surah will be revealed exposing what is in hearts, the hypocrites’ fear of exposure, showing opposition is not hidden from the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who knows what is in heavens and earth and who wrote twelve months in the Record. A common misunderstanding treats opposition as intellectual disagreement. The Quran treats it as choosing a side opposite to Allah and His Messenger, with the heart between which Allah stands, with the weighing just where the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers is weighed, and with the earth belonging to Allah to grant to whom He chooses. For those who hurt the Prophet by calling him an ear and then swear to please, the only rida that matters is turning to Allah with hope, “Allah is sufficient for us, He will grant from His bounty, and His Messenger, to Allah we turn with hope,” rather than the oath that opposes.",
  "64": "“The hypocrites fear that a sûrah should be revealed about them, exposing what is in their hearts. Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Keep mocking! Allah will definitely bring to light what you fear’” reveals the hypocrites’ anxiety after being warned that opposing Allah and His Messenger is ultimate disgrace in Hell forever. The phrase “The hypocrites fear that a sûrah should be revealed about them, exposing what is in their hearts” or “yahtharu al-munafiqoona an tunazzala alaihim sooratun tunabbi’uhum bima fi quloobihim” shows their hearts hide what was earlier described as doubt, torn by doubts, sickness that says believers are deluded, resentful donation, half-hearted prayer, loss of faith, and the “We will be forgiven” while seizing again. A surah, or “soorah,” revelation explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, is feared because it would “tunabbi’uhum,” inform about them, the same “nunabbi’” used for “We will give them a full account with sure knowledge, for We were never absent” and “to Him you will be gathered” and “We will inform them of what they used to do.” Hearts that were exposed when Sardis’ mountain was raised and when the calf’s lowing could not speak fear the Book that does speak.\n\nThe command “Say, O Prophet, ‘Keep mocking! Allah will definitely bring to light what you fear’” or “qul istahzi’oo inna Allaha mukhrijun ma tahtharoon” uses “istahzi’oo,” to mock, the same ridicule that made them joke “We were only talking idly” and that made them hurt the Prophet by calling him an ear, and that made them whistle and clap at the Sacred House. Keeping mocking is not permission but exposure, as earlier “Then wait, I too am waiting” and “Say nothing will befall us except what Allah has written for us” show the truth prevailing. “Mukhrijun,” to bring out, to extract, is the same “We bring forth the living from the dead” and the heavy clouds driven to lifeless land producing fruit, now applied to the hidden mockery inside chests tight as if climbing into the sky. Allah, who knows best what is hidden in hearts, who encompasses everything in knowledge, who is All-Aware of what you do, will extract it, not by ear but by revelation.\n\nA common error thinks hidden mockery stays hidden while public oaths please. The verse says the hypocrites themselves fear the surah, proving they know the ear that hears is not the Prophet’s ear alone but the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who revealed the Book. The next verses will confirm, when questioned they will say “We were only talking idly and joking,” the same mocking that the bringing to light will expose as ridiculing Allah, His revelations, and His Messenger, the same three that the unlettered Prophet believes in and that must be followed to be rightly guided.",
  "65": "“If you question them, they will certainly say, ‘We were only talking idly and joking around.’ Say, ‘Was it Allah, His revelations, and His Messenger that you ridiculed?’” quotes the hypocrites’ excuse when confronted after fearing a surah will expose what is in hearts. The previous verse said keep mocking, Allah will bring to light what you fear; this verse shows the mocking’s content and the excuse. The phrase “If you question them, they will certainly say, ‘We were only talking idly and joking around’” or “wa la-in sa’altahum la-yaqoolunna innama kunna nakhoodu wa nal‘ab” uses “nakhoodu,” to wade, to plunge into speech, and “nal‘ab,” to play, the same la‘ib that described worldly life as play and amusement, the taking of faith as amusement and play for which the Fire’s water is forbidden and today We forget them as they forgot the meeting. The excuse is that talk was idle, joking, not serious, the same lightness that made the Sabbath fish appear abundant on the forbidden day and that made the gate word changed from “Hittah” to jest, and that made the Sacred House’s prayer whistling.\n\nThe reply “Say, ‘Was it Allah, His revelations, and His Messenger that you ridiculed?’” or “a-billahi wa ayatihi wa rasoolihi kuntum tastahzi’oon” triples the object, showing mocking the deen is not mocking a tribe but the three that define the covenant: Allah, whose creation and command are His, blessed Lord of all worlds; His revelations, or “ayat,” the signs varied for the thankful, the book explained with knowledge, the she-camel not to be harmed, the five for close relatives, the alms for eight, the twelve months; and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet whose description is in Torah and Gospel, who is sufficient for you, whose ear is khair for you, whose hurt brings painful punishment. Ridicule, or “istihza’,” is the same mocking that made the chiefs say “We see you as fool, liar” and that made the magicians’ great magic seem comparable, now turned inward.\n\nThis triple links directly to the next excuse-barring “Make no excuses, you have certainly disbelieved after your faith, if We pardon one group We will punish another for being wicked,” showing joking while hearing the call to what gives life and while Allah stands between person and heart is not light talk but disbelief after faith.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats religious mocking as harmless humour. The Quran treats wading and playing with Allah, ayat, and Messenger as the line where hearts not understanding become like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless, whose donations even willing or unwilling will never be accepted because they come lazily and resentfully, whose wealth and children are worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers. For the heedless, the antidote is the seriousness of holding firmly to the Scripture under the mountain, taking the best of the Tablets, and remembering morning and evening without heedlessness.",
  "66": "“Make no excuses! You have lost faith after your belief. If We pardon a group of you, We will punish others for their wickedness” follows the exposure of idle joking about Allah, His revelations, and His Messenger. The previous verse recorded their excuse “We were only talking idly and joking” and the question “Was it Allah, His revelations, and His Messenger that you ridiculed?” This verse says “Make no excuses! You have lost faith after your belief” or “la ta‘tathiroo qad kafartum ba‘da imanikum.” The verb “kafar” after “imani” shows a sequence: there was a claim of faith, then ridicule made it void, as earlier deeds become void in this world and the Hereafter for those who enjoy their share and engage in idle talk as those before them and as earlier polytheists’ maintenance of mosques while professing disbelief was void. Excuse, or “i‘tithar,” is rejected because joking while hearing the call to what gives life and while Allah stands between person and heart is not light talk but disbelief.\n\nThe second sentence “If We pardon a group of you, We will punish others for their wickedness” or “in na‘fu an ta’ifatin minkum nu‘aththib ta’ifatan bi-annahum kanoo mujrimeen” distinguishes hearts that may still be turned. A group, or “ta’ifah,” may be pardoned if they sincerely turn, as earlier “Those who worshipped the calf will be afflicted with wrath and disgrace, this is how We reward inventors, but those who commit evil then repent and believe, your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” and as “Then afterwards He will turn to whoever He wills, Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.” The others remain mujrimeen, those who cut off, whose end the watcher is told to see, whose bed is Hell and whose treasure brands.\n\nThis verse links the primordial testimony and the mountain covenant’s “We will not say about Allah except truth” to the present: saying “We were only joking” violates that truth, and the prior verse’s “Do they not know Allah knows their evil thoughts and secret talks, Knower of all unseen” confirms the joke was known.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats religious mockery as harmless banter. The Quran treats it as the line where faith is lost after belief, the same line where wealth and children impress but are worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers. For those who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, the lesson is to guard tongue as well as wealth, to be gracious, enjoin right, turn from the ignorant, seek refuge when Satan tempts, remember and see clearly, lest the excuse be struck down as void before the weighing just.",
  "67": "“The hypocrites, both men and women, are all alike: they encourage what is evil, forbid what is good, and withhold ˹what is in˺ their hands. They neglected Allah, so He neglected them. Surely the hypocrites are the rebellious” defines the hypocrites as a unified type after the warning not to make excuses and that some will be pardoned and others punished for wickedness. The phrase “The hypocrites, both men and women, are all alike: they encourage what is evil, forbid what is good, and withhold what is in their hands” or “al-munafiqoona wa al-munafiqatu ba‘duhum min ba‘d ya’muroona bil-munkari wa yanhawna ani al-ma‘roofi wa yaqbidoona aidiyahum” uses the same “ya’muroona bil-munkar” to enjoin munkar, evil, and forbid ma‘roof, good, the inversion of the true believers who encourage good and forbid evil, establish prayer, pay zakat, obey Allah and Messenger, and are shown mercy. Withholding hands, or “yaqbidoona aidiyahum,” mirrors the hoarders who withhold gold and silver and do not spend in Allah’s cause, the same hands that will be branded foreheads, sides, backs where treasure is heated, and the same hands that earlier were beaten at soul-taking.\n\nThe reciprocal “They neglected Allah, so He neglected them” or “nasoo Allaha fa-nasi yahum” uses “nasiya,” to forget, to neglect, the same forgetting as those who take faith as play and were deluded and are today forgotten as they forgot the meeting, and as those who forgot what they were reminded after the fish test and were rescued only if they warned. Neglect here is willful, like clinging to land when asked to march forth and preferring the little enjoyment of this world.\n\nThe seal “Surely the hypocrites are the rebellious” or “inna al-munafiqeena humu al-fasiqoon” names them with the same fasiq who are most, who inherit Scripture and claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing, who reallocate sacred months as increase in disbelief, who break treaties every time.\n\nThis portrait links to the previous idle joking, swearing to please believers while Allah and Messenger are more deserving, opposing Allah and Messenger to abide in Hell forever, fearing a surah will expose hearts, and to the next promise of everlasting Hell, condemnation, and never-ending punishment for hypocrites and disbelievers, showing the rebellious type’s home. A common error sees hypocrisy as private doubt. The verse shows it as a social gender-inclusive alliance that actively inverts enjoining, forbidding, and giving, the opposite of the ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice, inviting the successor to choose the believers’ guardianship where Allah’s mercy is Almighty, All-Wise, not the withholders’ neglect.",
  "68": "“Allah has promised the hypocrites, both men and women, and the disbelievers an everlasting stay in the Fire of Hell—it is sufficient for them. Allah has condemned them, and they will suffer a never-ending punishment” states the recompense for the alike hypocrites who encourage evil, forbid good, withhold hands, and neglected Allah so He neglected them. The previous verse diagnosed them as the rebellious; this verse promises. The phrase “Allah has promised the hypocrites, both men and women, and the disbelievers an everlasting stay in the Fire of Hell—it is sufficient for them” or “wa‘ada Allahu al-munafiqeena wa al-munafiqati wa al-kuffara nara Jahannama khalideena fiha hiya hasbuhum” uses “wa‘ada,” promise, the same promise that for the believers is Gardens under which rivers flow forever and splendid homes and pleasure of Allah, ultimate triumph, and forgiveness and honourable provision, now mirrored as the opposite promise. “Khalideena fiha,” abiding forever, mirrors the everlasting Gardens abiding forever and the great reward with Allah, but here it is Hell. “Hiya hasbuhum,” it is sufficient for them, contrasts with “Allah is sufficient for you and followers” and “Allah is sufficient for us, He will grant from His bounty,” showing sufficiency for the rebellious is sufficient torment, as earlier the rich thought large numbers sufficient but proved of no advantage and earth closed.\n\nThe next “Allah has condemned them, and they will suffer a never-ending punishment” or “wa la‘anahumu Allahu wa lahum athabun muqeem” uses “la‘ana,” to curse, to condemn, as the caller announced “May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers” who hindered and made the Way seem crooked, and “mugem,” lasting, never-ending, like the everlasting bliss’s “muqeem” but for torment, where treasure heated brands and where wealth and children become worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats the promise of Hell as rhetoric. The verse makes it wa‘d, a promise as firm as the Word perfected, as the twelve-month Record, as the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured. For the community told not to be impressed by hypocrites’ wealth and children whose worldly torment precedes departure while disbelievers, the promise deters taking parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief, and the beloveds more dear than Allah, preventing the wait until His Will while rebellious not guided, and encouraging the steadfast among whom twenty overcome two hundred by His Will with Allah.",
  "69": "“˹You hypocrites are˺ like those ˹disbelievers˺ before you. They were far superior to you in might and more abundant in wealth and children. They enjoyed their share in this life. You have enjoyed your share, just as they did. And you have engaged in idle talk, just as they did. Their deeds have become void in this world and the Hereafter. And it is they who are the ˹true˺ losers” likens the present hypocrites to the superior ancients before them, extending the void and loss diagnosis. The phrase “They were far superior to you in might and more abundant in wealth and children” or “kanoo ashadda minkum quwwatan wa akthara amwalan wa awlada” invokes ’Ad increased in stature, Thamud’s palaces, Pharaoh’s constructions, Midian’s flourishing after adversity, the Jews and Christians who reallocated months and consumed wealth wrongfully, all whose might, wealth, and children were thought to guarantee, but whose deeds became void. “They enjoyed their share in this life” or “fa-istamta‘oo bi-khalaqihim” enjoyed their allotted share, the same enjoyments the Sabbath fish-seekers and the lowly-gain inheritors and the Badr caravan wishers sought as “This is what we deserve” and “Make for us a god like theirs.” The hypocrites’ enjoyment, “You have enjoyed your share, just as they did” or “fa-istamta‘tum bi-khalaqikum kama istamta‘a allatheena min qablikum,” shows replication, not originality, the same enjoyment that closed the earth at Hunain when pride in numbers proved of no advantage.\n\nThe second parallel “And you have engaged in idle talk, just as they did” or “wa khudt um kama khadoo” uses “khawd,” wading, plunging into idle talk, the same “We were only talking idly and joking” and “They engage in idle talk just as they did” that made deeds void like the dish where the mountain was raised and the covenant broken.\n\nThe verdict “Their deeds have become void in this world and the Hereafter. And it is they who are the true losers” or “ula’ika habitat a‘maluhum fid-dunya wa al-akhirah wa ula’ika humu al-khasiroon” repeats the void, or “habitat,” for maintainers of mosques while professing disbelief, for deniers whose meeting in Hereafter vain will be rewarded only by what they did, and the losers, or “khasiroon,” who feel secure from planning, whose scales are light, who were wiped as if never there.\n\nA common error thinks present hypocrites are less guilty than mighty ancients. The verse says despite being inferior in might and abundance, replication of enjoyment and wading makes void and loss equal, showing the test of wealth and children and beloveds is not about amount but about use. For those given good news of Gardens with everlasting bliss for believing, emigrating, striving, the warning is to not exchange that for the share that voids, but to be among those who encourage good, forbid evil, establish prayer, pay zakat, obey, and are shown mercy by Almighty, All-Wise.",
  "70": "“Have they not received the stories of those ˹destroyed˺ before them: the people of Noah, ’Âd, and Thamûd, the people of Abraham, the residents of Midian, and the overturned cities ˹of Lot˺? Their messengers came to them with clear proofs. Allah would have never wronged them, but it was they who wronged themselves” asks the hypocrites who enjoyed their share and waded as before them, whose deeds are void and who are true losers, whether they have heard the stories. The question “Have they not received the stories of those destroyed before them: the people of Noah, ’Ad, and Thamûd, the people of Abraham, the residents of Midian, and the overturned cities of Lot?” or “a-lam ya’tihim naba’u allatheena min qablihim” lists the very stories narrated as “We have narrated to you some of the stories of those societies, their messengers came with clear proofs but they would not believe in what they had already denied, this is how Allah seals hearts” and retold as winds, fertile barren, Noah’s flood for the blind, ’Ad’s uprooting, Thamud’s rajfah, Abraham’s father’s idols, Midian’s shaking, Lot’s brimstone rain. Each was the term that cannot be delayed, the night or day surprise, the spacious then tight chest.\n\nThe testimony “Their messengers came to them with clear proofs” or “ja’athum rusuluhum bil-bayyinat” reiterates the bayyinah of the staff-snake and shining hand, the she-camel not to be harmed, the just measure, the Tablets’ fundamentals, all varied for the thankful. The refutation “Allah would have never wronged them, but it was they who wronged themselves” or “fa-ma kana Allahu liyazlimahum wa lakin kanoo anfusahum yazlimoon” repeats “This is for what your hands have done, Allah is never unjust to His creation” and “They did not wrong Us but wronged themselves” after the twelve springs and manna, and “They only wronged their own souls” for the evil example of the dog. Wronging selves is the same self-dooming light scales, the same clutching to this life following desires, the same withholding hands.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats ancient stories as distant fables, the same “We have heard, we could produce similar, it is ancient fables” that the hoarders and reallocators rely on. The verse says the stories were received, messengers with clear proofs came, and the destruction was self-wronging, not divine wronging, inviting the hypocrites who neglect Allah so He neglects them, who wish to extinguish light with mouths but Allah perfects it, who hoard gold to be heated and branded, who reallocate months as increase in disbelief, to see the pattern and become among the believers, men and women guardians of one another who encourage good, forbid evil, establish prayer, pay zakat, obey, and are shown mercy, before the stories become their own.",
  "71": "“The believers, both men and women, are guardians of one another. They encourage good and forbid evil, establish prayer and pay alms-tax, and obey Allah and His Messenger. It is they who will be shown Allah’s mercy. Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” defines the believers as the exact opposite of the hypocrites, men and women alike, who encourage evil, forbid good, and withhold hands. The previous verses diagnosed hypocrites as alike, rebellious, promised Hell sufficient and condemned, likened to mighty ancients whose share was enjoyed and whose wading voided deeds as true losers, and asked whether stories of Noah, ’Ad, Thamud, Abraham, Midian, Lot had not come. This verse provides the counter-group. The phrase “The believers, both men and women, are guardians of one another” or “wa al-mu’minoon wa al-mu’minatu ba‘duhum awliya’u ba‘d” repeats the guardianship that prevents great oppression if believers act likewise, the same wilayah that made the hearts united when all riches could not, the same shelter and help that made Muhajirun and Ansar true guardians. Their actions “They encourage good and forbid evil, establish prayer and pay alms-tax, and obey Allah and His Messenger” or “ya’muroona bil-ma‘roofi wa yanhawna ani al-munkari wa yuqeemoona al-salata wa yu’toona al-zakata wa yutee‘oona Allaha wa rasoolahu” is the classic quartet that is the firm abiding by Scripture where reward is never discounted, the holding firmly to the Scripture under the mountain, the taking the best of the Tablets, the listening attentively and being silent so mercy is shown, and the remembering inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness. It is the opposite of hoarding gold, reallocating months, clinging to land, and mocking revelations.\n\nThe promise “It is they who will be shown Allah’s mercy” or “ula’ika sa-yarhamuhumu Allah” is the mercy close to good-doers, encompassing everything and ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, the same mercy that sent sakeenah and unseen forces at Hunain and that enriched after fearing poverty if the Sacred Mosque were purified. The names “Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” or “inna Allaha Azeezun Hakeem” close, as with victory only from Him, heart-uniting, and lightening knowing weakness, showing mercy is not weakness but might with wisdom, the same planning that frustrates evil plans.\n\nA common error sees these actions as optional. The verse makes them the definition of the guardian community that the hypocrites’ neglect mirrors, inviting the successor who is well-versed yet seized lowly gain to shift from the void, loser, self-wronging group to the mercy-shown group before the term that cannot be delayed and before the Hour that is too tremendous and will take by surprise.",
  "72": "“Allah has promised the believers, both men and women, Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever, and splendid homes in the Gardens of Eternity, and—above all—the pleasure of Allah. That is ˹truly˺ the ultimate triumph” details the mercy just promised to the guardians who encourage good, forbid evil, establish prayer, pay zakat, and obey. The previous verse said it is they who will be shown Allah’s mercy, Almighty, All-Wise; this verse says what that mercy contains. The phrase “Allah has promised the believers, both men and women, Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever, and splendid homes in the Gardens of Eternity” or “wa‘ada Allahu al-mu’mineena wa al-mu’minati jannatin tajri min tahtiha al-anharu khalideena fiha wa masakina tayyibatan fi jannati Adn” echoes the good news of mercy, pleasure, and Gardens with everlasting bliss where the great reward with Allah is forever, and the rivers flowing beneath after bitterness removed, and the honoured provision after elevated ranks. “Tajri min tahtiha al-anhar” rivers beneath, “khalideena fiha” abiding forever, and “masakina tayyibatan” splendid homes, good, pure dwellings, the same tayyib that Allah brought forth for servants and that is lawful and good to eat without waste. The Gardens of Eternity, or “jannati Adn,” are the same as the splendid homes promised, the opposite of the Fire’s everlasting stay sufficient for hypocrites and disbelievers and branded foreheads.\n\nThe climax “and—above all—the pleasure of Allah. That is truly the ultimate triumph” or “wa ridwanun mina Allahi akbar thalika huwa al-fawzu al-azeem” places ridwan, pleasure, contentment of Allah, above the rivers and homes, the same rida sought when hypocrites swore to please believers while Allah and Messenger are more deserving, and the same pleasure the Lord gives as good news earlier. The ultimate triumph, or “fawz azeem,” is the same triumph as falah for the mindful and as heavy scales who are successful and as those who believed, emigrated, strove, sheltered, helped are true believers with forgiveness and honourable provision, and as the Gardens with everlasting bliss.\n\nThis promise links Badr’s “elevated ranks” and Hunain’s “unseen forces” and Tabuk’s “march whether easy or difficult, that is best if only you knew” to the same end: the well-watered Gardens where the earth that closed at Hunain now opens wide forever. A common misunderstanding reduces triumph to spoils or to being pleased by people. The verse says triumph is pleasure of Allah outweighing all homes, inviting the alms critics who are pleased if given and enraged if not, the hoarders whose treasure will be heated, and the reallocators who adjust sacred months for gain to seek that pleasure by encouraging good, forbidding evil, establishing, paying, and obeying before the Hour too tremendous arrives by surprise.",
  "73": "“O Prophet! Struggle against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be firm with them. Hell will be their home. What an evil destination!” commands the struggle that fits the guardian community just promised the ultimate triumph. The previous verses contrasted hypocrites who encourage evil, forbid good, withhold, neglect so neglected, rebellious, promised Hell sufficient, like those before them superior in might yet void and losers, with believers guardians of one another, encouraging good, forbidding evil, establishing, paying, obeying, shown mercy, promised Gardens forever, splendid homes, and greatest is Allah’s pleasure. This verse says “O Prophet! Struggle against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be firm with them” or “ya ayyuha al-nabiyyu jahidi al-kuffara wa al-munafiqeena wa ughluz alaihim.” The verb “jahidi,” to strive, to struggle, covers the striving with wealth and lives in Allah’s cause that made rank greater with Allah and that is the test of wealth and children, the same striving that motivated to fight, twenty steadfast overcoming two hundred by His Will. Struggle against the disbelievers is the open “kill the violators wherever you find them, capture, besiege, lie in wait” after sacred months and discharge, and against hypocrites is the struggle of exposing what is in hearts that the surah feared would be revealed, the firmness, or “ughluz,” harshness, not cruelty but resoluteness, as Moses was firm with the calf worshippers and as the command to be gracious, enjoin right, and turn from the ignorant requires firmness with those who hurt the Prophet by calling him an ear.\n\nThe destination “Hell will be their home. What an evil destination!” or “wa ma’wahum Jahannamu wa bi’sa al-maseer” is the same evil destination, or “maseer,” earlier for those who turn backs on battle will earn displeasure and home is Hell, and for hoarders whose treasure will brand. The struggle is therefore not for spoils, for spoils are lawful and good after the prior decree, but to separate the evil from the good so evil piled can be cast, the true losers, and to prevent the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers and the great oppression if believers do not guard each other.\n\nA common error imagines struggle against hypocrites is only with the sword. The Quran’s struggle includes the tongue that reveals, the heart that is firm, the law that openly terminates on equal footing, and the spending that is paid in full. The next verse’s oath that they never said blasphemy while they did and plotted what they could not carry out shows the hypocrites’ struggle was also by tongue, requiring a firm prophetic response until repentance, which if it occurs, as earlier “If they repent it will be better for them, if they turn away painful torment,” remains open, but whose Home if persisted is Hell, the evil destination that no wealth and children can impress.",
  "74": "“They swear by Allah that they never said anything ˹blasphemous˺, while they did in fact utter a blasphemy, lost faith after accepting Islam, and plotted what they could not carry out. It is only through resentment that they pay Allah and His Messenger back for enriching them out of His bounty! If they repent, it will be better for them. But if they turn away, Allah will torment them with a painful punishment in this world and the Hereafter, and they will have no one on earth to protect or help them” exposes the perjury behind the struggle against hypocrites, after commanding firmness and warning Hell as evil destination. The phrase “They swear by Allah that they never said anything blasphemous, while they did in fact utter a blasphemy, lost faith after accepting Islam, and plotted what they could not carry out” or “yahlifoona billahi ma qaloo wa laqad qaloo kalimata al-kufri wa kafaroo ba‘da islamihim wa hammoo bima lam yanaloo” lists three layers: oath denial, the “kalimat al-kufr,” word of disbelief, like the idle joking “We were only talking idly” about Allah, ayat, Messenger after fearing a surah exposure, and like “Ezra is son of Allah” parroting, then apostasy, or “kafaroo ba‘da islamihim,” disbelief after Islam, the same loss of faith after belief for which no excuses, where if a group is pardoned another is punished for wickedness, and the plot, or “hammoo,” like the capture-kill-exile plot against the Prophet, the discord spread, and every plot until truth came and Allah’s Will prevailed to their dismay.\n\nThe motive “It is only through resentment that they pay Allah and His Messenger back for enriching them out of His bounty!” or “wa ma naqamoo illa an aghnahumu Allahu wa rasooluhu min fadlihi” shows they resent enrichment itself, as earlier “If you fear poverty Allah will enrich you out of His bounty if He wills, All-Knowing, All-Wise” after purifying the Sacred House, and the bounty that gave victory on many battlefields even at Hunain and sent sakeenah and unseen forces. Enrichment from bounty, or “fadlihi,” is the same infinite bounty for the mindful granted furqan, here turned into grievance.\n\nThe offer “If they repent, it will be better for them. But if they turn away, Allah will torment them with a painful punishment in this world and the Hereafter, and they will have no one on earth to protect or help them” or “fa-in yatooboo yaku khairan lahum wa in yatawallaw yu‘aththibhum Allahu athaban aleeman fid-dunya wa al-akhirah wa ma lahum fil-ardi min waliyyin wa la naseer” repeats the discharge’s “If you repent better, if you turn away no escape, give good news of painful punishment” and the alms-conditioned brotherhood, now individualized, showing the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous is the only Wali and Naseer, the same excellent protector and helper promised if they do not comply, fight until no persecution. No wali, no naseer on earth will shield the painful torment that is both worldly disgrace like apes and branded treasure and otherworldly Fire forever.\n\nA common misunderstanding excuses the word of kufr as slip. The verse lists it with apostasy and plot, showing the mouth reveals the neglected heart that neglected Allah so He neglected them, the rebellious whose evil example is the dog panting.",
  "75": "“And there are some who had made a vow to Allah: ‘If He gives us from His bounty, we will surely spend in charity and be of the righteous’” introduces the man of the broken vow, and the commentators identify him and explain the occasion. The verse records the pledge: “And there are some who had made a vow to Allah: ‘If He gives us from His bounty, we will surely spend in charity and be of the righteous,’” or “wa minhum man ‘ahada Allaha la-in atana min fadlihi la-nassaddaqanna wa la-nakoonanna mina as-saliheen,” and the commentators identify the man as Tha‘labah ibn Hatib, a man of the Ansar, who came to the Prophet and asked him to pray that God would grant him wealth, and who vowed that if God gave him the wealth, he would give every due from it and be among the righteous, and the verse records the vow as the example of the conditional pledge that the prosperity would betray. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account: Tha‘labah pressed the Prophet repeatedly to pray for his wealth, and the Prophet said to him, “A little that you are grateful for is better than much that you cannot bear,” and when Tha‘labah insisted, the Prophet prayed, and the wealth came — the sheep that multiplied until they filled the valleys — and the man withdrew from the congregation and the prayer and the due, and the vow that he had made was broken by the very prosperity that it had purchased. The word “‘ahada,” the covenant, the vow, is the key, for the verse records the solemn pledge that the man made to his Lord, and the solemnity of the pledge is the measure of the breach that the next verses record.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the two promises of the vow. The first is the charity: “we will surely spend in charity,” or “la-nassaddaqanna,” the giving of the due, the “sadaqah” that the wealth would require, and the second is the righteousness: “and be of the righteous,” or “la-nakoonanna mina as-saliheen,” the life of the righteous, the “saliheen,” the ones who set things aright, and the two promises are the complete pledge of the one who asks for the wealth, for the wealth that is sought for the sake of the Lord is the wealth that is spent for the Lord and lived for the Lord, and the man who made the vow made the two promises the condition of his request. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the conditional vow: the man who says “if You give me, I will give” has made his obedience conditional upon the gift, and the condition is the seed of the breach, for the heart that bargains with its Lord has not yet submitted, and the gift that comes to the bargaining heart is the gift that the heart will withhold. The verse is the introduction to the story of the broken vow, and the warning against the pledge that the prosperity will betray.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prayers. The believer who asks the Lord for the wealth is taught by the verse to ask with the intention of the gratitude, not with the vow of the bargain, and the man of the story is the warning of the one who asked and received and then forgot, for the wealth that is asked for the self is the wealth that the self will hoard, and the wealth that is asked for the Lord is the wealth that the Lord will bless. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the first of the four verses of the broken vow, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the wealth: the vow is easy in the poverty, and the breach is the test of the plenty, and the man who vows in the need is measured by the plenty that follows. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the vow: the pledge made, the two promises named, and the wealth awaited — the introduction to the story of the man whose prosperity betrayed his promise.",
  "76": "“But when He gave them out of His bounty, they withheld it and turned away indifferently” records the breach of the vow, and the commentators note the two acts that the verse names. The verse states the response to the gift: “But when He gave them out of His bounty, they withheld it and turned away indifferently,” or “fa-lamma atahum min fadlihi bakhiloo bihi wa tawallaw wa hum mu‘ridoon,” and the commentators explain the two verbs: the “bakhiloo,” the withholding, the miserliness, the refusal to give the due that the vow had promised, and the “tawallaw,” the turning away, the “i‘rad,” the indifference, the turning of the back upon the Lord and the congregation, and the two together are the complete breach, for the man who had vowed the charity withheld it, and the man who had vowed the righteousness turned away from it, and the gift that should have produced the gratitude produced the stinginess and the flight. Ibn Kathir relates the detail: when the wealth of Tha‘labah multiplied, the sheep spread through the valleys, and the man, occupied with his flocks, first missed the congregational prayers and then the Friday prayer, and when the collector of the zakah came to him with the command of the Quran, he said, “This is but a jizyah, a tribute,” and refused, and the refusal and the withdrawal are the “bakhil” and the “tawalli” that the verse names. The word “fadlihi,” His bounty, is the key, for the verse calls the wealth the “fadl,” the grace, the gift, and the withholding of the gift’s due is the ingratitude that the verse records.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the sequence of the two acts. The withholding came first, the refusal of the due, and the turning away followed, the withdrawal of the body from the worship, and the two are the two halves of the single fall, for the heart that withholds the wealth soon withholds the self, and the man who refuses the due of the Lord soon refuses the presence of the Lord, and the sequence is the warning of the verse, the teaching that the miserliness is the first step of the apostasy. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s diagnosis of the disease of the wealth: the “bakhil” and the “i‘rad” are the two symptoms of the heart that has been captured by the gift, and the one who loves the wealth withholds it and turns away, and the two symptoms together are the corruption that the next verse pronounces upon. The verse is the record of the breach, and the warning against the capture of the heart by the gift.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the test of the plenty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wealth. The believer is taught that the wealth is the test, and the test is not passed by the receiving but by the spending, and the man who passes the test of the poverty may fail the test of the plenty, for the plenty is the harder test, and the one who vows in the need and withholds in the plenty has failed the very test that his vow invited. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the second of the four verses of the broken vow, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the wealth: the bounty is the test, the withholding is the failure, and the turning away is the fruit of the failure, and the one who is given the bounty is measured by the due that he gives from it. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the breach: the bounty given, the due withheld, and the back turned — the record of the man whose wealth betrayed his vow, and the warning against the capture of the heart.",
  "77": "“So He caused hypocrisy to plague their hearts until the Day they will meet Him, for breaking their promise to Allah and for their lies” records the punishment of the breach, and the commentators note the two causes and the one result. The verse states the result: “So He caused hypocrisy to plague their hearts until the Day they will meet Him,” or “fa-a‘qabahum nifaqan fee quloobihim ila yawmi yalqawnahu,” the “nifaq,” the hypocrisy, the consequence that God attached to their hearts, the punishment that the breach produced, and the commentators explain the word “a‘qaba,” the causing to follow, the attaching of the consequence, for the hypocrisy was not an arbitrary curse but the natural fruit of the breach, the disease that the withholding and the turning had planted, and the verse declares that the hypocrisy would plague their hearts “until the Day they will meet Him,” the persistence of the disease until the meeting with the Lord, the “liqa’,” the day of the reckoning. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the heart of the man who broke the vow was given over to the hypocrisy that the breach had invited, and the hypocrisy became the permanent state of the heart, the companion of the man until the day he meets his Lord, and the permanence of the disease is the measure of the gravity of the breach.\n\nThe verse then states the two causes: “for breaking their promise to Allah and for their lies,” or “bima akhlafoo Allaha ma wa‘adoohu wa bima kanoo yakthiboon,” the “ikhlas,” the breaking of the promise, the failure of the vow that they had made, and the “kathib,” the lying, the falsehood of the pledge that they had never intended to keep, and the commentators note the two causes, for the breach of the promise and the lie of the pledge are the two roots of the hypocrisy, the two sins that the verse names as the ground of the punishment. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the source of the hypocrisy: the hypocrisy is the fruit of the broken promise, and the broken promise is the fruit of the false pledge, and the one who makes the vow and breaks it has planted the hypocrisy in his own heart, for the disease that the verse names is the harvest of the seed that the falsehood sowed. The verse is the statement of the punishment, and the teaching that the hypocrisy is the consequence of the breach, not the accident of the circumstance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the permanence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own promises. The believer is taught to weigh the vow before the making, for the vow that is broken is the vow that plants the hypocrisy, and the hypocrisy that is planted is the disease that persists until the meeting with the Lord, and the one who would be free of the disease must be free of the false pledge, the promise that is made with the tongue and not with the heart. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the third of the four verses of the broken vow, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the promise: the vow to the Lord is a trust, and the breach of the trust is the planting of the hypocrisy, and the hypocrisy is the companion of the breaker until the day of the meeting. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the punishment: the hypocrisy caused, the two causes named, and the permanence declared — the record of the heart that the broken vow had corrupted, and the warning against the false pledge.",
  "78": "“Do they not know that Allah ˹fully˺ knows their ˹evil˺ thoughts and secret talks, and that Allah is the Knower of all unseen?” closes the story of the broken vow with the question that exposes the self-deception, and the commentators note the two things that the verse names. The verse asks the question: “Do they not know that Allah fully knows their evil thoughts and secret talks,” or “a-lam ya‘lamoo anna Allaha ya‘lamu sirrahum wa najwahum,” the “sirr,” the secret, the hidden thought, and the “najwa,” the secret talk, the whispered counsel, and the commentators explain the application: the man of the broken vow had concealed his withholding and his refusal, and he had whispered his excuses to the collector and his complaints to his companions, and the verse asks whether he did not know that the Lord knows the secret and the whisper, the hidden thought and the private talk, for the one who knows that the Lord sees the secret is the one who cannot deceive himself with the concealment. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the verse exposes the self-deception of the breaker, for the man who breaks the vow imagines that the breach is hidden, and the verse asks the question that dissolves the illusion, the question that the man himself could not answer except with the confession of the knowledge that he had suppressed.\n\nThe verse then states the attribute that grounds the question: “and that Allah is the Knower of all unseen?” or “wa anna Allaha ‘allamu al-ghuyoob,” the “‘Allam al-Ghuyoob,” the Knower of the unseen, the One whose knowledge encompasses the hidden, and the commentators note the form of the name, the intensive “‘allam,” the perfect Knower, and the scope of the “ghuyoob,” the unseen things, the plural that gathers every hidden reality, so that the verse teaches that nothing is hidden from the Lord, not the secret of the heart and not the whisper of the tongue, and the one who knows this cannot but live in the awareness of the seen. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s call to the sincerity that the knowledge of the unseen demands: the believer who knows that the Lord knows the secret is the believer whose secret is as pure as his public, and the man of the broken vow is the warning of the one who forgot the knowledge and imagined the concealment, and the question of the verse is the summons to the remembrance of the One who knows. The verse is the closing of the story, and the teaching that the knowledge of the unseen is the guard of the sincerity.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two knowledges, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own heart. The believer is taught that the concealment from the people is not the concealment from the Lord, and the secret that is hidden from the eye is open to the One who knows the unseen, and the one who would be sincere must live as one who is seen, the secret and the whisper alike presented to the Lord, for the Knower of the unseen is the witness of the heart. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the fourth of the four verses of the broken vow, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the sincerity: the Lord knows the secret and the whisper, and the one who knows the knowledge lives the sincerity, and the one who forgets it lives the hypocrisy that the previous verse named. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the seen secret: the question asked, the knowledge declared, and the unseen known — the warning against the self-deception of the concealed, and the summons to the sincerity of the seen.",
  "79": "“˹There are˺ those who slander ˹some of˺ the believers for donating liberally and mock others for giving only the little they can afford. Allah will throw their mockery back at them, and they will suffer a painful punishment” brings the secret talks into the open as slander about charity. The previous verse asked Do they not know Allah knows their secret and private counsel and is Knower of all unseen? This verse says what that counsel looks like: “There are those who slander some of the believers for donating liberally and mock others for giving only the little they can afford” or “allatheena yalmizoona al-muttawwi‘eena mina al-mu’mineen fi al-sadaqati wa allatheena la yajidoona illa juhdahum fa-yaskharoona minhum.” The first group, or “muttawwi‘een,” volunteers who give much, like the generous Ansar who sheltered and gave, are slandered as showing off, the same yalmizuka criticism of alms distribution where if given they are pleased if not enraged, and the same hurt by calling the Prophet an ear. The second group, those who find only “juhd,” little effort, little they can afford, like the poor who donate a handful of dates, are mocked, as the lowly who believed among Thamud were questioned “Are you certain Salih is sent?” and the humble the Heights swore would never receive mercy.\n\nThe divine response “Allah will throw their mockery back at them, and they will suffer a painful punishment” or “sakhira Allahu minhum wa lahum athabun aleem” uses “sakhira,” to mock, to turn mockery back, as earlier truth prevailed and illusions failed, and the spending to hinder became regret. The painful, or “aleem,” punishment is the same that hoarders’ treasure heated brands, that those who hurt the Prophet will suffer, that hypocrites fear a surah will expose, and that awaits the rebellious who allocate sacred months and consume wealth wrongfully.\n\nThis behavior is the social form of withholding hands, encouraging evil, forbidding good, neglecting so neglected, the rebellious whose example is the dog panting whether chased or left, the worst deaf and dumb, the heedless cattle, the evil example whose deeds are void and who are true losers whose stories of Noah, ’Ad, Thamud should have warned.\n\nA common error sees mocking a small donation as harmless. The Quran makes it a sign of the same hypocrisy plaguing hearts until the meeting, whose donations even willing or unwilling will never be accepted because they come half-heartedly, resentfully, without faith, whose wealth and children are worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers. The community told “If only they had been content ... Allah is sufficient ... to Him we turn with hope” and given alms only for eight, poor, needy, administrators, those whose hearts are attracted, freeing slaves, debtors, Allah’s cause, travellers, obligation from the All-Knowing, All-Wise, is thereby taught to honour both the liberal and the little giver, as each knew its spring among twelve and each tribe knew its drinking place.",
  "80": "“˹It does not matter˺ whether you ˹O Prophet˺ pray for them to be forgiven or not. Even if you pray for their forgiveness seventy times, Allah will never forgive them. That is because they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger. And Allah does not guide the rebellious people” follows the slander of donors, where Allah throws mockery back with painful punishment, by closing the door on intercession for such persistent mockers. The opening “It does not matter whether you O Prophet pray for them to be forgiven or not. Even if you pray for their forgiveness seventy times, Allah will never forgive them” or “istaghfir lahum aw la tastaghfir lahum in tastaghfir lahum sab‘eena marratan fa-lan yaghfira Allahu lahum” is a strong statement after earlier “If they repent it will be better for them, if they turn away painful punishment in both worlds and no protector” and after “But those who commit evil then repent and become believers, your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” and after “We will turn in grace to whoever He wills, Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.” The seventy times is idiomatic for many, as the prior decree that made spoils lawful spared a tremendous punishment but did not make persistence without repentance acceptable, and as the prior seventy men chosen for the appointment were tested by earthquake.\n\nThe reason “That is because they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger” or “thalika bi-annahum kafaroo billahi wa rasoolihi” repeats the root of donations not being accepted because they lost faith, pray lazily, give resentfully, the same loss that makes deeds void and hypocrites the rebellious, the worst deaf and dumb, the heedless cattle, the losers who enjoyed their share as those before them who were superior in might and more abundant.\n\nThe seal “And Allah does not guide the rebellious people” or “wa Allahu la yahdi al-qawma al-fasiqeen” repeats the seal for reallocating sacred months as increase in disbelief, for consuming wealth wrongfully and hindering, for knowing rebellious hearts. Guidance is the same furqan granted to the mindful, the straight path perfectly straight, the best of the Tablets, the light with which the unlettered Prophet was sent to prevail, the same guidance the heedless are turned away from even if they see every sign.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks the Prophet’s prayer can override persistent rebellion without repentance. The verse says even seventy prayers will not, because the barrier is not the Prophet’s will but their own faithlessness and mocking, the same mocking “We were only talking idly and joking, was it Allah, His revelations, Messenger you ridiculed?” and the hurt “He listens to anyone.” The door remains open only through the triad repent, pray, pay, brotherhood, and the sincere “If our Lord does not have mercy and forgive we will be losers,” not through repeated prayer while persisting, as next verses will show those who remained behind rejoiced in staying and hated to strive, saying “Do not march in the heat” while Fire of Hell is hotter, and will be told they will weep much after laughing little.",
  "81": "“Those ˹hypocrites˺ who remained behind rejoiced for doing so in defiance of the Messenger of Allah and hated ˹the prospect of˺ striving with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allah. They said ˹to one another˺, ‘Do not march forth in the heat.’ Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘The Fire of Hell is far hotter!’ If only they could comprehend!” describes the rejoicing of those who lagged, after the seventy-times prayer that will never forgive the rebellious. The phrase “Those hypocrites who remained behind rejoiced for doing so in defiance of the Messenger of Allah and hated the prospect of striving with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allah” or “fariha al-mukhallafona bi-maq‘adihim khilafa Rasool Allahi wa karihoo an yujahidoo bi-amwalihim wa anfusihim fi sabeeli Allah” uses “fariha,” rejoiced, as earlier the fish hoarders and the prosperity claimants rejoiced, and the caravan wishers rejoiced at “This is what we deserve,” while the true believers’ joy is the Gardens’ everlasting bliss. Remaining behind, or “maqa‘d,” is the seat left, the same “Stay with those helpless who remain” for those whose hearts Allah disliked they should go, the infertile hardly producing that the winds still pass over. Hating to strive with wealth and lives is the wealth-and-children fitnah and beloveds more dear test, the same test that required marching whether easy or difficult, best if only you knew, and preparing what you can of power and horses.\n\nThe counsel they gave each other “Do not march forth in the heat” or “wa qaloo la tanfiroo fil-harri” refers to Tabuk in extreme summer, the long distance that seemed too long, the heavy clinging to land, the heat that made drowsiness as security for the patient. The reply “Say, O Prophet, ‘The Fire of Hell is far hotter!’ If only they could comprehend!” or “qul naru Jahannama ashaddu harran law kanoo yafqahoon” uses “ashaddu harran,” far hotter, the same heat that branded foreheads, sides, backs where treasure heated, the heat that makes wealth and children worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers, the heat whose gates do not open for the arrogant and whose bed is Hell with flames as cover. “Law kanoo yafqahoon,” if only they could comprehend, is the same “yafqahoon” for which lessons are varied for the thankful, the same understanding that hearts not understanding lack, even less than cattle.\n\nThis links to the next “So let them laugh a little—they will weep much as a reward for what they have committed” and to the wise “If Allah returns you to a group of them and they ask to go forth, say you will not ever go with me, you preferred to stay the first time, so stay with the helpless.” A common error excuses heat as valid exemption. The Quran says heat of Hell is hotter, the mindful with furqan see that, and the true heat to fear is the Fire whose ultimately disgrace is forever, not the sun whose wind ushers heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit for the thankful.",
  "82": "“So let them laugh a little—they will weep much as a reward for what they have committed” gives the recompense for the rejoicing behind in defiance of the Messenger and hating to strive, and for saying “Do not march in the heat.” The previous verse said the Fire of Hell is far hotter, if only they could comprehend. This verse says “So let them laugh a little—they will weep much as a reward for what they have committed” or “fa-l-yadha koo qaleelan wa la-yabkoo kathiran jaza’an bima kanu yaksiboon.” “Yadha koo qaleelan,” laugh a little, is the brief, near, lowly enjoyment, or “qaleel,” like the little enjoyment of this world compared to the Hereafter that was insignificant, like the fleeting gains this world for which the believers were rebuked for settling, like the share enjoyed by those before them superior in might. “Yabkoo kathiran,” weep much, is the long, abundant weeping, the same much weeping that the truthful would do if they knew the mountain raised like a cloud and the barrier between Paradise and Hell, the same much weeping that is the recompense, or “jaza’an,” as ranks according to deeds, as “They will receive what is destined for them until angels arrive asking where false gods you invoked are,” and “Enter the Fire with groups that preceded you, each cursing its predecessor.”\n\nThe phrase “as a reward for what they have committed” or “jaza’an bima kanu yaksiboon” ties directly to “Allah is never unjust to His creation, this is for what your hands have done” after angels beat faces and backs to taste burning, and to “You hypocrites are like those before you superior in might, more abundant in wealth and children, enjoyed share, waded as they waded, their deeds void, they are true losers.” The rejoicing was the enjoying of share, the wading as before, the clinging to land, the heat-avoidance, all earned, or “kasab,” acquisition that makes the scale light.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats laugh and weep as mere emotions. The Quran treats them as measures of the two abodes: laugh little is the near lowly gain, weep much is the everlasting stay sufficient for hypocrites and disbelievers, condemned, never-ending punishment, the disgrace that is ultimate, the Fire that engulfs, the treasure heated to brand. The next verse’s command “If Allah returns you to a group of them and they ask to go, say you will not ever go with me, you preferred to stay the first time, so stay with the helpless” makes the little laugh a lasting exclusion from future marching, showing the choice to cling heavily now becomes the weeping much later, before the appointed term that cannot be delayed.",
  "83": "“If Allah returns you ˹O Prophet˺ to a group of them and they ask to go forth with you, say, ‘You will not ever go forth or fight an enemy along with me. You preferred to stay behind the first time, so stay with those ˹helpless˺ who remain behind’” gives the practical exclusion for those who laughed a little to weep much. The previous verse decreed they will weep much as reward for what they committed, the same rejoicing who remained behind in defiance, hating to strive, saying “Do not march in the heat.” The condition “If Allah returns you O Prophet to a group of them and they ask to go forth with you” or “fa-in raja‘aka Allahu ila ta’ifatin minhum fa-istathanooka lil-khurooj” anticipates the aftermath of Tabuk, where the lagging group that was disliked to go and told “Stay with the helpless” now wants to join a later expedition, perhaps one with near gain within reach and short journey that they would have followed. The answer is firm: “You will not ever go forth or fight an enemy along with me. You preferred to stay behind the first time, so stay with those helpless who remain behind” or “fa-qul lan takhrujoo ma‘iya abadan wa lan tuqaatiloo ma‘iya aduwwan innakum radeetum bil-qu‘oodi awwala marrah fa-uq‘udoo ma‘a al-khalifeen.” The “lan... abadan,” never ever, mirrors the “gates will not be opened until a camel passes through the needle” impossibility, but here it is disciplinary, not eternal damnation unless they persist. The reason cites preference, or “radeetum,” you were pleased, content with sitting behind the first time, the same contentment that should have been “Allah is sufficient for us, He will grant from His bounty” but instead was contentment to remain with the helpless, or “khalifeen,” those left behind, the same helpless mentioned for those who would be trouble if they had gone.\n\nThis exclusion mirrors the earlier “Had they gone they would have been trouble, scrambling to spread discord, with some eager listeners, Allah has perfect knowledge of wrongdoers,” and earlier “Had they intended they would have prepared, but Allah disliked they should go, so He let them lag, stay with the helpless.” Staying is therefore both their choice and divine dislike, the two faces of the same fitnah.\n\nA common error expects that after Tabuk, booty and honour can still be shared without having shared the heat. The verse says the heat’s avoidance has a cost: exclusion from future going forth and fighting along with the Prophet, the same Prophet whose ear is good for you, whose protection is from Allah who revealed the Book, whose sufficiency is Allah and followers. The next verse, not offering funeral prayers for any of their dead nor standing by grave for they lost faith and died rebellious, shows the exclusion extends, reinforcing that wealth and children of the rebellious are not impressive, those who hoard will be heated and branded, while the believers’ merchandise is the great reward with Allah forever.",
  "84": "“And do not ever offer ˹funeral˺ prayers for any of their dead, nor stand by their grave ˹at burial˺, for they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger and died rebellious” extends the exclusion from going forth to the rites for the dead, after commanding “You will not ever go forth or fight an enemy along with me, you preferred to stay the first time, so stay with the helpless.” The phrase “And do not ever offer funeral prayers for any of their dead, nor stand by their grave at burial” or “wa la tusalli ala ahadin minhum maata abadan wa la taqum ala qabrihi” uses “tusalli,” to pray, the same establishing prayer praised for the mindful and the true believers, now forbidden for the hypocrites who lost faith after belief, mocked revelations, hurt the Prophet, waded as those before them, whose deeds are void. Funeral prayer, or “salat al-janazah,” is a mercy, asking forgiveness, the same forgiveness seventy times sought but never to be given for the rebellious, the same “Had Allah not been with them while you are among them nor while they seek forgiveness they would be punished,” but here forgiveness is not sought for those who died rebellious, or “matoo wa hum fasiqoon.” Standing by the grave, or “taqum ala qabrihi,” is the honour of witnessing burial, the same honour denied to the disgraced apes and the lifeless in homes, the opposite of the honour of the Gardens’ splendid homes.\n\nThe reason “for they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger and died rebellious” or “innahum kafaroo billahi wa rasoolihi wa maatoo wa hum fasiqoon” repeats the diagnosis “That is because they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger, they never come to prayer except half-heartedly, never donate except resentfully, they are the rebellious” that made donations not accepted even willing or unwilling, the same rebellious who reallocate sacred months as increase in disbelief, who consume wealth wrongfully and hinder, whose evil deeds are made appealing.\n\nThis prohibition links to the wealth-and-children not to impress, for Allah intends to torment them through these in this world then souls depart while disbelievers, and to the not being pleased by their numbers at Hunain, to the seizing lowly gain while claiming “We will be forgiven.”\n\nA common misunderstanding sees funeral prayer as mere courtesy. The Quran shows it is a testimony of belonging, like the declaration “Are these the humble you swore would never receive mercy? Enter Paradise, no fear for you,” and like the barrier’s recognition by marks. To pray for the dead who died after losing faith and being rebellious would blur the furqan granted to the mindful to distinguish. For the community that shelters, helps, and guards one another as true believers, this teaches that guardianship does not include praying for those who chose opposition to Allah and His Messenger and persisted, whose fire is forever and disgrace is ultimate, while the everlasting Gardens remain for those who believed, emigrated, strove, sheltered, and helped.",
  "85": "“And let neither their wealth nor children impress you ˹O Prophet˺. Allah only intends to torment them through these things in this world, and ˹then˺ their souls will depart while they are disbelievers” repeats the wealth-children warning after the funeral exclusion, now after the hypocrites’ rejoicing, laughing little, and exclusion from future going forth. The phrase “And let neither their wealth nor children impress you” or “wa la tu‘jibka amwaluhum wa awladuhum” repeats the earlier “So let neither their wealth nor children impress you, Allah only intends to torment them through these in this worldly life, then their souls will depart while they are disbelievers” said before they swore they are part of you but are not, out of fear, rushing to refuge. The repetition is intentional, bracketing the entire passage from slander of alms, through hoarding gold to branding, through sacred months, clinging to land, painful torment and replacement, cave support, striving whether easy or difficult, near gain and long distance, pardon why give permission before truthful distinguished, and the whole hypocrite anatomy from seeking discord, falling into temptation, grieving at blessing, trusting in Allah, awaiting two best things, to the remaining-behind rejoicing. At both ends, the same wealth and children that make the hypocrites appear successful are the very instrument of worldly torment, like the treasure heated to brand foreheads, sides, backs where hoarded, like the spending to hinder that becomes regret, like the cattle-like heedlessness that is like the dog panting.\n\nThe intent “Allah only intends to torment them through these things in this world” or “innama yureedu Allahu an yu‘aththibahum biha fid-dunya” shows the torment is not yet the Fire forever and ultimate disgrace, but a worldly foretaste: anxiety to protect wealth, need to distribute alms begrudgingly, fear of exposure that makes them rush to caves, and the final departure “and then their souls will depart while they are disbelievers” or “wa tazhaqa anfusuhum wa hum kafiroon,” the same departure beating faces and backs to taste burning, for what hands sent forth, with Allah never unjust to creation. The torment through wealth and children in this world parallels the torment through the same wealth at Hunain when earth closed despite vastness, and the enrichment that would have been from bounty if He wills but was withheld from the rebellious.\n\nA common error envies the hypocrites’ apparent family success. The verse says do not be impressed; the very things envied are the snare. The true impressiveness remains the Gardens under which rivers flow forever, splendid homes, and greatest is Allah’s pleasure, the ultimate triumph, for those who encouraged good, forbade evil, established, paid, obeyed, and are shown mercy by the Almighty, All-Wise.",
  "86": "“Whenever a sûrah is revealed stating, ‘Believe in Allah and struggle along with His Messenger,’ the rich among them would ask to be exempt, saying, ‘Leave us with those who remain behind’” pinpoints the hypocrites’ reaction to the call that should have produced the opposite. The previous verses described those who remained behind rejoicing, hating to strive, saying “Do not march in the heat,” then the command to let them laugh little and weep much, and the exclusion from ever going forth or fighting alongside, and the ban on funeral prayers. Now the trigger is named: “Whenever a sûrah is revealed stating, ‘Believe in Allah and struggle along with His Messenger’” or “wa itha unzilat sooratun an aminoo billahi wa jahidoo ma‘a rasoolihi.” The surah’s revelation is the Book explained with knowledge, the insight, guide, and mercy that the mindful listen to attentively and silently. Its command is belief and jihad with wealth and lives, the same triad of belief, emigration, striving that made guardians of one another and that made rank greater with Allah.\n\nThe rich among them, or “oolu al-tawli minhum,” those with means, resources, ability, ask to be exempt, saying “Leave us with those who remain behind” or “thar na nakun ma‘a al-qa‘ideen.” The rich have the means that earlier was a fitnah test and a preparation of power and horses resource, yet they choose the helpless bench, the same “Stay with the helpless” told to those whose going Allah disliked and made lag, the same helpless whose hearts are sealed so they do not comprehend, and whose wealth and children are worldly torment. Their request mirrors the earlier “Exempt me and do not tempt me” already fallen, and the “We hear but do not listen” worst beings.\n\nThis links to the next verse where they preferred to stay with the helpless and hearts were sealed so they do not comprehend, showing preference determines sealing, as earlier those who even if they see every sign will not believe prefer the crooked path.\n\nA common error excuses the rich from striving due to wealth management. The Quran shows the rich are the very ones called first when the surah says believe and struggle with the Messenger; their exemption request, “Leave us with those who remain,” reveals the heart between which Allah stands has chosen the little enjoyment of this world over the Hereafter, the laugh little, the hoarded gold to be heated and branded, the reallocating of sacred months as increase in disbelief. The alternative remains the Messenger and believers who strived with wealth and lives and will have all the best and ultimate triumph in Gardens forever, the same gardens under rivers and splendid homes whose greatest is Allah’s pleasure.",
  "87": "“They preferred to stay behind with the helpless, and their hearts have been sealed so they do not comprehend” states the consequence of the rich asking to be left with those who remain. The previous verse described whenever a surah is revealed to believe and struggle with the Messenger, the rich ask to be exempt, saying “Leave us with those who remain.” This verse says “They preferred to stay behind with the helpless” or “radoo bi-an yakoonoo ma‘a al-khawalifi.” The verb “radoo,” to be pleased, to be content, echoes the wrong contentment of those critical of alms who are pleased if given, enraged if not, and the false contentment “Allah is sufficient” that the hypocrites lacked, and the contentment that should have been “He gives from His bounty” for alms. Here they are pleased, or “radoo,” to be with the “khawalif,” those left behind, the helpless, women, children, ill, and the excused poor, the same “Stay with the helpless” told to those Allah disliked should go.\n\nThe result “and their hearts have been sealed so they do not comprehend” or “wa tubi‘a ala quloobihim fa-hum la yafqahoon” uses “tubi‘a,” sealed, as earlier “We will turn away from My signs those who act arrogantly” even if they see every sign will not believe, the Sabbath town’s rescue of warners and dreadful punishment for the rebellious and transformation into apes, and the mountain covenant to hold firmly. Sealing, or “tab‘,” makes the heart unable to hear even if hearing is granted, as earlier had Allah known any goodness He would have made them hear, but even if made to hear they would turn heedlessly. The lack of “yafqahoon,” understanding, comprehension, is the infertile hardly producing despite winds, the cattle-like heedless, the dog panting whether chased or left.\n\nThis connects to the next verse where the Messenger and believers who strived with wealth and lives will have all the best and ultimate triumph, contrasting the sealed who preferred helplessness with the strivers who will have Gardens forever, the ultimate triumph, whose reward with Allah is great and everlasting.\n\nA common misunderstanding pities the sealed as victims. The verse shows sealing follows preference: they preferred to stay with the helpless, so hearts were sealed. For the community reminded not to be impressed by hypocrites’ wealth and children whose worldly torment precedes departure while disbelievers, and whose hoarded gold will brand, the warning is that preferring the bench when the surah says believe and struggle makes the heart join the helpless permanently, before the barrier and the weighing just and the Fire sufficient for hypocrites and disbelievers.",
  "88": "“But the Messenger and the believers with him strived with their wealth and their lives. They will have all the best, and it is they who will be successful” provides the luminous counterpart to the rich who preferred to stay with the helpless and whose hearts were sealed so they do not comprehend. The phrase “But the Messenger and the believers with him strived with their wealth and their lives” or “lakini al-rasoolu wa allatheena amanoo ma‘ahu jahadoo bi-amwalihim wa anfusihim” repeats the triad of belief, wealth, and lives, the same striving that made believers guardians of one another to prevent great oppression, that made rank greater with Allah for those who believed, emigrated, and strove, and that was the content of the surah revealed to believe and struggle with the Messenger. Their striving includes preparing power and horses to deter, spending what will be paid in full, and donating from what He provided, the opposite of withholding hands and consuming wealth wrongfully and hoarding gold to be branded.\n\nThe promise “They will have all the best, and it is they who will be successful” or “ula’ika lahumu al-khayratu wa ula’ika humu al-muflihoon” uses “khayrat,” all the good, best things, the same khair that wealth and children test and that the beloveds rival, now as elevated ranks, forgiveness, honourable provision, mercy, pleasure, Gardens forever, splendid homes, and greatest is Allah’s pleasure. Success, or “muflihoon,” is the same falah as heavy scales, as the Gardens with rivers, as the ultimate triumph, the same falah that the Heights hoped to enter, the same success the hypocrites called delusion when they said believers are deluded by faith but who put trust in Allah, Almighty, All-Wise, is not deluded.\n\nThis links to the next verse’s Gardens under rivers forever, the ultimate triumph, prepared for them, showing the best is not the near lowly gain seized claiming “We will be forgiven” but the eternal. A common error despairs that striving is only for the battlefield. The Quran shows striving with wealth and lives includes the alms for eight, the hospitality that sheltered when few and oppressed, the contentment “Allah is sufficient,” and the firm abiding where reward is never discounted, all of which the sealed who preferred helplessness refused. The Messenger who is an ear of good for you and whose hurt brings painful punishment leads this striving, but the believers with him share the khayrat, proving guardianship is shared.",
  "89": "“Allah has prepared for them Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever. That is the ultimate triumph” details the “all the best” just promised to the Messenger and believers who strived with wealth and lives. The phrase “Allah has prepared for them Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever” or “a‘adda Allahu lahum jannatin tajri min tahtiha al-anharu khalideena fiha” repeats the Paradise described for the guardians of one another who encourage good, forbid evil, establish, pay, and obey, whose mercy is shown, for those who believed, emigrated, strived, sheltered, helped, true believers with forgiveness and honourable provision, and for the foremost Emigrants and Helpers and those who follow them in goodness, whom Allah is pleased with and they with Him, Gardens forever, the ultimate triumph. The rivers flow beneath, the same rivers where bitterness is removed and the noble Word fulfilled for those who patiently endured, the same provision where the earth, despite its vastness, does not close.\n\nThe seal “That is the ultimate triumph” or “thalika al-fawzu al-azeem” uses the ultimate, or “azeem,” as earlier the great reward with Allah forever, the great disgrace for opposing, the everlasting stay sufficient for hypocrites, the painful punishment for hoarding and mocking. Ultimate triumph is the opposite of the ultimate disgrace and the “so let them laugh little, they will weep much” recompense for what they committed.\n\nThis verse shows the temporal gap between preparation and triumph: the Messenger and believers who strived when the surah was revealed and the rich asked to be left with the helpless did not see immediate rivers, but Allah has already prepared, or “a‘adda,” past tense, indicating the provision is ready, like the twelve springs ready when Moses struck the rock and each tribe knew its drinking place, and like the shade of clouds and manna and quails, all already prepared for the thankful.\n\nA common misunderstanding measures triumph by spoils’ fifth or by the caravan’s near gain within reach and short journey. The verse measures by Gardens forever, prepared, where the pleasure of Allah is greatest, inviting the rich who preferred helplessness and the nomadic Arabs who came with excuses and those untrue who remained behind and will be afflicted with painful punishment to reconsider, and inviting the weak, sick, or lacking means who are true to Allah and Messenger and are good-doers with no blame, and those who came for mounts weeping that they have nothing to contribute, to see that striving is not only wealth and lives on the field but also truthfulness, and that the prepared Gardens await both.",
  "90": "“Some nomadic Arabs ˹also˺ came with excuses, seeking exemption. And those who were untrue to Allah and His Messenger remained behind ˹with no excuse˺. The unfaithful among them will be afflicted with a painful punishment” widens the exemption discussion from the town’s rich to the desert tribes. The previous verses exposed the rich who asked to be left with the helpless and were sealed, versus the Messenger and believers who strived and will have ultimate triumph in Gardens forever. This verse says “Some nomadic Arabs also came with excuses, seeking exemption. And those who were untrue to Allah and His Messenger remained behind with no excuse” or “wa ja’a al-mu‘aththiroona mina al-a‘rabi li-yu’thana lahum wa qa‘ada allatheena kathaboo Allaha wa rasoolahu.” The “mu‘aththiroon,” those with excuses, are those who presented a reason to be left, like being told “Had the gain been within reach and journey shorter they would have followed” but distance seemed too long, and “Exempt me, do not tempt me” already fallen into fitnah, and the rich who preferred helplessness. Some nomads came and were excused, as the next verses will excuse the weak, sick, and lacking means who are true, and those who came weeping for no mounts and left with eyes overflowing. The others, “those who were untrue to Allah and His Messenger remained behind with no excuse” or “qa‘ada allatheena kathaboo,” simply stayed, the same “qaa‘ada” as the lagging told “Stay with the helpless,” the same self-ruin of swearing “Had we been able we would have joined,” Allah knows they lie.\n\nThe warning “The unfaithful among them will be afflicted with a painful punishment” or “sa-yuseebu allatheena kafaroo minhum athabun aleem” repeats the Aleem that branded hoarders, that awaits those who hurt the Messenger, that is sufficient for hypocrites and disbelievers in Hell, the ultimate disgrace. The painful here is both worldly disgrace like apes and branded foreheads and otherworldly Fire, as earlier “He will torment them through wealth and children in this world then souls depart while disbelievers” and “If they repent better, if they turn away painful torment in both worlds and no protector.”\n\nThis links to the next distinction of no blame on the weak, sick, lacking if true, good-doers, with Allah Forgiving, Merciful, versus blame only on those with means who preferred to stay with the helpless and whose hearts are sealed so they do not realize.\n\nA common error lumps all nomads as sincere or all as hypocrites. The Quran distinguishes the excused who came, the untrue who stayed with no excuse, and later the nomads worse in disbelief and hypocrisy less likely to know laws versus those who believe and consider donation as closeness and prayers of Messenger bringing closer, to be admitted into mercy, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  "91": "“There is no blame on the weak, the sick, or those lacking the means ˹if they stay behind˺, as long as they are true to Allah and His Messenger. There is no blame on the good-doers. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” provides the merciful exception after the painful warning to the unfaithful nomads who remained behind with no excuse. The opening “There is no blame on the weak, the sick, or those lacking the means if they stay behind, as long as they are true to Allah and His Messenger” or “laisa ala al-du‘afa’i wa la ala al-marda wa la ala allatheena la yajidoona ma yunfiqoona harajun itha nasahoo lillahi wa rasoolihi” lists three excused groups: the weak, or “du‘afa,” like the helpless behind, the sick, or “marda,” like those with sickness in hearts but here bodily illness, and those who find nothing to spend, like the weeping seekers of mounts who had nothing to contribute and whose eyes overflowed. “Haraj,” blame, burden, is like the heaviness “iththaqaltum ila al-ardi” and the “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford” after commanding full measure. The condition “as long as they are true to Allah and His Messenger” or “itha nasahoo,” to be sincere, to be true, to give nasihah, the same sincere advice that Noah gave, that Hud gave, and that the hypocrites lacked, shows even the excused must be inwardly true, not merely outwardly weak.\n\nThe reassurance “There is no blame on the good-doers. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” or “ma ala al-muhsineena min sabeel wa Allahu Ghafoorun Raheem” uses “muhsineen,” good-doers, those who do ihsan, like those who guide with truth and establish justice, who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, whose spending to hinder is not theirs. Their path has “no sabil,” no blame, no way against them, opposite to blame only on those who seek exemption though they have means and preferred to stay with the helpless and whose hearts are sealed.\n\nThis connects directly to the next verse where those who came for mounts and were told “I can find no mounts for you” left weeping, and to the later foremost Emigrants and Helpers pleased, and to the purification of wealth through charity to bless and comfort with prayer from the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks exemption equals discouragement. The verse shows the excused true good-doers are not discouraged but forgiven, merciful, while the capable who preferred helplessness are sealed. For the community told to respond to what gives life, where Allah stands between person and heart, this teaches to distinguish true weakness from the rich who asked to be left, and to remain good-doers even when staying behind, so the great reward with Allah forever remains reachable.",
  "92": "“Nor ˹is there any blame on˺ those who came to you ˹O Prophet˺ for mounts, then when you said, ‘I can find no mounts for you,’ they left with eyes overflowing with tears out of grief that they had nothing to contribute” describes the most moving of the blameless, the weepers. The previous verse excused the weak, sick, and lacking means if they are true to Allah and Messenger, calling them good-doers with no blame, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. This verse singles out “those who came to you O Prophet for mounts, then when you said, ‘I can find no mounts for you,’ they left with eyes overflowing with tears out of grief that they had nothing to contribute” or “wa la ala allatheena itha ma atawka li-tahmilahum qulta la ajidu ma ahmilukum alaihi tawallaw wa a‘yunuhum tafeedu mina al-dam‘i hazanan alla yajidoo ma yunfiqoon.” The seekers, or “ith ma atawka li-tahmilahum,” came to be carried, to be mounted, like preparing power and cavalry to deter, like the twelve springs where each tribe knew its place, like the manna and quails provided, but no mount was found, as earlier the Prophet had no mounts to give, showing the community’s poverty despite being sheltered and strengthened with help. Their response, eyes overflowing with tears out of grief that they had nothing to contribute, is the opposite of the rich who asked to be left, of the hoarders whose treasure will brand, of the reallocators whose evil deeds are made appealing, and of those critical of alms who are pleased if given, enraged if not. Tears, or “tafedu mina al-dam‘i,” overflowing, show the heart trembling at remembrance, faith increasing, trust in the Lord, the same weep much that will later be punishment for hypocrites who laughed little, but here weeping is out of love for the cause.\n\nThis group is the fertile land that would produce abundantly if given water, the thankful for whom lessons are varied, the mindful who will have far better Home, the ones who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, the foremost who will be pleased. Their lack of means does not make them Helpless in censure but helpless in capacity, excused like the weak and sick, but distinguished by tears that prove naseehah is true.\n\nThe next verse will say blame is only on those who seek exemption though they have means, preferred to stay, hearts sealed so they do not realize, showing the tearful have no blame while the capable who preferred have all blame.\n\nA common error equates inability with hypocrisy. The Quran honors these weepers as good-doers, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, whose eyes, unlike the hypocrites’ eyes facing you but not seeing and whose ears like cattle not hearing, see and hear with grief that testifies to faith, and whose provision will be, as the next alms for eight and the charity to purify and bless, part of the honourable provision whose ultimate triumph is Gardens forever where the word “We were promised” is fulfilled and the barrier’s Heights finally enter with “No fear for you.”",
  "93": "“Blame is only on those who seek exemption from you although they have the means. They preferred to stay behind with the helpless, and Allah has sealed their hearts so they do not realize ˹the consequences˺” returns to blame after praising the tearful who had no mounts and no blame as good-doers. The previous verse said there is no blame on the weak, sick, lacking if true, and on those weeping for no mounts. This verse says “Blame is only on those who seek exemption from you although they have the means. They preferred to stay behind with the helpless, and Allah has sealed their hearts so they do not realize” or “innama al-sabeelu ala allatheena yasta’thinoonaka wa hum aghniyau’ radoo bi-an yakoonoo ma‘a al-khawalifi wa taba‘a Allahu ala quloobihim fa-hum la ya‘lamoon.” The blame, or “sabeel,” a way against, is only on those with “aghniya,” rich, having means like the rich who asked to be left with those who remain, the same rich whose alms criticism showed conditional pleasure and whose wealth and children are worldly torment.\n\nThe description “They preferred to stay behind with the helpless, and Allah has sealed their hearts so they do not realize” repeats the earlier “They preferred to stay with the helpless, and their hearts have been sealed so they do not comprehend” for the same group, but with “la ya‘lamoon,” not realize, not know consequences, the same lack of knowledge that made them say “This is what we deserve” in prosperity and blame Moses in adversity and that makes most not know that all omens are with Allah. Sealing here is like turning away from signs even if they see every sign, the mountain that was levelled, the sea split, the calf that neither speaks, the gate “Hittah” changed, all become for them unseen.\n\nThis also links to the earlier hearing test: had Allah known any goodness He would have made them hear, but even if made to hear they would turn heedlessly. The seal is therefore the recompense for preferring helplessness when they had means, just as theHoarders’ treasure heated is requital for withholding.\n\nA common misunderstanding lumps the tearful with the rich excuse-seekers. The Quran separates with “innama,” only, making the sabil exclusive to the capable who preferred, while the tearful are muhsineen with no sabil. For the warner who is only to convey, this exclusivity clarifies that the surah revealed stating “Believe and struggle with the Messenger” distinguishes, as earlier “If you ask why give them permission before the truthful were distinguished,” and as later “Your deeds will be observed by Allah, Messenger, and believers, and you will be returned to the Knower of seen and unseen, He will inform you,” showing the sabil is observed and will be informed.",
  "94": "“They will make excuses to you ˹believers˺ when you return to them. Say, ‘Make no excuses, ˹for˺ we will not believe you. Allah has already informed us about your ˹true˺ state ˹of faith˺. Your ˹future˺ deeds will be observed by Allah and His Messenger as well. And you will be returned to the Knower of the seen and unseen, then He will inform you of what you used to do’” addresses the return from Tabuk, when the rich who preferred to stay with the helpless and whose hearts were sealed will now offer excuses. The phrase “They will make excuses to you believers when you return to them” or “ya‘tathiroona ilaikum itha raja‘tum ilaihim” is the same “i‘tithar,” excuse, that was rejected as “Make no excuses, you have lost faith after belief” for the mockers who waded and joked about Allah, revelations, Messenger. Now it is the exemption seekers who swore “Had we been able we would have joined” and asked “Leave us with those who remain.” Their excuses mirror the earlier “We were only talking idly” and the “We took precaution” and the “We will be forgiven while seizing again,” all heard by the All-Hearing who knows sirr and najwa and is Knower of all unseen.\n\nThe commanded reply “Say, ‘Make no excuses, for we will not believe you. Allah has already informed us about your true state’” or “qul la ta‘tathiroo lan nu’mina lakum qad nabba’ana Allahu min akhbarikum” uses “nabba’a,” to inform, the same informing as “We will give them a full account with sure knowledge, for We were never absent” and “You will be returned to the Knower, He will inform you,” showing the believers’ disbelief is not stubbornness but divine informing, the same informing that made the valley’s appointment, the dream few, and the heart-union.\n\nThe future watch “Your future deeds will be observed by Allah and His Messenger as well. And you will be returned to the Knower of the seen and unseen, then He will inform you of what you used to do” or “wa sa-yara Allahu amalakum wa rasooluhu thumma turaddoona ila Aalimi al-ghaybi wa al-shahadati fa-yunabbi’ukum bima kuntum ta‘maloon” parallels the later “Do as you will, your deeds will be observed by Allah, Messenger, believers, and you will be returned to the Knower, He will inform you,” and the earlier gathering where deeds are weighed and ranks given. Observation is both worldly, by Allah and His Messenger and later believers, and otherworldly, by the Knower of ghayb and shahadah. The excuse-makers’ deeds are already marked, and future deeds will also be seen.\n\nA common error treats excuses after return as harmless. The Quran treats them as continuations of the disbelief that even if they see every sign will not believe, whose donations even willing or unwilling will never be accepted, whose wealth and children are torment, whose swearing they are part of you out of fear is not believed. The proper response is to not believe the excuse, to leave them alone—they are evil, Hell home as reward—while still being told to firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted.",
  "95": "“When you return, they will swear to you by Allah so that you may leave them alone. So leave them alone—they are truly evil. Hell will be their home as a reward for what they have committed” follows the command to say “Make no excuses, we will not believe you, Allah has informed us.” The phrase “When you return, they will swear to you by Allah so that you may leave them alone” or “sa-yahlifoona billahi lakum itha inqalabtum ilaihim li-tu‘ridoo anhum” describes the oath tactic, the same swearing that earlier “They swear by Allah they are part of you but they are not, out of fear” and “Had we been able we would have joined” Allah knows they lie, and the oath to please believers while Allah and Messenger are more deserving. The purpose “so that you may leave them alone” or “li-tu‘ridoo anhum” is to be left alone, excused, as earlier “Leave them alone—they are evil” and “Turn away from those who act ignorantly” and “Be gracious, enjoin right, turn from the ignorant” were the Prophetic ethic. Here leaving is commanded but not as approval; it is exposure.\n\nThe divine assessment “So leave them alone—they are truly evil. Hell will be their home as a reward for what they have committed” or “fa-a‘ridoo anhum innahum rijsun wa ma’wahum Jahannamu jaza’an bima kanu yaksiboon” uses “rijsun,” impurity, evil, filth, like the spiritual impurity of polytheists who should not approach the Sacred Mosque after this year and whose whistling prayer was void, like the disgrace of apes, the dreadful punishment for rebellious. “Ma’wahum Jahannamu,” home is Hell, is the same Hell that will be their home, evil destination, for those who turned backs without manoeuvre, the bed with flames as cover, the gates not opening until camel passes needle, the everlasting stay sufficient for hypocrites and disbelievers, condemned, never-ending. “Jaza’an bima kanu yaksiboon,” as reward for what they earned, is the same earning that made hoarded treasure heated and that earned the branding, the same earning that made deeds void and losers, the same earning that will be weighed just and piled as foul to cast.\n\nThis leaving is the other side of “They will swear to please you, even if you are pleased, Allah will never be pleased with the rebellious.” Human leaving is granted, divine leaving is not; Allah leaves them in rijs until the Day they meet Him, hypocrisy plaguing hearts, as earlier He caused hypocrisy to plague until the meeting for breaking promise and lies.\n\nA common misunderstanding confuses leaving them alone with acceptance. The Quran says leave them alone because they are rijs, and Hell is home as jaza, while the believers’ way remains to encourage good, forbid evil, establish, pay, obey, and be shown mercy, and to firmly abide where reward is never discounted, and to remember the mountain held as a cloud to hold firmly.",
  "96": "“They will swear to you in order to please you. And even if you are pleased with them, Allah will never be pleased with the rebellious people” completes the oath cycle after “When you return they will swear so you may leave them alone, leave them—they are evil, Hell home as reward.” The phrase “They will swear to you in order to please you. And even if you are pleased with them, Allah will never be pleased with the rebellious people” or “yahlifoona lakum li-tardaw anhum fa-in tardaw anhum fa-inna Allaha la yarda an al-qawmi al-fasiqeen” shows the ultimate futility of their oath strategy. Swearing to please believers after being told “Make no excuses, we will not believe you, Allah has informed us” and after being left alone as rijs whose home is Hell is an attempt to regain the rida, pleasure, that should have been sought from Allah and His Messenger if they were true believers, as earlier “They swear to please you while Allah and His Messenger are more deserving to please if they are believers” and as the mercy, pleasure, Gardens with everlasting bliss and great reward that the Lord gives as good news. Their rida is now directed at people, while the divine rida, or “rida Allahu,” is withheld.\n\nThe verdict “Allah will never be pleased with the rebellious people” or “fa-inna Allaha la yarda an al-qawmi al-fasiqeen” names them fasiqeen, the rebellious who step out, the majority found not true to covenant, the Sabbath violators tested for rebelliousness, the reallocators whose evil deeds are made appealing, the hoarders whose treasure brands, the mockers whose deeds are void, the worst deaf and dumb, the dog panting, the cattle even less guided. Allah’s pleasure, or “rida,” is the opposite of disgrace that is ultimate for opposing, the same rida that is above the Gardens and the greatest triumph, and that is the pleasure the believers sought by believing, emigrating, striving, sheltering and helping to be guardians and prevent great oppression.\n\nThis verse links to the next about nomadic Arabs far worse in disbelief and hypocrisy less likely to know laws and Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise, showing that the rebellious people whose swearing to please is rejected are the hard nomads and city hypocrites alike, whose hearts were sealed so they do not comprehend.\n\nA common error thinks human pleasure can substitute for divine pleasure. The Quran shows even if the believers were pleased after swearing, Allah never will be pleased with the fasiq, because the pleasure they should have sought was His and His Messenger’s, through repentance, prayer, and zakat that makes them brothers, through contentment “Allah is sufficient for us,” through the alms only for eight as obligation, not through oaths that cover the secret and najwa that Allah fully knows as Knower of all unseen. For the successor who is well-versed and claims “We will be forgiven” while seizing, the path to rida remains firmly abiding where reward is never discounted, not swear-pleasing the people.",
  "97": "“The nomadic Arabs ˹around Medina˺ are far worse in disbelief and hypocrisy, and less likely to know the laws revealed by Allah to His Messenger. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” shifts from the city hypocrites who swore to please after being left alone, whose Hell is home, to the desert nomads around Medina. The phrase “The nomadic Arabs around Medina are far worse in disbelief and hypocrisy, and less likely to know the laws revealed by Allah to His Messenger” or “al-a‘rabu ashaddu kufran wa nifaqan wa ajdaru alla ya‘lamoo hudooda ma anzala Allahu ala rasoolihi” uses “a‘rab,” bedouin, nomads, whose harsh life, distance from knowledge circles, and tribal pride made them “ashaddu,” more intense, more severe in kufr and nifaq than city hypocrites who at least heard the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy and the surah revealed stating “Believe and struggle,” and who feared a surah exposing hearts. Less likely to know “hudood,” limits, boundaries, laws, like the twelve months, four sacred, not wronging in them, preparing power, alms for eight, fifth for close relatives, inheritance for arham, all detailed as fundamentals on the Tablets to take best of, the same limits the reallocators violated as increase in disbelief and the hoarders consumed wrongfully.\n\nThe names “And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “wa Allahu Aleemun Hakeem” close, as with the excused weak, sick, lacking who are good-doers, Forgiving, Merciful, and with the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful and good, and with the mountain covenant. He knows best what is hidden in hearts and who has goodness to make hear and who prefers to stay with helpless and is sealed, and He is wise to legislate hudood that distinguish the fertile from infertile, the thankful from hardly.\n\nThis verse links to the next two contrasting nomad types: among them are those who consider what they donate as loss and await misfortune, may ill-fortune befall them, Allah All-Hearing, All-Knowing, and those who believe and consider donation as closeness and prayers of Messenger bringing closer, to be admitted into mercy, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, showing the bedouin are not monolithic.\n\nA common misunderstanding stereotypes all nomads as pious simplicity or as irredeemable. The Quran says some are worse, less likely to know hudood, but others are believers whose donation is closeness. The criterion is not residence but learning the limits, the same limits the well-versed successors claimed to know yet indulged, saying “We will be forgiven,” while firmly abiding is rewarded never discounted. For the heedless like cattle even less guided, the lesson is to learn hudood from the Messenger, to call by the Most Beautiful Names, to be the group that guides with truth and establishes justice, lest the severe disbelief and hypocrisy of the desert become the hard seal.",
  "98": "“And among the nomads are those who consider what they donate to be a loss and await your misfortune. May ill-fortune befall them! And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” gives the first nomad type after saying nomads are far worse in disbelief and hypocrisy less likely to know hudood. The phrase “And among the nomads are those who consider what they donate to be a loss and await your misfortune” or “wa mina al-a‘rabi man yattakhithu ma yunfiqu maghraman wa yatarabbasu bikum al-dawa’ir” describes donation, or “Yunfiqu,” the same spending to be paid in full for deterrence and the alms for eight, but they see it as “maghram,” a fine, a loss, a penalty, like the hoarders who withhold hands and whose treasure will brand, like those who donate resentfully and come to prayer lazily and whose donations even willing or unwilling will never be accepted because they lost faith. Awaiting misfortune, or “yatarabbasu bikum al-dawa’ir,” to expect circles of evil to turn, to await the believers’ misfortune, is the same tarabbus as “Are you awaiting except one of the two best things? We await affliction,” and as the valley’s appointment and the rain’s trial. They await the believers’ circles, or “dawa’ir,” to close, as earlier the spending to hinder becomes regret, the whistling prayer becomes taste, and the treaty violators’ large numbers prove of no use.\n\nThe imprecation “May ill-fortune befall them!” or “alaihim da’iratu al-saw’” means upon them is the evil circle, the same circle they awaited, like the mountain that was thought to fall on them when raised like a cloud and levelled to dust when He appeared, and like the Fire that will be their home and the disgrace ultimate for opposing.\n\nThe names “And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” or “wa Allahu Samee‘un Aleem” close, as with “If you are tempted then seek refuge Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and with the sick hearts who say believers are deluded but whoever trusts, Almighty, All-Wise, and with the wealth-children impress warning. He hears the donation as loss talk and the awaiting misfortune whisper, and knows the reallocation of sacred months and the hoarding and the oath to please.\n\nA common error excuses donation as loss thinking as frugality. The Quran calls it nifaq worse in disbelief, the devil’s persistent plunge without sparing effort, the worst deaf and dumb, the cattle even less guided. The alternative next verse shows among nomads are those who consider donation as closeness and prayers of Messenger bringing closer, to be admitted into mercy, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, the fertile land producing abundantly by the Lord’s leave, whose closeness is the true da’irah of mercy.",
  "99": "“However, among the nomadic Arabs are those who believe in Allah and the Last Day, and consider what they donate as a means of coming closer to Allah and ˹receiving˺ the prayers of the Messenger. It will certainly bring them closer. Allah will admit them into His mercy. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” gives the second nomad type, the faithful donors, after the loss-awaiting type. The phrase “However, among the nomadic Arabs are those who believe in Allah and the Last Day, and consider what they donate as a means of coming closer to Allah and receiving the prayers of the Messenger” or “wa mina al-a‘rabi man yu’minu billahi wa al-yawmi al-akhiri wa yattakhithu ma yunfiqu qurubatin inda Allahi wa salawati al-rasooli” shows donative, or “yunfiqu,” not as maghram, loss, but as “qurubat,” nearness, closeness, means of nearness to Allah, the same nearness the guardian angels nearest to your Lord have in prostration and glorification, the same nearness the mountain raised sought to make mindful, and the same prayers, or “salawat,” of the Messenger that are a source of comfort for those purified by charity and for whom the Prophet prays, surely your prayer is comfort. This donation is the opposite of hoarding gold to be heated and branded, of spending to hinder until regret, of withholding hands and critical alms distribution where pleased if given, enraged if not.\n\nThe assurance “It will certainly bring them closer. Allah will admit them into His mercy” or “ala innaha qurbatun lahum sa-yudkhiluhumu Allahu fi rahmatihi” uses “rahmatihi,” His mercy, the same mercy close to good-doers, encompassing everything, ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, and that the Messenger’s prayer brings, like the reassurance and unseen forces at Hunain and the sakeenah in the cave. The donor’s closeness through giving mirrors the believer’s guardianship that prevents great oppression, the female and male believers guardians of one another who encourage good, forbid evil, establish prayer, pay zakat, obey, and are shown mercy.\n\nThe names “Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” or “inna Allaha Ghafoorun Raheem” close, as with the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful and good, the weak, sick, lacking true good-doers with no blame, and the weepers for no mounts whose eyes overflowed.\n\nA common misunderstanding lumps all nomads as worse in disbelief. The Quran distinguishes: some await misfortune and see donation as loss, may ill-fortune befall them, All-Hearing, All-Knowing; others believe and see donation as qurbat and salawat, will be admitted into mercy, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. The criterion is not tribe but whether hudood revealed to Messenger are learned, as earlier the foremost Emigrants, Helpers, and followers in goodness are pleased and Allah pleased with them, Gardens forever, ultimate triumph, and some around hypocrites have mastered hypocrisy known to Us who will punish twice then tremendous, while some others confessed mixing goodness with evil hoping He will turn, Ghafoor Raheem.",
  "100": "“As for the foremost—the first of the Emigrants and the Helpers—and those who follow them in goodness, Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him. And He has prepared for them Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. That is the ultimate triumph” crowns the nomad contrast with the highest rank in the community. The previous verses showed nomads worse in disbelief less likely to know limits, nomads who see donation as loss awaiting misfortune, and nomads who believe and see donation as closeness and prayers of Messenger bringing closer into mercy, Forgiving, Merciful. This verse says “As for the foremost—the first of the Emigrants and the Helpers—and those who follow them in goodness, Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him” or “wa al-sabiqoona al-awwaloona mina al-muhajireena wa al-ansari wa allatheena ittaba‘oohum bi-ihsan radiya Allahu anhum wa radoo anhu.” The foremost, or “sabiqoon,” are the first to believe after the primordial testimony and the mountain covenant, the same foremost who will be told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you” on the Heights, the same who struggled at Badr when few, at Hunain when reassurance came, at the cave as second of two. Emigrants, or “muhajiroon,” who believed, emigrated, and strived with wealth and lives, and Helpers, or “ansar,” who sheltered and helped, whose hearts He united when all riches could not, are the core guardians of one another. Those who follow them in goodness, or “ittaba‘oohum bi-ihsan,” extends the guardianship to every later believer who follows the light sent down with the unlettered Prophet, who firmly abides and establishes prayer, whose reward is never discounted.\n\nThe mutual pleasure “Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him” or “radiya Allahu anhum wa radoo anhu” is the rida greater than Gardens themselves, the same pleasure the Lord gives as good news of mercy, pleasure, and Gardens with everlasting bliss, the pleasure hypocrites swore to please believers for while Allah and Messenger are more deserving. Here pleasure is mutual and eternal, unlike the flattery with tongues while hearts deny.\n\nThe prepared “And He has prepared for them Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. That is the ultimate triumph” or “wa a‘adda lahum jannatin tajri tahtaha al-anharu khalideena fiha abadan thalika al-fawzu al-azeem” repeats the Gardens promised to the Messenger and believers who strived with wealth and lives will have all the best and ultimate triumph, now generationally extended.\n\nA common error restricts triumph to the foremost alone. The verse extends it to followers in goodness, inviting the impure-wealth hoarder, the sacred-month allocator, the clinger to land, to follow in goodness the foremost’s path, the same path perfectly straight made clear to the mindful, so perhaps they will return and reflect, before the home of the rebellious is shown.",
  "101": "“Some of the nomads around you ˹believers˺ are hypocrites, as are some of the people of Medina. They have mastered hypocrisy. They are not known to you ˹O Prophet˺; they are known to Us. We will punish them twice ˹in this world˺, then they will be brought back ˹to their Lord˺ for a tremendous punishment” warns that the nomad and city landscape still hides hypocrisy after praising the foremost. The phrase “Some of the nomads around you believers are hypocrites, as are some of the people of Medina. They have mastered hypocrisy” or “wa mimman hawlakum mina al-a‘rabi munafiqoona wa min ahli al-madeenati maradoo ala al-nifaqi” shows hypocrisy’s mastery, or “maradoo,” to be skilled, to be entrenched, like the skilled magician’s great magic and the hoarders’ consuming wrongfully, the same mastery that made devils persistently plunge associates deeper without sparing effort. The city hypocrites, like those who hurt the Prophet by calling him an ear, swear to please, oppose to abide in Hell, fear a surah exposing hearts, wade and joke, make no excuses, lost faith after belief, and whose donations even willing or unwilling will never be accepted because they pray lazily, give resentfully, and whose wealth and children are worldly torment, have mastered the art of concealment.\n\nThe distinction “They are not known to you O Prophet; they are known to Us” or “la ta‘lamuhum nahnu na‘lamuhum” shows the Knower of all unseen, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who hears humble and knows what is hidden in hearts, who stands between person and heart, who is never absent when giving a full account, who wrote twelve months in the Record, knows the hypocrites the Prophet, though ear of good, may not identify, just as the mountain’s firmness and the dream’s fewness were known to Him.\n\nThe punishment “We will punish them twice in this world, then they will be brought back to their Lord for a tremendous punishment” or “sa-nu‘aththibuhum marrataini thumma yuraddoona ila athabin azeem” uses twice, like the double torment of apes and of hoarders branded, like the punishment from Him or at your hands awaiting hypocrites, and like the famine and shortage to make them remember, then the tremendous, or “azeem,” punishment after return, like the Fire sufficient and never-ending, the ultimate disgrace, the heated treasure branding, the bed of Hell and flames as cover.\n\nA common misunderstanding assumes hidden hypocrisy escapes. The verse says though you do not know them, We know, and will punish twice then tremendous, while the foremost pleased and He pleased with them have Gardens forever, showing the two tracks: mastered hypocrisy leads to doubled then tremendous, mastered goodness, firmly abiding and establishing prayer, leads to ultimate triumph where reward is never discounted, whose prayer as mercy is comfort.",
  "102": "“Some others have confessed their wrongdoing: they have mixed goodness with evil. It is right to hope that Allah will turn to them in mercy. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” presents the middle group after the mastered hypocrites who will be punished twice then tremendous. The phrase “Some others have confessed their wrongdoing: they have mixed goodness with evil” or “wa akharoona i‘taraf oo bi-thunoobihim khalatoo amalan salihan wa akhara sayyi’an” describes those who neither were foremost nor mastered hypocrisy, but mixed, like the fertile-infertile wavering, the inheritors who indulged in lowly gain yet were well-versed, the weepers who had no mounts but had sincerity, the vow-makers who withheld then felt hypocrisy plague. Mixed, or “khalatoo,” mingled, shows deeds not void as with hypocrites whose deeds became void, nor pure as with the triumphant Gardens, but mixed, as the piling of evil and good before the weighing that will be just where heavy succeeds and light dooms.\n\nThe hope “It is right to hope that Allah will turn to them in mercy” or “asa Allahu an yatooba alaihim” uses “asa,” perhaps, hope, the same perhaps that motivates “perhaps they will return, abstain, become mindful, understand” and the cave’s “perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors.” Hope for turning, or “yatooba,” is the same turning whose seventy times prayer will never forgive the rebellious, but whose single sincere turn after calf worship was met with All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, whose sakeenah after Hunain was sent, and whose mercy encompasses everything and is ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe.\n\nThe names “Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” or “inna Allaha Ghafoorun Raheem” close, as with the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful and good, the charity to purify and bless and prayer as comfort, the weak, sick, lacking true good-doers with no blame, and the weepers whose tearful faith is honored. The same names that made the mountain a cloud and levelled it, that made the sea a road, now hold hope for the mixed.\n\nA common error despairs mixed deeds as equally doomed. The verse says mixed is not void but hoped for turning, distinguishing it from the mastered hypocrisy punished twice then tremendous and from the everlasting Gardens of the foremost. The next verses will show the way to turn: taking from their wealth charity to purify and bless and praying for them as comfort, for Allah alone accepts repentance and receives charity, He alone is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful.",
  "103": "“Take from their wealth ˹O Prophet˺ charity to purify and bless them, and pray for them— surely your prayer is a source of comfort for them. And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” gives the practical means for the mixed who confessed and hope to be turned to. The previous verse left them in hopeful suspension, mixed good with evil, right to hope Allah will turn, Forgiving, Merciful. This verse says “Take from their wealth O Prophet charity to purify and bless them, and pray for them—surely your prayer is a source of comfort for them” or “khuth min amwalihim sadaqatan tutahhiruhum wa tuzakkeehim biha wa salli alaihim inna salataka sakanun lahum.” The three acts: take sadaqah, charity, the same alms whose distribution is only for eight, whose criticism pleases or enrages hypocrites, whose hoarding brands foreheads, whose spending to hinder becomes regret; purify, or “tutahhiruhum,” cleanse, like rain purifying from Satan’s whispers and firming steps at Badr, like the Book’s guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe of the Tablets; bless, or “tuzakkeehim,” to grow, to increase, the same zakat root that makes the land produce abundantly by the Lord’s leave for the thankful, the opposite of hardly producing for the infertile.\n\nThe command “and pray for them—surely your prayer is a source of comfort for them” or “wa salli alaihim inna salataka sakanun lahum” makes the Prophet’s salat, or prayer, a “sakan,” tranquillity, like the sakeenah sent upon Messenger and believers at Hunain and in the cave, and the shelter when few and oppressed, the same shelter that will be Gardens where tranquillity is rivers flow. The Prophet’s prayer comforts the mixed, as his ear is good for you, his listening is mercy for believers, and his hurt brings painful punishment, but his prayer brings comfort.\n\nThe names “And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” or “wa Allahu Samee‘un Aleem” close, as with the hypocrites’ secret and private counsel and Knower of all unseen, and the “Who has forbidden adornments” question, and the “Do they not know Allah knows their thoughts” warning. He hears the confession mixed and the prayer, and knows the true good to make hear, the weakness to lighten, the market to deter, the hearts to unite when all riches could not.\n\nA common error thinks charity is mere finance. The verse shows it is purification and growth, the mechanism by which the mixed can move from “right to hope” to the foremost’s Gardens, where the bounty is from Him if He wills, who is All-Knowing, All-Wise. For the inheritors who claimed “We will be forgiven” while seizing lowly gain, taking the best of the Tablets, firmly abiding, and establishing prayer where reward is never discounted is the alternative that, with charity taken and prayer given, leads to the Knower of seen and unseen informing favorably.",
  "104": "“Do they not know that Allah alone accepts the repentance of His servants and receives ˹their˺ charity, and that Allah alone is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful?” follows the command to take charity to purify and pray for comfort, clarifying to whom repentance and charity go. The previous verse said take from their wealth sadaqah to purify and bless and pray for them, your prayer is comfort, All-Hearing, All-Knowing. This verse asks “Do they not know that Allah alone accepts the repentance of His servants and receives their charity, and that Allah alone is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful?” or “a-lam ya‘lamoo anna Allaha huwa yaqbalu al-tawbata an ibadihi wa ya’khuthu al-sadaqati wa anna Allaha huwa al-Tawwabu al-Raheem.” The question “Do they not know” echoes the earlier “Do they not know that Allah knows their secret and private counsel and is Knower of all unseen” and “Do they not know that whoever opposes will be in Hell forever, ultimate disgrace,” showing knowledge of Allah’s attributes is the remedy for hypocrisy, hoarding, and withholding.\n\nThe first clause “Allah alone accepts the repentance of His servants and receives their charity” means acceptance is His alone, not the Prophet’s alone, not the alms critic’s pleasure, not the hoarder’s market, but the Lord who destines, who knows hidden in hearts, who is never absent, who wrote twelve months in the Record, who created heavens and earth in six Days. The Prophet takes charity, but Allah receives it, the same receiving that makes spending in His cause paid in full without wrong, that makes the fifth for orphans, poor, travellers honourable, and that makes the donation of nomads who believe as qurbah bringing closer and prayers of Messenger.\n\nThe names “Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful” or “al-Tawwab al-Raheem” are the same that made the primordial testimony return possible, that turned to the mixed who confessed, that made the seventy who were seized by earthquake forgiven when Moses pleaded, that made the calf worshippers’ remorse lead to “If He does not have mercy we will be losers” and found Him All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nA common misunderstanding directs repentance to people’s opinion. The verse directs to Allah alone, whose mercy encompasses everything and whose punishment is severe for the persistent. The next verse will say “Do as you will, your deeds will be observed by Allah, Messenger, and believers, and you will be returned to the Knower, He will inform you,” showing observation is tripartite but acceptance is His alone, inviting the mixed, the reallocated sacred months whose evil deeds are made appealing, and the wealth-impressed to turn to the Accepter before the deeds observed become the information that condemns, and before the building founded on fear and pleasure of Allah is compared to the crumbling cliff.",
  "105": "“Tell ˹them, O Prophet˺, ‘Do as you will. Your deeds will be observed by Allah, His Messenger, and the believers. And you will be returned to the Knower of the seen and unseen, then He will inform you of what you used to do’” places the mixed who confessed, the mastered hypocrites, and the foremost on a common watch, after clarifying that Allah alone accepts repentance and receives charity as the Accepter, Most Merciful. The command “Tell them, Do as you will. Your deeds will be observed by Allah, His Messenger, and the believers” or “wa qul i‘maloo fa-sa-yara Allahu amalakum wa rasooluhu wa al-mu’minoon” uses “i‘maloo,” to work, to do, the same “each will have ranks according to what they did, your Lord is not unaware” and “Observe what was the end of the corruptors.” Observation is by three witnesses: Allah, who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing, never absent, who knows sirr and najwa and is Knower of all unseen, who hears the weeping for no mounts and the hoarded gold’s brand; His Messenger, the ear of good who believes in Allah and has faith in believers and whose hurt brings painful punishment; and the believers, the guardians of one another who encourage good, forbid evil, establish, pay, obey, and are shown mercy, the true witnesses like the Heights who recognize by marks.\n\nThe ultimate return “And you will be returned to the Knower of the seen and unseen, then He will inform you of what you used to do” or “wa sa-turaddoona ila Aalimi al-ghaybi wa al-shahadati fa-yunabbi’ukum bima kuntum ta‘maloon” repeats the earlier “Your future deeds will be observed by Allah and His Messenger as well, and you will be returned to the Knower, He will inform you” and the “We will give them a full account with sure knowledge, for We were never absent” and the weighing just where heavy succeed and light doom themselves. The seen, or “shahadah,” includes the overt march whether easy or difficult and the overt spending to hinder, while the unseen, or “ghayb,” includes the doubt that tears, the secret that Allah knows, the wealth children that torment, the heart between which Allah stands.\n\nThis verse links the alms criticism and the “right to hope” for the mixed to a final accountability: deeds now observed will later be informed, whether they are hoarding to brand, reallocating sacred months as increase in disbelief, or firmly abiding where reward is never discounted.\n\nA common error thinks unobserved deeds are safe. The verse says three watch now, and the Knower will inform later, so the mindful granted furqan will choose deeds that please Allah and Messenger and believers, the gracious, enjoining, turning from the ignorant, seeking refuge, remembering and seeing clearly, whose building is founded on fear and pleasure of Allah, not on the crumbling cliff that tumbles into Fire, whose prayer in the mosque founded on righteousness from the first day is more worthy and loves purification, whom Allah loves.",
  "106": "“And some others are left for Allah’s decision, either to punish them or turn to them in mercy. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” describes the third group among the lagging, after the mixed who confessed and were hoped to be turned to and the mastered hypocrites who will be punished twice then tremendous. The previous verses said do as you will, deeds observed by Allah, Messenger, and believers, and you will be returned to the Knower who will inform you. This verse says “And some others are left for Allah’s decision, either to punish them or turn to them in mercy” or “wa akharoona murjawna li-amri Allahi imma yu‘aththibuhum wa imma yatoobu alaihim.” These are the three who remained behind without hypocrisy but without excuse, the same three later named in the verse “And Allah has also turned in mercy to the three who had remained behind, whose guilt distressed them until the earth, despite its vastness, seemed to close in on them,” the well-known companions Ka‘b ibn Malik, Hilal ibn Umayyah, and Murarah ibn al-Rabi‘ whose story is the earth closing after Ḥunain’s earth closed. They were not the rich who asked to be left, nor the weeping poor with no mounts, but sincere believers who lagged without preparation and without lie.\n\nThe phrase “left for Allah’s decision” or “murjawna li-amri Allah” means deferred, suspended, waiting His command, as earlier “If you fear poverty, Allah will enrich out of His bounty if He wills” and “We will punish them twice then tremendous.” Their affair is not with the Prophet’s ear alone but with Allah’s will, the same will that gave the fourth month travel, the same will that would enrich, the same will that distinguishes the worst deaf and dumb who will never believe.\n\nThe names “And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “wa Allahu Aleemun Hakeem” close, as with the alms for eight as obligation from All-Knowing, All-Wise, the twelve-month Record, and the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful, showing the decision will be with knowledge of the goodness that would make hearing possible and with wisdom that knows the appointed term that cannot be delayed.\n\nA common misunderstanding rushes to judge the deferred. The verse holds them in suspense, mirroring the Heights’ suspense where they had not yet entered Paradise but eagerly hoped, teaching the community to leave judgment to the Knower while remaining firm themselves, as earlier the sincere were told to be gracious, enjoin right, and turn from the ignorant and to seek refuge when Satan tempts.",
  "107": "“There are also those ˹hypocrites˺ who set up a mosque ˹only˺ to cause harm, promote disbelief, divide the believers, and as a base for those who had previously fought against Allah and His Messenger. They will definitely swear, ‘We intended nothing but good,’ but Allah bears witness that they are surely liars” exposes the mosque of harm, and the commentators identify its builders and the occasion of the revelation. The verse describes the deed: “There are also those hypocrites who set up a mosque only to cause harm, promote disbelief, divide the believers, and as a base for those who had previously fought against Allah and His Messenger,” or “wa allatheena ittakhathoo masjidan diraran wa kufran wa tafreeqan bayna al-mu’mineena wa irsadan li-man haraba Allaha wa rasoolahu min qabl,” and the commentators identify the mosque as the Masjid ad-Dirar, the mosque of opposition, which a group of the hypocrites of Medina had built, and the four motives that the verse names are the four purposes of its construction: the “dirar,” the harm, the injury intended against the mosque of the Prophet; the “kufr,” the disbelief, the strengthening of the unbelief; the “tafreeq,” the division, the splitting of the believers into the two congregations; and the “irsad,” the base, the station prepared for the one who had fought God and His Messenger before — and the commentators identify that one as Abu ‘Amir, the monk known as Abu ‘Amir ar-Rahib, who had opposed the Prophet and who had sent word to the hypocrites to build the mosque as a base for his return with the armies of the Romans. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account: a group of twelve hypocrites built the mosque at Quba, near the mosque of the Prophet, and they came to the Prophet asking him to pray in it, and the verse descended exposing their purpose, and the Prophet commanded its destruction, and the mosque was burned and razed, and the place became a refuse dump.\n\nThe verse then records the oath of the builders: “They will definitely swear, ‘We intended nothing but good,’ but Allah bears witness that they are surely liars,” or “wa la-yahlifunna in aradna illa al-husna wallahu yashhadu innahum la-kathiboon.” The commentators note the oath, the “halif,” the swearing of the builders that they had intended nothing but the good, the “husna,” the claim that the mosque was for the prayer of the weak and the sick and the rainy nights, and the verse answers the oath with the divine witness: “Allah bears witness that they are surely liars,” the “shahadah” of the Lord against the false oath, and the commentators observe the force of the answer, for the oath of the hypocrites was the oath of the falsehood, and the witness of the Lord was the witness of the truth, and the verse places the two side by side so that the believer may know which to trust. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the false piety, the deed that is dressed in the garments of the worship and is, in its heart, the harm and the division, and the oath that covers the falsehood with the claim of the good, and the divine witness that strips the cover and exposes the lie. The verse is the exposure of the mosque of harm, and the warning against the deed that the tongue beautifies and the heart betrays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the intention, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own works. The believer is taught that the outward form of the deed is not the measure of the deed, and the mosque that is built for the harm is a mosque that the Lord exposes, whatever the oath of its builders, for the intention is the soul of the work, and the work that is offered with the false intention is the work that the Lord rejects. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the first of the four verses of the mosque of harm, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the sincerity: the deed is weighed by its purpose, and the purpose that is false is the deed that the Lord witnesses against, and the oath of the false is the oath that the witness of the Lord refutes. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the mosque of harm: the four motives named, the oath recorded, and the witness declared — the exposure of the false piety, and the warning against the deed that the tongue beautifies.",
  "108": "“Do not ˹O Prophet˺ ever pray in it. Certainly, a mosque founded on righteousness from the first day is more worthy of your prayers. In it are men who love to be purified. And Allah loves those who purify themselves” commands the Prophet against the mosque of harm and points to the mosque of righteousness, and the commentators note the two mosques and the contrast. The verse opens with the command: “Do not O Prophet ever pray in it,” or “la taqum feehi abadan,” the prohibition of the prayer in the mosque of harm, the “la taqum” — do not stand, do not pray — and the word “abadan,” ever, the absoluteness of the prohibition, and the commentators note the significance of the command, for the prayer of the Prophet in the mosque would have been the seal of its legitimacy, and the prohibition was the withdrawal of the seal, the declaration that the mosque of harm was no mosque at all. Ibn Kathir explains the command: the Prophet was forbidden to stand in the mosque of the hypocrites, and the prohibition was the divine verdict upon the building, the stripping of the false mosque of the very thing that would have made it a mosque, the prayer of the messenger of God.\n\nThe verse then points to the true mosque: “Certainly, a mosque founded on righteousness from the first day is more worthy of your prayers,” or “la-masjidun ussisa ‘ala at-taqwa min awwali yawmin ahaqqu an taqooma feehi,” and the commentators identify it as the mosque of Quba, the mosque that the Prophet had built on the first day of his arrival in Medina, the mosque that was founded upon the “taqwa,” the mindfulness, from its first stone, and the verse declares it the worthier place of the prayer, the “ahaqq,” the more deserving, and the contrast between the two mosques is the verse’s lesson, for the one was founded on the harm and the other on the mindfulness, and the prayer belongs to the second. The verse then describes the men of the true mosque: “In it are men who love to be purified. And Allah loves those who purify themselves,” or “feehi rijalun yuhibboona an yatatahharoo wallahu yuhibbu al-mutahhireen,” the men who love the purification, the “tatahhur,” the cleanliness of the body and the soul, and the commentators relate the detail: the men of Quba were known for their washing with the water after the relief, the care of the cleanliness that the verse records, and the verse was revealed commending them, and the closing declaration — “Allah loves those who purify themselves” — is the praise of the purity that the true mosque contained. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the measure of the mosque: the true mosque is the mosque of the taqwa and the purity, and the false mosque is the mosque of the harm and the division, and the prayer of the believer belongs to the first and not to the second, and the love of the Lord is for the ones who purify themselves. The verse is the contrast of the two mosques, and the teaching of the worth of the pure.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the foundation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own works. The believer is taught that the work is judged by its foundation, the “ta’sis,” the first stone, the original intention, and the mosque that is founded upon the mindfulness is the mosque that the Lord loves, while the mosque that is founded upon the harm is the mosque that the Lord rejects, and the two foundations are the two destinies of every work, the foundation of the taqwa and the foundation of the harm. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the second of the four verses of the mosque of harm, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the foundation: the worth of the work is the worth of its first day, and the one who founds the work upon the mindfulness has founded it upon the love of the Lord, and the one who founds it upon the harm has founded it upon the rejection. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two mosques: the prayer forbidden in the one, the worth declared of the other, and the purity loved — the contrast of the foundations, and the summons to the mosque of the taqwa.",
  "109": "“Which is better: those who laid the foundation of their building on the fear and pleasure of Allah, or those who did so on the edge of a crumbling cliff that tumbled down with them into the Fire of Hell? And Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people” poses the question of the two foundations, and the commentators note the simile and its force. The verse asks the question: “Which is better: those who laid the foundation of their building on the fear and pleasure of Allah, or those who did so on the edge of a crumbling cliff that tumbled down with them into the Fire of Hell?” or “a-faman assasa bunyanahu ‘ala taqwa mina Allahi wa ridwanin khayrun am man assasa bunyanahu ‘ala shafa jurufin harin fanhara bihi fee nari jahannama,” and the commentators explain the two sides: the first is the one who founds his building upon the “taqwa,” the fear of God, and the “ridwan,” the pleasure of God, the foundation of the mindfulness and the divine approval, and the second is the one who founds his building upon the “shafa jurufin har,” the edge of a crumbling cliff, the bank of the torrent that is undermined and collapsing, and the second building “tumbled down with him into the Fire of Hell,” the “inhara,” the collapse of the cliff and the building and the builder into the Fire, and the simile is the verse’s portrait of the false mosque and its builders. Ibn Kathir explains the image: the mosque of the hypocrites, founded upon the harm and the division, was like the building on the edge of the crumbling bank, and its collapse was the collapse of the builder with it, and the question of the verse is the question whose answer is self-evident, for the building of the fear and the pleasure is the building that stands, and the building of the crumbling edge is the building that falls.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the word “jurufin har,” the crumbling cliff, the undermined bank, and its meaning. The bank that is washed by the torrent from beneath is the bank that appears firm and is hollow, and the building upon it is the building that stands upon the hidden collapse, and the verse likens the false foundation to this, for the mosque of the harm was built upon the hidden intention of the harm, and the hidden intention was the undermining that the appearance concealed, and the collapse of the mosque was the exposure of the hidden. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the truth of the foundation: the work that is founded upon the fear and the pleasure of God is the work that stands in both worlds, and the work that is founded upon the crumbling edge is the work that falls with its builder into the Fire, and the two foundations are the two destinies that the question places before the believer. The verse closes with the declaration: “And Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people,” or “wallahu la yahdi al-qawma az-zalimeen,” the “zalimeen,” the wrongdoers, the ones who founded their building upon the harm, and the non-guidance of the wrongdoers is the seal of the question, for the one who chooses the crumbling edge has chosen the path that the guidance does not accompany.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the choice, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own foundations. The believer is taught that every work is a building, and every building has a foundation, and the foundation is the intention, and the intention is the choice between the fear and the pleasure of God on the one hand and the crumbling edge on the other, and the one who founds his works upon the taqwa has founded them upon the eternal, while the one who founds them upon the harm has founded them upon the collapse. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the third of the four verses of the mosque of harm, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the foundation: the question of the two buildings is the question of every soul, and the answer is the choice that the foundation reveals. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two foundations: the fear and the pleasure against the crumbling edge, and the Fire of the collapse — the question that the believer must answer, and the warning against the foundation that falls.",
  "110": "“The building which they erected will never cease to fuel hypocrisy in their hearts until their hearts are torn apart. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” records the verdict upon the building and its builders, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the statement of the lasting effect: “The building which they erected will never cease to fuel hypocrisy in their hearts until their hearts are torn apart,” or “la yazalu bunyanuhumu allathee banaw reebatan fee quloobihim illa an tuqatta‘a quloobuhum,” the “reebah,” the doubt, the hypocrisy, the suspicion that the building would continue to fuel in the hearts of its builders, the persistence of the disease that the mosque had planted, and the commentators explain the meaning: the mosque of harm, even after its destruction, remained a source of the doubt and the hypocrisy in the hearts of those who had built it, for the exposure of their plot had not cleansed them but deepened their resentment, and the building would continue to fuel the “reebah” until the hearts were “torn apart,” the “taqatta‘a,” the cutting, the severing, and the commentators differ on the meaning of the tearing: some say it is the tearing of the death, the end of the hearts in the death of the builders, and others say it is the tearing of the repentance, the breaking of the hearts in the remorse that the truth might yet bring, and Ibn Kathir records both, and the verse leaves the meaning open, for the tearing is the end of the disease, whether by the death or by the repentance. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the persistence of the sin’s effect: the building of the harm is not a deed that ends with the destruction of the building, for the deed leaves its mark in the heart, and the mark of the harm is the doubt that fuels until the heart is torn, and the one who would be free of the doubt must be free of the deed that plants it.\n\nThe verse closes with the two names: “And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise,” or “wallahu ‘aleemun hakeem,” the All-Knowing, who knew the intentions of the builders and the hidden purpose of the building, and the All-Wise, whose command to destroy the mosque and whose exposure of the builders were the acts of the perfect wisdom, and the commentators note the placement of the names, for the verse that began with the exposure of the harm ends with the knowledge and the wisdom that governed the exposure, and the believer is taught to trust the judgment of the One who knows the hearts and judges them with the wisdom that the verse declares. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the account of the mosque of harm, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the hidden: the building that is founded upon the harm is the building that the Lord exposes, and the exposure is the mercy of the One who knows and the wisdom of the One who judges. The verse is the seal of the account, and the warning against the deed that the heart conceals.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the lasting mark, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own deeds. The believer is taught that the deed leaves its trace in the heart, and the trace of the good is the light that grows, and the trace of the evil is the doubt that fuels, and the one who sows the harm reaps the doubt, the “reebah” that the verse names, and the reaping is the harvest that the heart carries until its tearing. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the fourth of the four verses of the mosque of harm, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the deed: the building of the harm fuels the doubt, and the doubt is the disease that the heart carries, and the healing is the tearing that the death or the repentance brings. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the lasting mark: the building fueling, the hearts torn, and the knowledge and the wisdom declared — the warning against the deed that leaves the doubt, and the summons to the deed that leaves the light.",
  "111": "“Allah has indeed purchased from the believers their lives and wealth in exchange for Paradise. They fight in the cause of Allah and kill or are killed. This is a true promise binding on Him in the Torah, the Gospel, and the Quran. And whose promise is truer than Allah’s? So rejoice in the exchange you have made with Him. That is ˹truly˺ the ultimate triumph” presents the transaction that succeeds the tearing hearts of the harm builders. The phrase “Allah has indeed purchased from the believers their lives and wealth in exchange for Paradise” or “inna Allaha ishtara mina al-mu’mineena anfusahum wa amwalahum bi-anna lahum al-jannah” uses “ishtara,” to buy, a commercial image like spending to hinder that becomes regret, like hoarding gold to be heated, like the lowly gain seized claiming “We will be forgiven,” like the alms for eight as obligation, but here the seller is the believer and the buyer is Allah, the price is Paradise, the same Gardens under rivers forever, splendid homes, pleasure greatest, honourable provision, elevated ranks, the ultimate triumph. Lives and wealth are the same lives and wealth that are a test, the same beloveds more dear that must not outweigh Allah, the same wealth and lives with which the Prophet motivates to fight, twenty steadfast overcoming two hundred, and the same “March forth whether easy or difficult and strive with wealth and lives, that is best if only you knew.”\n\nThe description “They fight in the cause of Allah and kill or are killed” or “yuqaatiloona fi sabeeli Allahi fa-yaqtuloona wa yuqtaloon” is the fighting until no persecution and devotion entirely for Allah, the firmness when facing the enemy, the remembrance often, the obedience without dispute, the perseverance with Allah, the deterrent preparation what you can.\n\nThe guarantee “This is a true promise binding on Him in the Torah, the Gospel, and the Quran. And whose promise is truer than Allah’s?” or “wa‘dan alaihi haqqan fil-Tawrati wa al-Injeeli wa al-Qur’an wa man awfa bi-‘ahdihi mina Allahi” ties the three Books, showing continuity from Tablets fundamentals to the light perfected, the Word perfected, the Book explained with knowledge, the promise that is never changed, the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful and good.\n\nThe call “So rejoice in the exchange you have made with Him. That is truly the ultimate triumph” or “fa-istabshiroo bi-bay‘ikumu allathee baya‘tum bihi wa thalika huwa al-fawzu al-azeem” mirrors the good news of Gardens with everlasting bliss and the pleasure of Allah, the ultimate triumph, the Gardens prepared for the Messenger and believers who strived with wealth and lives will have all the best and be successful.\n\nA common error counts the price as loss. The Quran calls it purchase, rejoice, ultimate triumph, the fertile abundant, the everlasting, far better than the little enjoyment of this world, the little laugh that will be wept much, the hoarded heated. For the inheritors who seized lowly gain, the exchange is the alternative: sell lives and wealth to Him for Paradise, not to the hoard.",
  "112": "“˹It is the believers˺ who repent, who are devoted to worship, who praise ˹their Lord˺, who fast, who bow down and prostrate themselves, who encourage good and forbid evil, and who observe the limits set by Allah. And give good news to the believers” lists the traits of the sellers whose lives and wealth are purchased for Paradise. The previous verse said Allah purchased lives and wealth for Paradise, they fight and kill or are killed, a true promise in Torah, Gospel, Quran, truer than any, rejoice, ultimate triumph. This verse says who they are: “who repent, who are devoted to worship, who praise their Lord, who fast, who bow down and prostrate themselves, who encourage good and forbid evil, and who observe the limits set by Allah” or “al-ta’iboona al-aabidoona al-hamidoona al-sa’ihoona al-raki‘oona al-sajidoona al-aamiroona bil-ma‘roofi wa al-nahoona ani al-munkari wa al-hafizoona li-hudoodi Allahi.” Each trait mirrors earlier praises: repent, or “ta’iboon,” like the three who remained behind whose earth closed and souls torn and who knew no refuge except in Him, then He turned so they might repent, and like “We will turn in grace to whoever He wills,” the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful; devoted to worship, or “aabidoon,” like the angels nearest not too proud to worship, glorifying and prostrating; praise, or “hamidoon,” like the Gardens’ removed bitterness and “Praise be to Allah who guided us”; fast, or “sa’ihoon,” wanderers, travelers, fasters who discipline; bowing and prostrating, or “raki‘oon, sajidoon,” like the Messenger and believers who strived and the firm abiders who establish prayer; encouraging good and forbidding evil, or “aamiroona bil-ma‘roofi,” the guardians of one another who encourage good, forbid evil, establish prayer, pay zakat, obey, shown mercy, Almighty, All-Wise; observing limits, or “hafizoona li-hudoodi Allah,” the limits, or “hudood,” less likely known by nomads far worse in disbelief, the twelve months in the Record, the sacred four, the alms for eight, the fifth, the inheritance for arham, the limits the reallocators violated as increase in disbelief and the hoarders consumed wrongfully.\n\nThe command “And give good news to the believers” or “wa bashshiri al-mu’mineen” is the same basharah for those who believe, for whom this Quran is insight, guide, and mercy, the same good news to those who do not believe of painful punishment is reversed.\n\nA common misunderstanding narrows the sold lives to battlefield only. The list shows the sold life is lived as repentance, worship, praise, fasting, bowing, prostrating, enjoining, forbidding, guarding limits, the whole straight path from firm abiding to listening attentively and silently so mercy is shown and remembering inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness, whose building is on taqwa and ridwan, not on crumbling cliff.",
  "113": "“It is not ˹proper˺ for the Prophet and the believers to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they were close relatives, after it has become clear to the believers that they are bound for the Hellfire” limits the prayer for forgiveness after the good news to the repenting, worshipping, praising, fasting, bowing, prostrating, enjoining, forbidding, limit-observing believers. The next verse will explain Abraham’s father prayer was a promise then broken when enmity was clear. This verse says “It is not proper for the Prophet and the believers to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they were close relatives, after it has become clear to the believers that they are bound for the Hellfire” or “ma kana li-nabiyyi wa allatheena amanoo an yastaghfiroo lil-mushrikeen wa law kanoo oolee qurba min ba‘di ma tabayyana lahum annahum as-habu al-jaheem.” The prohibition uses “ma kana,” it was not, not proper, like “It is not for polytheists to maintain mosques while they profess disbelief, their deeds void” and “It is not proper for the Prophet to take captives until thoroughly subdued.” Seeking forgiveness, or “yastaghfiroo,” is the prayer of the most merciful of the merciful, the seventy times that will never forgive the rebellious, the comfort prayer for the mixed, the “If our Lord does not have mercy and forgive we will be losers,” but here it is barred for polytheists after it has become clear they are “as-habu al-jaheem,” companions of Hellfire, the same Fire that is bed and flames as cover, everlasting stay sufficient for hypocrites, branding, ultimate disgrace.\n\nThe phrase “even if they were close relatives” or “wa law kanoo oolee qurba” tests the earlier inheritance for arham and the beloveds more beloved than Allah where parents, children, siblings, spouses, family, wealth, trade, homes rival love, and the “Do not take your parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief, they are true wrongdoers” where kinship guardianship ends at disbelief. Close kinship does not override the fire companionship once clarity has come, as with Noah’s and Lut’s wives among the doomed despite being family of prophets.\n\nThis connects to the previous good news to the believers whose limits are observed and to the next Abraham example, where his promise to pray for his father was kept until enmity was clear, then he broke ties, tender-hearted, forbearing but firm.\n\nA common error prays for the polytheist dead as if love of kin could override Hellfire companionship. The verse says after clarity, seeking forgiveness is not proper, because the most beautiful names are to call upon, but abuse of names includes praying for those who opposed Allah and Messenger to be in Hell forever, ultimate disgrace, whose trial would not only affect wrongdoers and whose spending to hinder is heaped for Hell, the true losers.",
  "114": "“As for Abraham’s prayer for his father’s forgiveness, it was only in fulfilment of a promise he had made to him. But when it became clear to Abraham that his father was an enemy of Allah, he broke ties with him. Abraham was truly tender-hearted, forbearing” explains the exception that might seem to contradict the prohibition just given, using the earlier Abraham father story. The previous verse said it is not proper for the Prophet and believers to seek forgiveness for polytheists, even close relatives, after it is clear they are companions of Hellfire. This verse says “As for Abraham’s prayer for his father’s forgiveness, it was only in fulfilment of a promise he had made to him” or “wa ma kana istighfaru Ibraheema li-abeehi illa an maw‘idatin wa‘adaha iyyah.” The promise is the one mentioned when Abraham said to his father Azar, who took idols as gods while being in clear error, that he would pray for forgiveness, a promise made before the father’s enmity was fully clear, similar to the Prophet’s pardon “Why did you give them permission before the truthful were distinguished” where permission was given before distinction.\n\nThe sequel “But when it became clear to Abraham that his father was an enemy of Allah, he broke ties with him” or “fa-lamma tabayyana lahu annahu aduwwun lillahi tabarra’a minhu” uses “tabayyana,” to become clear, as earlier “after it has become clear that they are companions of Hellfire,” showing the same criterion. “Tabarra’a,” to disavow, to free oneself, is the same bara’ah as the discharge from obligations to polytheists who violate every time, the same “free of the polytheists” announced on the day of greater pilgrimage, the same “brave” of Lot and Shu‘ayb who turned away and said their people like sincere advisors were not loved. Bara’ah is the opposite of guardianship of believers for one another and of parents as allies if they choose disbelief, the latter being wrongdoers.\n\nThe character note “Abraham was truly tender-hearted, forbearing” or “inna Ibraheema la-awwahun haleem” describes him as “awwah,” one who sighs, tender, soft-hearted, and “haleem,” forbearing, clement, not harsh, as earlier his argument against his father was gentle despite clear error, and as the Prophet is concerned by your suffering, anxious for well-being, gracious and merciful to believers. Tenderness does not prevent bara’ah when enmity is clear.\n\nA common misunderstanding cites Abraham’s prayer to justify seeking forgiveness for polytheists generally. The verse limits it to the promise before clarity, after clarity he disavowed, showing the rule stands: after clarity of Hellfire companionship, seeking forgiveness is not proper. For the forbearing who maintain mosques only by belief, prayer, zakat, and fear of Allah alone, Abraham’s tenderness and forbearingness model the balance: pray for the father while hope remains, but when enmity is clear, break ties, keep the building on taqwa, not on the crumbling cliff.",
  "115": "“Allah would never consider a people deviant after He has guided them, until He makes clear to them what they must avoid. Surely Allah has ˹full˺ knowledge of everything” states the principle behind Abraham’s disavowal timing and the Prophet’s pardon for giving permission. The previous verse excused Abraham’s father prayer as a promise fulfilled, then disavowal when enmity became clear, describing him as tender-hearted, forbearing. This verse says “Allah would never consider a people deviant after He has guided them, until He makes clear to them what they must avoid” or “wa ma kana Allahu li-yudilla qawman ba‘da ith hadahum hatta yubayyina lahum ma yattaqoon.” The word “yudilla,” to lead astray, to consider deviant, is the same deviant who clung to earth following desires like a dog panting and the people who deny signs, whose deeds become void, and the hypocrites whose hearts are sealed. The condition “until He makes clear to them what they must avoid” or “hatta yubayyina lahum ma yattaqoon” is the bayan, clarification, that makes the path perfectly straight visible, the varying of lessons for the thankful, the fundamentals on the Tablets, the alms for eight, the sacred months as twelve in the Record, the hunters’ “Am I not your Lord?” testimony, the mountain covenant, the discharge’s four months, the protection so they may hear the Word.\n\nThis principle explains why the three who remained behind were left for Allah’s decision either to punish or turn in mercy, All-Knowing, All-Wise, and why some mixed who confessed were hoped to be turned to, and why the hypocrites’ building fuelled hypocrisy until hearts are torn, All-Knowing, All-Wise. Guidance is not considered deviation until the “ma yattaqoon,” what to be mindful of, has been clarified, like the true prohibitions “only open and secret indecencies, sin, aggression, shirk without sultan, and saying about Allah what you do not know,” and like the five for maintaining mosques.\n\nThe seal “Surely Allah has full knowledge of everything” or “inna Allaha bi-kulli shay’in aleem” is the All-Knowing who knows hidden in hearts, between person and heart, the goodness to make hear, the weakness to lighten, the unseen hour’s tremendous heaviness, the most beautiful names, and the unseen thoughts and secret talks, Knower of all unseen. Knowing everything, He makes clear before considering deviant.\n\nA common error accuses the community of deviation before clarification. The verse says deviation after guidance is only after bayan of what to be mindful of, the same bayan that is the varying after which perhaps they will return, the same bayan that the discharge made to the polytheists before it was said travel four months. For the heedless like cattle, the bayan is already the Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy, insight, so perhaps they will reflect, not be like those who do not reflect on wonders and think the fellow is mad.",
  "116": "“Indeed, to Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. He gives life and causes death. And besides Allah you have no guardian or helper” anchors the bayan principle in absolute Lordship after stating Allah would never consider a people deviant after guiding until He makes clear what they must avoid. The phrase “Indeed, to Allah alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. He gives life and causes death” or “inna Allaha lahu mulku al-samawati wal-ardi yuhyee wa yumeet” repeats the kingdom, or “mulk,” earlier “To Him belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth, there is no god worthy of worship except Him, He gives life and causes death” when calling humanity to the unlettered Prophet, the same kingdom that the heavens and earth in six Days and the Throne, the night and day overlapping, sun, moon, stars subjected, winds producing fruit, and the twelve-month Record all demonstrate. Giving life and causing death is the same “We brought you into being, you will be brought to life again” uprightness and the same raising of the dead like fruit, the same taking of souls beating faces and backs to taste burning, and the same “There you will live, there you will die, from there you will be resurrected.”\n\nThe consequence “And besides Allah you have no guardian or helper” or “wa ma lakum min dooni Allahi min waliyyin wa la naseer” negates other guardians, or “wali,” besides Him, the same warning not to take parents and siblings as allies if they choose disbelief, not to take trusted allies other than Allah, His Messenger, or believers, and the same guardianship that disbelievers have of one another leading to great oppression if believers do not. Helper, or “naseer,” the same victory only from Almighty, All-Wise, the same Protector who revealed the Book, excellent protector and helper for those who firmly abide, the same “Allah is sufficient for you and for the believers who follow you” and “Allah will enrich you out of His bounty, if He wills.” No wali or naseer besides Him means the three deferred, the mixed who confessed, and the foremost will all return to Him who has full knowledge and who is All-Knowing, All-Wise, to be informed.\n\nA common misunderstanding seeks guardianship in treaties that violate every time, in rabbis taken as lords, in hoarded gold that will brand, or in wealth and children that torment. The verse says kingdom, life, death, guardianship, and help belong to Him alone, the One whose light He perfects even to disbelievers’ dismay and whose religion of truth He makes prevail, so the mindful granted furqan will keep to the straight path while the rebellious who reallocate sacred months and consume wrongfully remain like cattle even less guided until their hearts are torn.",
  "117": "“Allah has certainly turned in mercy to the Prophet as well as the Emigrants and the Helpers who stood by him in the time of hardship, after the hearts of a group of them had almost faltered. He then accepted their repentance. Surely He is Ever Gracious and Most Merciful to them” announces the acceptance of the faithful of Tabuk, and the commentators note the occasion and the three groups. The verse states the acceptance: “Allah has certainly turned in mercy to the Prophet as well as the Emigrants and the Helpers who stood by him in the time of hardship,” or “laqad taba Allahu ‘ala an-nabiyyi wal-muhajireena wal-ansari allatheena ittaba‘oohu fee sa‘ati al-‘usrah,” the “tawbah” of the Lord upon the Prophet and upon the emigrants and the helpers who followed him in the “sa‘at al-‘usrah,” the hour of the hardship, and the commentators identify the hardship as the expedition of Tabuk, the march to the far north in the heat of the summer, at the time of the ripening of the fruits and the scarcity of the provision, and the expedition was called the “jaysh al-‘usrah,” the army of the hardship, for the difficulty of the march and the poverty of the time, and the verse records that the Lord turned in mercy upon the Prophet and upon the two groups — the “muhajireen,” the emigrants of Mecca, and the “ansar,” the helpers of Medina — who had stood by him in the hardship and marched with him despite it. Ibn Kathir explains the context: the expedition of Tabuk was the greatest of the trials of the community, the march into the distant land against the gathered armies of the Romans, and the believers who marched had spent their wealth and their strength, and the verse announces the acceptance of their devotion, the turning of the Lord upon the faithful of the hardship.\n\nThe verse then records the mercy that followed the near-faltering: “after the hearts of a group of them had almost faltered. He then accepted their repentance,” or “min ba‘di ma kada yazeeghu quloobu fareeqin minhum thumma taba ‘alayhim,” the hearts of a group had almost “zagh,” deviated, faltered, under the weight of the hardship, and the Lord accepted their repentance, the “tawbah” that the verse records, and the commentators note the honesty of the verse, for it does not conceal the near-faltering of the hearts but records it and records the mercy that followed it, and the two together teach the believer that the faltering of the heart is not the end, and that the Lord who accepts the repentance of the faltered is the Lord who turns in mercy upon the faithful. The verse closes with the two names: “Surely He is Ever Gracious and Most Merciful to them,” or “innahu bihim ra’oofun raheem,” the “ra’oof,” the Ever Gracious, the tender, and the “raheem,” the Most Merciful, and the commentators note the tenderness of the names, for the verse that records the hardship and the near-faltering ends with the tenderness and the mercy, and the believer is taught to trust the Lord who is gracious and merciful to the faithful of the hardship. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the announcement of the acceptance, and that the believer reads in it the comfort of the trial: the hardship is the test, the faltering is the human weakness, and the turning of the Lord is the mercy that the weakness invites, and the faithful of the hardship are the ones whom the Ever Gracious, the Most Merciful receives.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the three groups, and the commentators connect it to the three who remained behind, the subject of the next verse. The verse announces the acceptance of the Prophet and the emigrants and the helpers, the three groups of the faithful, and the next verse announces the acceptance of the three who remained, and the two announcements together are the complete account of the mercy of Tabuk, the mercy that encompassed the faithful of the march and the repentant of the delay. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the first of the two verses of the acceptance, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the mercy: the Lord turns upon the faithful, and the faithful are the ones who stood in the hardship, and the standing is the proof of the faith that the mercy receives. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the acceptance: the three groups named, the hardship recalled, and the mercy declared — the announcement of the turning of the Lord upon the faithful of the trial, and the tenderness of the Ever Gracious, the Most Merciful.",
  "118": "“And ˹Allah has also turned in mercy to˺ the three who had remained behind, ˹whose guilt distressed them˺ until the earth, despite its vastness, seemed to close in on them, and their souls were torn in anguish. They knew there was no refuge from Allah except in Him. Then He turned to them in mercy so that they might repent. Surely Allah is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful” records the acceptance of the three, and the commentators identify them and relate their story in full. The verse states the acceptance: “And Allah has also turned in mercy to the three who had remained behind,” or “wa ‘ala ath-thalathati allatheena khullifoo,” and the commentators identify the three as Ka‘b ibn Malik, Hilal ibn Umayyah, and Murarah ibn ar-Rabi‘, the three believers who had stayed behind from the expedition of Tabuk without the excuse of the hypocrites and without the lie of the false, and the verse records their trial and their acceptance, and the commentators note the significance of their story, for it is the Quran’s great account of the repentance that is tested and accepted. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account from the long hadith of Ka‘b ibn Malik: the three had stayed behind, and when the Prophet returned, they confessed the truth of their delay without excuse, and the Prophet commanded the community to boycott them, and the boycott lasted fifty nights, during which the three were cut off from the community and the greeting of the Prophet, and the trial of the separation was the test of their sincerity, and the verse records the distress that the separation brought.\n\nThe verse then describes the distress: “whose guilt distressed them until the earth, despite its vastness, seemed to close in on them, and their souls were torn in anguish,” or “hatta itha daqat ‘alayhim al-ardu bima rahubat wa daqat ‘alayhim anfusuhum,” the narrowing of the earth upon them despite its vastness, the “daqat,” the closing, the constriction, and the narrowing of their own souls, the anguish of the heart, and the commentators note the phrase, for it describes the condition of the repentant who finds no place in the world, the earth closed upon them and the soul closed within them, and the distress is the measure of the trial that the three endured. Then the confession: “They knew there was no refuge from Allah except in Him,” or “wa zannoo an la malja’a mina Allahi illa ilayhi,” the knowledge that the only refuge from the Lord is the Lord, the “malja’,” the shelter, and the commentators note the depth of the confession, for the three had exhausted every refuge and found none, and the finding of the none was the finding of the one, the refuge that is in the Lord alone. Then the acceptance: “Then He turned to them in mercy so that they might repent. Surely Allah is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful,” or “thumma taba ‘alayhim li-yatooboo inna Allaha huwa at-tawwabu ar-raheem,” the turning of the Lord in mercy, the “tawbah” of the Lord that preceded and enabled the “tawbah” of the three, and the commentators note the reciprocity, for the Lord turned first, and the turning of the Lord is the “Tawwab,” the Accepter of Repentance, whose acceptance is the cause of the repentance, and the verse closes with the two names that crown the story. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s great teaching on the repentance: the three confessed, were tested, were distressed, and were accepted, and the acceptance is the mercy that the sincerity invited, and the believer reads in it the truth of the return, for the one who turns to the Lord with the sincerity of the three is the one whom the Accepter of Repentance, the Most Merciful receives.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sincerity, and the commentators connect it to the example of the three for every repentant. The three had no excuse, and they spoke no lie, and their honesty was the seed of their acceptance, and the believer is taught by their story that the repentance is the honesty, the confession without the excuse and the return without the condition, and the one who repents with the truth of the three is the one whom the Lord turns upon with the mercy of the verse. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the second of the two verses of the acceptance, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the return: the earth may close upon the repentant, and the soul may be torn in the anguish, but the refuge is the Lord, and the Lord is the Accepter of Repentance, the Most Merciful. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the three: the guilt confessed, the earth closed, the refuge found, and the mercy turned — the story of the repentance that was tested and accepted, and the assurance of the return that is never closed.",
  "119": "“O believers! Be mindful of Allah and be with the truthful” is the ethic that follows the mercy turned to the Prophet, the Emigrants and Helpers in hard hour, and the three whose earth closed until they knew no refuge except in Him. The previous verse detailed the three’s anguish and that He turned so they might repent, Accepter, Most Merciful. This verse says “O believers! Be mindful of Allah and be with the truthful” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo ittaqoo Allaha wa koonoo ma‘a al-sadiqeen.” “Ittaqoo Allaha,” be mindful, is the best clothing, the fertile thankfulness, the mountain-hold firmly so perhaps you will become mindful, the sacred months not wronging, the furqan for the mindful, the obedience while you hear, the preparation what you can. The second, “be with the truthful,” or “koonoo ma‘a al-sadiqeen,” defines the company that mindfulness keeps, the same sadiqeen whose charity will distinguish truthful from liars after permission before distinguishing, the same truthfulness that the truthful Emigrants and Helpers embodied when they said we will surely spend and be of the righteous and fulfilled, unlike the withholding vow-makers whose hypocrisy plagued until meeting, and whose wealth and children impress but are torment.\n\nThe truthful, or “sadiqeen,” are those who never took trusted allies other than Allah, Messenger, believers, whose oaths are not “We were only joking,” whose building is on taqwa and ridwan, not on crumbling cliff, whose alms are for eight and whose hearts are attracted not for loss-awaiting misfortune but for closeness and Messenger’s prayers, the same three’s truthfulness in confessing “We lagged without excuse” versus the hypocrites’ “We were only talking idly.” Being with the truthful is the opposite of rushing to refuge, cave, hiding-place headlong, and of asking to be exempt saying “Leave us with those who remain.”\n\nThis verse links to the next: it was not proper for Medina and nomads around them to avoid marching with the Messenger or prefer their lives above his, for whenever they suffer thirst, fatigue, hunger, tread unnerving disbelievers, or inflict loss, it is written as good deed, Allah never discounts reward of good-doers, showing what being with the truthful practically does—march, suffer, and gain, not remain behind and then seek the little laugh.\n\nA common error thinks mindfulness is solitary. The Quran joins it to company: be with the truthful, as earlier the hypocrites, men and women, are all alike encouraging evil, forbidding good, withholding, so He neglected them, the rebellious, while believers men and women are guardians of one another, encouraging good, forbidding evil, establishing, paying, obeying, shown mercy, Almighty, All-Wise, and prepared Gardens, splendid homes, ultimate triumph with Allah’s pleasure above all.",
  "120": "“It was not ˹proper˺ for the people of Medina and the nomadic Arabs around them to avoid marching with the Messenger of Allah or to prefer their own lives above his. That is because whenever they suffer from thirst, fatigue, or hunger in the cause of Allah; or tread on a territory, unnerving the disbelievers; or inflict any loss on an enemy—it is written to their credit as a good deed. Surely Allah never discounts the reward of the good-doers” explains the being with the truthful in practical terms after commanding mindfulness. The phrase “It was not proper for the people of Medina and the nomadic Arabs around them to avoid marching with the Messenger of Allah or to prefer their own lives above his” or “ma kana li-ahli al-madeenati wa man hawlahum mina al-a‘rabi an yatakhallafoo an Rasool Allahi wa la yarghaboo bi-anfusihim an nafsihi” uses “ma kana,” it was not, not proper, like “It is not for polytheists to maintain mosques while professing disbelief” and “It is not proper for the Prophet and believers to seek forgiveness for polytheists after clarity that they are companions of Hellfire.” Avoiding marching, or “yatakhallaf o,” to stay behind, is the same remaining behind that the rich preferred with the helpless and the hypocrites rejoiced in defiance, hating to strive, saying “Do not march in the heat.” Preferring lives above his is the beloveds more dear test where parents, wealth, trade, homes outweigh Allah, Messenger, striving, then wait for His Will, rebellious not guided.\n\nThe reason “That is because whenever they suffer from thirst, fatigue, or hunger in the cause of Allah; or tread on a territory, unnerving the disbelievers; or inflict any loss on an enemy—it is written to their credit as a good deed” or “thalika bi-annahum la yuseebuhum zama’un wa la nasabun wa la makhmasatun fi sabeeli Allahi wa la yata’oona mawti’an yagheezo al-kuffara wa la yanaloona min aduwwin nailan illa kutiba lahum bihi amalun salih” lists sufferings: zama’, thirst, nasab, fatigue, makhmasa, hunger, the hard hour of Tabuk’s heat where earth seemed to close on the three, the same hard hour the Emigrants and Helpers followed in after hearts almost faltered and were turned to with mercy ever gracious. Treading to unnerve and inflicting loss are the deterrent preparation what you can and the making firm, the phrasing mirrors the Badr rain purifying and firming steps and the deterrent horses.\n\nThe seal “Surely Allah never discounts the reward of the good-doers” or “inna Allaha la yudee‘u ajra al-muhsineen” repeats “We never discount reward of those acting righteously” for those who firmly abide and establish prayer, where alms for eight is obligation, and for those who hoard not but donate, the opposite of the treasure heated.\n\nA common misunderstanding measures deeds by spoils’ fifth or by near gain. The verse measures every thirst, fatigue, hunger, tread, and nail as amal salih, written, not discounted, inviting the mindful to march whether easy or difficult, best if only they knew, with wealth and lives, so the great reward with Allah forever is written, as the everlasting Gardens with honourable provision.",
  "121": "“And whenever they make a donation, small or large, or cross a valley ˹in Allah’s cause˺—it is written to their credit, so that Allah may grant them the best reward for what they used to do” continues the writing of good deeds beyond thirst, fatigue, hunger, treading, and loss. The previous verse said those sufferings are written as good deed and Allah never discounts reward of good-doers. This verse says “And whenever they make a donation, small or large, or cross a valley in Allah’s cause—it is written to their credit, so that Allah may grant them the best reward for what they used to do” or “wa la yunfiqoona nafaqatan sagheeratan wa la kabeeratan wa la yaqta‘oona wadiyan illa kutiba lahum li-yajziyahumu Allahu ahsana ma kanu ya‘maloon.” “Nafaqatan sagheeratan wa la kabeeratan,” small or large donation, covers the liberal donors slandered and the little-givers mocked, the poor who find only juhd, the weeping seekers of mounts with nothing to contribute and eyes overflowing, now shown that even small is written. The valley, or “wadi,” crossing, is the Tabuk march through the vast earth that closed on the three and on Hunain’s pride, the same valleys where angels reinforced and rain firmed, now each crossing written.\n\nThe purpose “so that Allah may grant them the best reward for what they used to do” or “li-yajziyahumu Allahu ahsana ma kanu ya‘maloon” is the same “ahsana ma kanu ya‘maloon,” best of what they did, that the Gardens’ splendid homes and pleasure of Allah represent, the same “great reward with Allah” for the donor whose donation is qurbat and Messenger’s prayers, the same honourable provision after elevated ranks, whose calculation is tenfold and whose reward is never discounted for those acting righteously, and whose promise is binding in Torah, Gospel, Quran, whose purchase is lives and wealth for Paradise, ultimate triumph. Best reward means the small donation is not repaid small but as best, the valley crossing not counted as mere steps but as best, the same “best of it” to take from the Tablets and the “best” to march whether easy or difficult.\n\nThis links the earlier “We will never discount reward of those acting righteously” for firm abiders and the “We will not be wronged” for whatever you spend in Allah’s cause will be paid in full, showing the Record since creation that wrote twelve months writes also each nafaqah and valley, the same Record that knows sirr and najwa and unseen.\n\nA common error counts only large donations and short valleys. The Quran counts small and large, every wadi, as written for best reward, inviting the mindful to be with the truthful, to firmly abide, to give from what He provided, and to know the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous will grant the best, far better than hoarded gold heated to brand foreheads, sides, backs where taste is “Taste what you hoarded.”",
  "122": "“˹However,˺ it is not necessary for the believers to march forth all at once. Only a party from each group should march forth, leaving the rest to gain religious knowledge then enlighten their people when they return to them, so that they ˹too˺ may beware ˹of evil˺” balances the march that earned written good deeds for every thirst, hunger, donation, and valley. After saying it was not proper for Medina and nomads to avoid marching or prefer lives above his and that every suffering, tread, loss, donation, valley is written for best reward, this verse says “However, it is not necessary for the believers to march forth all at once. Only a party from each group should march forth, leaving the rest to gain religious knowledge then enlighten their people when they return to them, so that they too may beware” or “wa ma kana al-mu’minoon li-yanfiroo kaffah fa-lawla nafara min kulli firqatin minhum ta’ifatun li-yatafaqqahoon fi al-deen wa li-yunthiroo qawmahum itha raja‘oo ilaihim la‘allahum yahtharoon.” The phrase “li-yatafaqqahoo fi al-deen,” to gain deep understanding in religion, is the same “yafqahoon” for which lessons are varied for the thankful, the same comprehension the Sabbath violators lacked and the heedless cattle lack, the same “perhaps they will beware” that motivates the warner’s excuse and perhaps they may abstain. The division “Only a party from each group should march forth, leaving the rest to gain knowledge” shows the tireless devils who persistently plunge associates deeper without sparing effort are countered by tireless teaching, and the border enemies known and unknown but known to Allah are deterred by power and horses, but also by knowledge that prevents fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers and great corruption if believers do not guard each other.\n\nThe purpose “so that they too may beware of evil” or “la‘allahum yahtharoon” is the same “yathakkaroon” and “yarji‘oon” hoped for when the mountain was raised, the primordial testimony taken, and the stories narrated. Beware, or “yathar,” caution, mirrors the mindful who will be shown mercy.\n\nThis verse links the earlier “We will never discount reward of good-doers” for firm abiders, and the “We have brought them a Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy for those who believe” and the “When the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy” and “Remember your Lord inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness” and the angels nearest not too proud to worship, showing marching and learning are two forms of striving that are written as good.\n\nA common misunderstanding pits marching against learning. The Quran makes them complementary: a party marches, a party deepens understanding, then enlightens their people when they return, so beware, completing the circle of the unlettered Prophet sent to all, the guidance that prevails, and the light perfected even to disbelievers’ dismay.",
  "123": "“O believers! Fight the disbelievers around you and let them find firmness in you. And know that Allah is with those mindful ˹of Him˺” returns to fighting after the balanced marching-learning division, now focusing on the near enemy. The previous verse said not all need march at once, but a party marches while others deepen understanding then warn. This verse says “O believers! Fight the disbelievers around you and let them find firmness in you” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo qaatiloo allatheena yaloonakum mina al-kuffari wa li-yajidoo feekum ghilzah.” The near, or “yaloonakum,” those around you, reflects the Medina situation where after Tabuk the next perimeter is the surrounding Arab and Byzantine frontier, the same frontier where the hypocrites hoped to hide in refuges, caves, and entrances rushing headlong. “Ghilzah,” firmness, harshness, sternness, is like the firmness of rain-firmed steps and the “Be firm” when facing the enemy, and the harshness against disbelievers and hypocrites with Hell as evil destination, not harshness to the mindful. Firmness deters the treaty violators who violate every time, the Sabbath fish tricksters, and the hoarders whose treasure will brand, and it prevents the great oppression if believers do not guard each other.\n\nThe seal “And know that Allah is with those mindful of Him” or “wa i‘lamoo anna Allaha ma‘a al-muttaqeen” uses the withness, or “ma‘iyyah,” that throughout this chapter has been “Allah is with those who persevere,” “Allah is with believers,” “Allah is with those who are mindful during sacred months together fighting as they fight together,” the same withness that united hearts when all riches could not, sent sere nity upon the Prophet in the cave, and sent unseen forces at Badr and Hunain. Mindful, or “muttaqeen,” are those who wear the best clothing, who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, who do not wrong one another during sacred months, who prepare power to deter, who incline to peace and trust the All-Hearing, All-Knowing, whose alms are only for eight as obligation.\n\nThis links to the next verses where a surah revealed asks mockingly “Which of you has this increased in faith?” for believers it increases faith and they rejoice, for the sick it increases wickedness and they die as disbelievers, tried once or twice every year yet neither repent nor learn, showing firmness in you is visible as increased faith.\n\nA common error confuses firmness with harshness to the weak. The Quran limits firmness to the near disbelievers around you, the combatants, while the previous division leaves the rest to learn and warn, and the earlier protection to hear the Word and escort to safety remains for seekers of protection who have no knowledge. Knowing Allah is with the mindful makes firmness not arrogance like those who left homes to be seen, but the steadfastness that the three who remained behind learned when the earth closed and no refuge from Allah except in Him was known.",
  "124": "“Whenever a sûrah is revealed, some of them ask ˹mockingly˺, ‘Which of you has this increased in faith?’ As for the believers, it has increased them in faith and they rejoice” describes the litmus of revelation after commanding to fight the near disbelievers with firmness and knowing Allah is with the mindful. The phrase “Whenever a sûrah is revealed, some of them ask mockingly, ‘Which of you has this increased in faith?’” or “wa itha ma unzilat sooratun fa-minhum man yaqoolu ayyukum zadathu hathihi imana” shows hypocrites and sick-hearted using a new surah as occasion for mockery, as earlier they said “We were only talking idly” and “Was it Allah, His revelations, Messenger you ridiculed?” and as hoarders and sacred-month reallocators increased in disbelief. The question “Which of you has this increased in faith?” mimics the angels’ “am hasibtum” testing and the believers’ “We have believed, our Lord gives good news,” reducing revelation to a contest.\n\nThe answer divides the audience: “As for the believers, it has increased them in faith and they rejoice” or “fa-amma allatheena amanoo fa-zadathum imanan wa hum yastabshiroon.” The increase, or “zadathum,” is the same increase praised for true believers whose faith increases when revelations are recited, whose hearts tremble, who trust, establish, and donate, the same increase that varies lessons for the thankful and makes fertile land produce abundantly. Rejoicing, or “yastabshiroon,” is the same rejoicing that the Messenger and believers who strived will have all the best and ultimate triumph in Gardens, splendid homes, and greatest pleasure of Allah, the same rejoicing that the foremost, the first Emigrants and Helpers and followers in goodness, have when Allah is pleased, and the same rejoicing that is the opposite of “grieve at blessing and rejoice at disaster” of those who said “We took precaution.”\n\nThis also links to the next verse where for those with sickness it increased only wickedness and they die as disbelievers, tried once or twice yearly yet neither repent nor learn, and who whenever a surah is revealed look at each other “Is anyone watching?” then slip away, Allah has turned hearts because they do not comprehend.\n\nA common misunderstanding measures revelation’s effect by immediate worldly gain within reach and short journey. The Quran measures by faith increase. For the community that was told “Do not be like those who say we hear but do not listen” and “Indeed worst beings are deaf and dumb who do not understand,” the increased faith is the proof of hearing, the heaviness that makes the scale heavy, the firmness the near disbelievers will find, the mindfulness with which Allah is. The mindful listener, whose prayer is not lazy and whose donation is not resentful, hears the surah revealed stating “Believe and struggle with the Messenger” not as a burden to ask “Leave us with those who remain” but as the surah that increases faith and to be followed in goodness.",
  "125": "“But as for those with sickness in their hearts, it has increased them only in wickedness upon their wickedness, and they die as disbelievers” gives the counterpart increase for the sick-hearted, directly after saying believers rejoice at increased faith. The previous verse said whenever a surah is revealed some mockingly ask “Which of you has this increased in faith?” for believers it increased faith and they rejoice. This verse says “But as for those with sickness in their hearts, it has increased them only in wickedness upon their wickedness, and they die as disbelievers” or “wa amma allatheena fi quloobihim maradun fa-zadathum rijsan ila rijsihim wa maatoo wa hum kafiroon.” The phrase “maradun,” sickness, is the same sickness in hypocrites who said believers are deluded and whose hearts are in doubt torn by doubts and whose alms criticism shows conditional pleasure. The increase “rij san ila rijsihim,” wickedness upon wickedness, impurity upon impurity, mirrors the hoarders whose treasure will be heated to brand, the reallocators whose evil deeds are made appealing, and the building on crumbling cliff that fuels hypocrisy until hearts are torn. “Rijs” wickedness is the spiritual impurity for which polytheists should not approach the Sacred Mosque after this year, the same impurity that will be told to taste what you hoarded, and the same impurity that Hell will be home as reward for what they committed, the ultimate disgrace.\n\nThe end “and they die as disbelievers” or “wa maatoo wa hum kafiroon” seals the sickness’s trajectory, the same souls departing while disbelievers after wealth and children torment in this world, the same souls beaten at taking to taste burning for what hands sent forth, never unjust, the same “They will be in Fire forever” for polytheists maintaining mosques while professing disbelief, the same “If you pray seventy times never forgiven because they disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger and Allah does not guide the rebellious,” and “Do not ever pray for their dead nor stand by grave for they disbelieved and died rebellious.”\n\nThis links the surah’s effect to the heart’s state: the same surah revealed stating “Believe and struggle” increases faith for the mindful, firm abiders whose reward is never discounted, the foremost pleased, but increases wickedness for the sick, as earlier fertile land produces abundantly by the Lord’s leave while infertile hardly produces, and as the weighing distinguishes heavy success from light self-doom.\n\nA common error blames the surah for the increase in wickedness. The Quran shows the sickness predates, the surah merely reveals it, as earlier “They are tried once or twice every year, yet they neither repent nor learn a lesson” and “Whenever a surah is revealed they look at each other ‘Is anyone watching?’ then slip away, Allah has turned hearts because they do not comprehend,” proving the sick choose wading and play, and the Book explained with knowledge becomes guide and mercy for those who believe but increases impurity for those who choose disease.",
  "126": "“Do they not see that they are tried once or twice every year? Yet they neither repent nor do they learn a lesson” exposes the heedlessness of those whose sickness increased with each sûrah. The previous verse said those with sickness in hearts, when a sûrah is revealed, it increases them only in wickedness and they die as disbelievers. This verse says they are tried, or “yuftanoona,” tested, once or twice every year, the same trial that earlier was “We tested them with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return” and the Sabbath fish every week and the four-month travel and the valley appointment. The test once or twice yearly could be famine, illness, exposure of hypocrisy through a new surah, or the trial of wealth and children as worldly torment, the same testing that makes the hypocrites fear a surah will expose what is in hearts. The phrase “Yet they neither repent nor do they learn a lesson” or “thumma la yatooboona wa la hum yaththakkaroon” uses “yatooboon,” to turn in repentance, and “yaththakkaroon,” to remember, to be mindful, the same twin that defines the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful for those who shun evil, pay zakat, believe, and the condition that We would never consider a people deviant after guiding until We make clear. Their failure to repent despite yearly trials shows the sealing that makes even if they see every sign they will not believe, preferring the crooked, and the hoarding that will brand, and the sacred-month reallocating as increase in disbelief.\n\nThis verse links to the next where whenever a surah is revealed they look at each other “Is anyone watching you?” then slip away, Allah has turned hearts because they do not comprehend, showing yearly trials are also surah revelations that test. The trial is not only famine or shortage but the Book itself, the insight, guide, and mercy that, when listened to attentively and silently, brings mercy, but when heard as those who say we hear but do not listen, increases wickedness.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks trials must be dramatic plagues. The verse shows being tried once or twice yearly includes the regular opening of a new surah, a regular observation of the believers increasing in faith and rejoicing, the same rejoicing that the Messenger and believers who strived will have in Gardens, splendid homes, and greatest pleasure of Allah, the same rejoicing that is the opposite of “grieve at blessing and rejoice at disaster” of those who said “We took precaution.” The trial is the Book itself, whose remembrance morning and evening keeps from heedlessness, whose mosque founded on righteousness is more worthy.",
  "127": "“Whenever a sûrah is revealed, they look at one another, ˹saying,˺ ‘Is anyone watching you?’ Then they slip away. ˹It is˺ Allah ˹Who˺ has turned their hearts away because they are a people who do not comprehend” depicts the physical reaction that follows the yearly test where they neither repent nor learn. The previous verse said they are tried once or twice every year with a surah’s increase for believers and in wickedness for the sick, yet they neither repent nor learn. This verse shows the moment in the gathering: “Whenever a sûrah is revealed, they look at one another, saying, ‘Is anyone watching you?’ Then they slip away” or “wa itha ma unzilat sooratun nazara ba‘duhum ila ba‘d hal yarakum min ahadin thumma insarafoo.” The surah, like the “Sûrah is revealed stating Believe and struggle with the Messenger” that the rich used to ask to be left with those who remain, is now the test of faith. The glance “Is anyone watching you?” seeks cover for slipping, the same rushing to refuge, cave, hiding-place headlong, the same deceit as elegant words that incline hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter. “Insarafoo,” to slip away, to turn, to disperse, is the opposite of standing firm and remembering often to triumph, of obeying and not disputing so wind does not go, and of being faithful to the mosque where men love to be purified.\n\nThe verdict “It is Allah Who has turned their hearts away because they are a people who do not comprehend” or “sara fa Allahu quloobahum bi-annahum qawmun la yafqahoon” uses “sara fa,” to turn, to divert, as earlier “We will turn away from My signs those who act arrogantly” even if they see every sign will not believe, and “He caused hypocrisy to plague hearts until the meeting for breaking promise and lies.” Turning here follows not comprehending, or “la yafqahoon,” not understanding, the same lack for which lessons are varied for the thankful, the same lack that makes the worst deaf and dumb not understand, the same lack that made the three who remained behind need the earth to close before they understood refuge is only in Him.\n\nA common error views slipping away as harmless exit. The Quran says slipping away after a surah is the heart being turned, the same turning that makes wealth and children worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers. The alternative is to be among those who, when a surah is revealed, faith increases and they rejoice, whose hearts tremble, whose donation is qurbat bringing closer and prayers of Messenger, who will be admitted into mercy, Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  "128": "“There certainly has come to you a messenger from among yourselves. He is concerned by your suffering, anxious for your well-being, and gracious and merciful to the believers” presents the messenger whose surah’s revelation the previous verse said some slip away from, now as the compassionate alternative. The phrase “There certainly has come to you a messenger from among yourselves” or “la-qad ja’akum rasoolun min anfusikum” repeats the same “from among yourselves” that made reminders astonishing to Noah’s and Hud’s peoples, that made “Is it astonishing that a reminder comes through one of your own” the test, and that makes the unlettered Prophet whose description is in Torah and Gospel a mercy because he shares language and hardship. “From among yourselves” also echoes the primordial testimony from the loins of children of Adam, “Am I not your Lord,” where all testified, showing the messenger is the reminder of that testimony.\n\nThe traits “He is concerned by your suffering, anxious for your well-being, and gracious and merciful to the believers” or “azeezun alaihi ma anittum hareesun alaikum bil-mu’mineena ra’oofun raheem” describe a heart like the fertile land that suffers when the community suffers. “Azeezun alaihi ma anittum,” hard upon him is what distresses you, is the same distress that made the earth close at Hunain and for the three, and that made the Prophet’s ear good for you, believing in Allah and having faith in believers. “Hareesun alaikum,” eager, anxious for you, is the same eagerness that motivated to fight with twenty steadfast overcoming two hundred and that pardoned the rich before distinguishing truthful, and that prayed seventy times though never forgiven for the rebellious, but here eagerness is for well-being, or “huda,” guidance. “Ra’oofun raheem,” gracious, merciful, repeats the names that closed the turning to the Prophet, Emigrants, Helpers in hard hour, Ever Gracious, Most Merciful, and that made charity a purification and prayer a comfort.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next and final verse for those who turn away, “But if they turn away, say ‘Allah is sufficient for me, there is no god except Him, in Him I put trust, He is Lord of the Mighty Throne,’” showing the messenger’s concern is offered, but sufficiency remains with Allah if they turn, the same sufficiency promised as hearts united when all riches could not and as unseen forces at Badr and Hunain. A common misunderstanding reads the messenger’s concern as softness that overlooks hypocrisy. The tender concern coexists with firmness against disbelievers and hypocrites whose home is Hell, evil destination, and with the “Do not pray in the harm mosque, mosque founded on righteousness is more worthy, men love to be purified, Allah loves those who purify.” The messenger’s ra’fah and rahmah are therefore the mercy encompassing everything that is ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, believe, follow the light, whose building is on taqwa and ridwan.",
  "129": "“But if they turn away, then say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Allah is sufficient for me. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. In Him I put my trust. And He is the Lord of the Mighty Throne’” closes the chapter and the surah of bara’ah with the same sufficiency that threaded the cave, Badr, and Hunain. The condition “But if they turn away” or “fa-in tawallaw” refers to the hypocrites who slip away asking “Is anyone watching” and to those with sickness whose surah increases wickedness, to those who are tried yearly yet neither repent nor learn, to the nomads who see donation as loss and await misfortune, to the worst beings who persist, violate every time, who would be trouble if they had gone, who sought discord before and plotted every plot. Turning away, or “tawallaw,” is the same turning forbidden while you hear his call and in battle except for manoeuvre, the same turning that makes wealth and children torment and souls depart while disbelievers.\n\nThe reply “then say, O Prophet, Allah is sufficient for me. There is no god worthy of worship except Him. In Him I put my trust. And He is the Lord of the Mighty Throne” or “fa-qul hasbiya Allah la ilaha illa huwa alaihi tawakkaltu wa huwa Rabbu al-‘arshi al-azeem” gathers the creed of the gardener who said “He is Allah, my Lord, associate none with your Lord,” the mountain’s “Glory be to You, I turn to You, I am first of believers,” and the Prophet’s earlier “I have no power to benefit or protect myself except by Allah’s will, I only follow what is revealed.” Sufficiency, or “hasbiya Allah,” is the same sufficient for you and followers, sufficient for peace deceivers, sufficient for the ear of good, whose help and believers united hearts, whose thousand angels murdifeen reassured, whose rain purified. Trust, or “tawakkaltu,” is the same trust of whoever puts trust in Allah, Almighty, All-Wise, not deluded, whose earth belongs to Him to grant to whom He chooses, whose outcome is for the righteous, whose great reward is forever. The Throne, or “Arsh al-Azeem,” is the Mighty Throne after creation of heavens and earth in six Days and establishment, the Throne whose kingdom discipline is twelve months, four sacred, the Record, the Deen al-Qayyim, the best planner whose plan is flawless.\n\nA common error thinks turning away of many leaves the Prophet unsupported. The verse says sufficiency is the Mighty Throne’s Lord Himself, the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, excellent protector and helper, whose beautiful names are to be called upon, whose guidance via a group that guides with truth remains, whose promise of Gardens under rivers forever, splendid homes, and greatest pleasure remains. For the community that hoarded gold to be heated, reallocated months as increase in disbelief, clung to land preferring the little enjoyment, the closing trust is the fertile alternative to clinging, to be with the truthful, mindful of Allah."
};
